        <header class="udaw-hero">
            <span class="udaw-mark">AI/SI-Assisted Digital Audio Workstation</span>
            <h1>UDAW Help Centre</h1>
            <p>Recording &middot; Timeline &middot; Mixing &middot; DSP &middot; Tracker &middot; MIDI &middot; AI/SI Composition</p>
            <p class="udaw-build-info">
                Version <!--UDAW_VERSION-->1.0.659<!--/UDAW_VERSION--> &middot;
                Built <!--UDAW_BUILDDATE-->01/11/2026<!--/UDAW_BUILDDATE-->
            </p>
        </header>

        <!-- ============================================================ -->
        <section id="overview">
            <h2>1. Overview</h2>
            <div class="udaw-purpose">
                <p><b>Purpose</b> Explain what UDAW is and which of its four workflows to reach for.</p>
                <p><b>Why</b> UDAW is unusual in bundling a linear DAW, a tracker and a MIDI sequencer in one project - knowing which tool fits your idea saves time.</p>
            </div>
            <p>
                <strong>UDAW</strong> (Ultimanium Digital Audio Workstation) is a self-contained desktop
                DAW for Windows, with no third-party audio libraries. Every codec,
                DSP algorithm, mixer, tracker engine, MIDI engine and file-format reader/writer is
                implemented from scratch inside the application, so a UDAW project never depends on
                external runtimes, VST hosts or system codecs to open, play or render correctly. That
                also means a finished song is completely portable: hand someone your <code>.udawproj</code>
                file and every sound in it - synth, sample, effect, tracker instrument - travels with it.
            </p>
            <p>UDAW combines four workflows in one project, and you can freely mix them in the same song:</p>
            <ul>
                <li><strong>Linear multitrack recording and editing</strong> (the <a href="#tab-timeline">Timeline</a>) &ndash; use this for recorded vocals/instruments, arranging audio clips, and comping takes, the way a conventional DAW works.</li>
                <li><strong>A native tracker</strong> (the <a href="#tab-tracker">Tracker</a>) &ndash; use this for chiptune/tracker-style composition: patterns of notes typed directly into a grid, classic MOD/XM/IT effect commands, and sample-based instruments.</li>
                <li><strong>MIDI sequencing</strong> (the <a href="#tab-midi">Piano Roll</a>) &ndash; use this to compose with an internal polyphonic synth by drawing notes, or to import a Standard MIDI File and hear it immediately with no external instrument needed.</li>
                <li><strong>An AI/SI assistant</strong> (the <a href="#tab-ai">AI/SI tab</a>) &ndash; use this to ask questions about your project, have the assistant make mix/mastering moves for you (with Preview/Compare/Apply/Reject safety rails), generate new musical material via Suno, control UDAW by voice, and - via <a href="#ai-mcp">MCP</a> - connect external tool servers into the same conversation.</li>
            </ul>
            <p>
                A practical example of mixing workflows: build a drum-and-bass pattern in the Tracker,
                record a live vocal over it on the Timeline, add a MIDI bass line played through the
                internal synth, and mix all three together on the same Mixer - one project, one
                <code>.udawproj</code> file, one Undo stack.
            </p>
            <div class="udaw-note">
                UDAW brings together a complete audio engine, DSP, project format, WAV codec, timeline,
                recording, mixer, effects, tracker, MOD/XM/MPTM import-export, MP3, MP4, MIDI, AI/SI
                providers and MCP. See
                <a href="#about">About &amp; Version</a> for the current build's feature set.
            </div>
        </section>

        <!-- ============================================================ -->
        <section id="start">
            <h2>2. Getting Started</h2>
            <div class="udaw-purpose">
                <p><b>Purpose</b> Get from a fresh install to your first recorded/imported sound.</p>
                <p><b>Why</b> UDAW has no separate "new song wizard" - the app itself, with sensible defaults, is the fastest path in.</p>
            </div>
            <h3>First run</h3>
            <p>
                On first launch, a startup screen identifies UDAW, its build version and author while a
                determinate progress bar reports the modules being loaded. UDAW then opens on the Timeline - with an empty project, or the one you last used - and, after a short pause, shows the <a href="#welcome">Welcome screen</a>. Use the <strong>Settings</strong> page
                to confirm your audio device and sample rate, then use <strong>Project &rarr; New</strong>
                (or just start recording/importing) to begin working. A project is a single file that
                embeds the graph, mixer, timeline, tracker module and MIDI sequence together, so there
                is nothing else to configure before you can record or import audio.
            </p>
            <h3>Choosing an audio device</h3>
            <p>
                The Output and Input device lists on <strong>Settings &gt; General</strong> show the available output/input devices. On
                Windows, UDAW talks to the hardware directly for low-latency playback and
                recording; if no suitable device is found it falls back to a portable software audio
                backend so the application still runs. Pick the smallest buffer size your machine can
                run glitch-free while recording (lower buffer = lower latency = tighter feel when
                playing/singing along to existing tracks) and a sample rate that matches your source
                material (44.1&nbsp;kHz for most music work, 48&nbsp;kHz if you also do video/game audio).
            </p>
            <p>
                To record from a specific microphone/interface rather than whatever Windows currently
                treats as the default recording device, pick it explicitly from the <strong>Input
                device</strong> list on the <a href="#tab-settings">Settings</a> tab's General page (next
                to Input channels). The choice is saved with your other audio settings and re-selected
                automatically the next time UDAW starts. Every setting on that page is saved and applied
                the moment you change it, so there is nothing to confirm; if something is playing at that
                instant, the change takes effect the next time the audio stream opens.
            </p>
            <h3 id="welcome">The Welcome screen</h3>
            <div class="udaw-purpose">
                <p><b>Purpose</b> Start work in one click: a new or recent project, the help, or straight into the editor you want.</p>
                <p><b>Why</b> Most sessions begin the same few ways - carry on with yesterday's song, start a new one, or jump into one tool (the tracker, the drum pads) to try an idea. The Welcome screen puts all of those in one place so you never have to hunt through menus first.</p>
            </div>
            <p>
                A short time after UDAW's main window appears (long enough for the Timeline to finish drawing
                behind it), the Welcome screen opens over it. It is a round-cornered window with a large
                title; drag the title area to move it. Nothing happens until you choose, and nothing is
                changed by closing it.
            </p>
            <table class="udaw-table udaw-col-22">
                <tr><td>START &ndash; New Project</td><td>Begins a new, empty project. <em>Why:</em> a clean slate for a new song. If the open project has unsaved changes you are asked first.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>START &ndash; Open Project...</td><td>Shows the Open dialog for a saved project file. <em>Why:</em> carry on with work that is not in the recent list.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>RECENT PROJECTS</td><td>The last five projects you opened or saved, newest first, shown by file name only (hover for the full path). <em>Why:</em> the quickest way back into what you were doing. A project that was open when UDAW last closed unexpectedly is recovered from its newest autosave automatically.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>LEARN &ndash; Help</td><td>Opens this Help Centre.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>LEARN &ndash; About UDAW</td><td>Version, publisher and credits.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>FUNCTIONS &ndash; Tracker</td><td>Opens the <a href="#tracker">Tracker</a> full-page. <em>Why:</em> type notes into a pattern grid for chiptune/tracker music, or to load a MOD / XM / IT module.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>FUNCTIONS &ndash; Foley</td><td>Opens the <a href="#timeline-foley">Foley Sampler</a>. <em>Why:</em> play sound effects live against a picture or a song and have them placed on the Timeline for you.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>FUNCTIONS &ndash; Drum Pad (Pattern)</td><td>Opens the <a href="#timeline-patterns">Pattern editor</a>, the step sequencer. <em>Why:</em> build a drum or rhythm loop by switching steps on, without recording anything.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>FUNCTIONS &ndash; Piano Roll</td><td>Opens the <a href="#midi">Piano Roll</a>. <em>Why:</em> compose melodies, chords and bass lines by drawing notes, played by UDAW's built-in synth.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>FUNCTIONS &ndash; Instrument</td><td>Opens the drum machine (the <b>Inst</b> mode): sample pads on every MIDI note. <em>Why:</em> load your own drum and percussion sounds onto pads and play or edit them.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>FUNCTIONS &ndash; Open AI/SI</td><td>Opens the <a href="#tab-ai">AI/SI tab</a>. <em>Why:</em> go straight to the chat, composition, mixing and mastering help and music generation.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>SETTINGS &ndash; Open Settings</td><td>Opens the <a href="#settings">Settings</a> tab. <em>Why:</em> choose your audio output and input devices, sample rate and buffer, folders and theme before you start, without closing the Welcome screen first and hunting for the tab.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Show on Startup</td><td>Untick to stop the Welcome screen appearing when UDAW starts. It is the same setting as <em>Show the Project Startup screen on startup</em> on the Settings page, so changing either changes both.</td></tr>
            </table>
            <p>
                Press <span class="kbd">Esc</span> or click the round close icon at the top right to dismiss
                the screen and go straight to the Timeline. The FUNCTIONS buttons are shortcuts to the same
                editors the mode bar under the Timeline transport gives you (see
                <a href="#timeline-modes">Modes</a>); you can switch between them at any time.
            </p>

            <h3 id="start-output">Choosing where sound comes out</h3>
            <p>
                Everything UDAW plays - the Timeline, the WAV editor, the Tracker, the Piano Roll and drum
                machine, the Foley pads, previews, and even the warning sounds - leaves through one
                <strong>Output device</strong> that you choose on the <a href="#tab-settings">Settings</a>
                tab's General page (next to Output channels). Leave it on <em>Default (system)</em> to follow
                whatever Windows uses, or pick a specific interface or speakers. The <strong>Out</strong> entry in the status box at the right of the menu bar always shows the
                device UDAW is really using right now. Full details, including the audio backend and
                exclusive mode, are in <a href="#audio-output">Settings &amp; Themes</a>.
            </p>
            <h3>Opening vs. importing</h3>
            <p>
                <strong>Open</strong> loads a native <code>.udawproj</code> project file (or an older
                version &ndash; see <a href="#project-doc">Projects, Undo &amp; Recovery</a>).
                <strong>Import</strong> brings in a foreign file &ndash; WAV/MP3/FLAC/MP4 audio or an Ableton
                Live Set (<code>.als</code>) / FL Studio Project (<code>.flp</code>) onto the Timeline,
                a Standard MIDI File into the Piano Roll, or a MOD/XM/MPTM/IT module into the Tracker
                &ndash; converting it into UDAW's own internal representation so it can be edited,
                mixed and processed exactly like anything created natively in UDAW.
            </p>
            <p>
                <strong>Project &rarr; Open</strong>, <strong>Save</strong>, and <strong>Save As</strong>
                also have keyboard shortcuts: <span class="kbd">Ctrl+O</span>,
                <span class="kbd">Ctrl+S</span>, and <span class="kbd">Ctrl+Shift+S</span>. These are
                listed as built-in, non-reassignable rows in <strong>Settings &rarr; Key Hooks</strong>
                alongside the global Undo/Redo shortcuts.
            </p>
            <h4>A first project, start to finish</h4>
            <ol>
                <li>Open <strong>Settings</strong> and confirm your <a href="#audio-output">Output device</a> (and Input device if you will record).</li>
                <li>Go to the <a href="#tab-timeline">Timeline</a> tab and add an audio track, or the
                    <a href="#tab-tracker">Tracker</a> tab to start a pattern, or import an existing
                    WAV/MP3/MOD/MIDI file to build on top of.</li>
                <li>If recording: arm the track on the <a href="#tab-recording">Recording</a> tab, set a
                    pre-roll/count-in if you want one, and hit record.</li>
                <li>Open the <a href="#tab-mixer">Mixer</a> tab, set levels/pan, and add effects from the
                    <a href="#dsp">DSP suite</a> to taste.</li>
                <li>Save the project (<strong>Project &rarr; Save</strong>) - this is one file containing
                    everything above.</li>
            </ol>
        </section>

        <!-- ============================================================ -->
        <section id="workspace">
            <h2>3. The Workspace</h2>
            <div class="udaw-purpose">
                <p><b>Purpose</b> A one-line map of where everything lives, with a pointer to the full section for each part.</p>
                <p><b>Why</b> Each mode and page is a dedicated workspace for one job, so knowing what lives where gets you to the right tool faster.</p>
            </div>
            <p>
                UDAW keeps almost everything on two main pages. The <strong>Timeline</strong> page is where you
                make music: the lanes are always the starting point, and the <strong>mode bar</strong> under the
                transport (Timeline, Piano, Score, Inst, Pattern, Mix, Tracker, Rec, AI/SI and Foley) turns the same
                page into the piano roll, the mixer, the tracker and so on - or the <strong>Editor</strong>
                switch docks one of them under the lanes so you can work on it while still seeing the
                arrangement. The <strong>Settings</strong> page holds everything you configure once and then
                forget: your audio devices, folders, look, keyboard shortcuts and the diagnostic pages.
                Not every project uses every part &ndash; a purely tracker-based song may never touch Rec or
                Piano. Jump straight to a section with the links below or the sidebar.
            </p>

            <h3 id="tab-platform">Audio devices &amp; diagnostics</h3>
            <p>
                You choose your devices on <strong>Settings &gt; General</strong>: the <strong>Output device</strong>
                everything plays through, the <strong>Input device</strong> you record from, the audio backend,
                sample rate, buffer size and exclusive mode - all described in
                <a href="#audio-output">Settings &amp; Themes</a>. The low-level engine diagnostics (device
                list, stream statistics, drop-outs) are on <strong>Settings &gt; Diagnostics &gt; Audio</strong>.
                <em>Why start here:</em> on a new machine, set the output and input once before recording
                anything, so you know sound and microphone work.
            </p>

            <h3 id="tab-dsp">DSP</h3>
            <p>
                <strong>Settings &gt; Diagnostics &gt; DSP</strong> (and <strong>Advanced DSP</strong>) is a workbench
                for the internal DSP building blocks &ndash; interpolation, filters, FFT, resamplers, envelopes and
                level/loudness metering (including BS.1770 loudness). <em>Why:</em> audition a signal chain or check
                a track's loudness before you commit the same processing on the <a href="#tab-mixer">Mixer</a>. Choose
                <em>Project mix (current timeline)</em> as the signal to run the filters, FFT and loudness meters against one
                second of your actual song instead of a test tone. See <a href="#dsp">DSP &amp; Effects</a>.
            </p>

            <h3 id="tab-graph">Graph</h3>
            <p>
                <strong>Settings &gt; Diagnostics &gt; Graph</strong> is the node-based audio graph editor. Effects, sources
                and utility nodes are wired together here; the graph can be saved/loaded as its own versioned text format,
                and is also embedded directly inside mixer insert slots. <em>Why:</em> build a reusable custom effect chain
                (for example a parallel-compression rig) once and drop it wherever you need it. See
                <a href="#graph">Audio Graph</a>.
            </p>

            <h3 id="tab-project">Project</h3>
            <p>
                <strong>Settings &gt; Project</strong> shows project-level details, the undo/redo history and the
                autosave / crash-recovery status. <em>Why:</em> check what UDAW has saved for you before you rely on it.
                See <a href="#project-doc">Projects, Undo &amp; Recovery</a>.
            </p>

            <h3 id="tab-wav">Supported SFX (WAV)</h3>
            <p>
                <strong>Settings &gt; Diagnostics &gt; Supported SFX</strong> imports, exports and inspects PCM/float WAV and RF64
                (64-bit WAV) files; sounds can also be opened in the WAV editor from the Browser or the Timeline.
                See <a href="#codec-wav">WAV / RF64</a>.
            </p>

            <h3 id="tab-timeline">Timeline</h3>
            <p>
                The main multitrack view &ndash; tracks, clips, waveforms, grid/snap and the playback transport
                (with its icon buttons). This is where you arrange, trim, move and
                fade audio, and where the mode bar switches to every other editor. See
                <a href="#timeline">Timeline &amp; Non-Destructive Editing</a>. The
                <a href="#welcome">Welcome screen</a> that opens when UDAW starts gets you to the right one in a click.
            </p>

            <h3 id="tab-recording">Recording</h3>
            <p>
                The <strong>Rec</strong> mode (or the Record icon): punch-in/out, loop recording, pre-roll, count-in,
                latency compensation, input monitoring, take management/comping, and commit-to-timeline.
                <em>Why:</em> capture a live vocal/instrument performance and pick the best parts of several takes.
                See <a href="#recording">Recording</a>.
            </p>

            <h3 id="tab-mixer">Mixer</h3>
            <p>
                The <strong>Mix</strong> mode: channel strips, sends, buses, aux and VCA groups, insert effect chains
                (hosting Graph nodes), pan/width/fader controls and metering. <em>Why:</em> this is where the whole
                project gets balanced and glued together. See <a href="#mixer">Mixer &amp; Routing</a>.
            </p>

            <h3 id="tab-tracker">Tracker</h3>
            <p>
                The <strong>Tracker</strong> mode: the pattern-grid editor for the native tracker engine, with
                sequence/order-list navigation and MOD/XM/MPTM(IT) import/export. <em>Why:</em> compose by typing
                notes straight into a grid, the classic chiptune way. See <a href="#tracker">Tracker Module</a>.
            </p>

            <h3 id="tab-mp3">MP3</h3>
            <p><strong>Settings &gt; Diagnostics &gt; MP3</strong>: MP3 decode/encode inspection and ID3 tag handling. See <a href="#codec-mp3">MP3</a>.</p>

            <h3 id="tab-mp4">MP4</h3>
            <p><strong>Settings &gt; Diagnostics &gt; MP4</strong>: MP4 container inspection (sample table, codec detection/report), plus play/stop auditioning and Import to Timeline for AAC-, MP3- and PCM-audio MP4/M4A/MKV files. See <a href="#codec-mp4">MP4</a>.</p>

            <h3 id="tab-midi">MIDI</h3>
            <p>
                The <strong>Piano</strong> mode (with <strong>Score</strong>, <strong>Inst</strong> and <strong>Pattern</strong>
                beside it): note editing with scale/chord helpers, tempo map, the drum machine and step sequencer, played
                through the internal 64-voice polyphonic synth. <em>Why:</em> compose with an instrument without
                recording one. See <a href="#midi">MIDI &amp; Piano Roll</a>.
            </p>

            <h3 id="tab-foley">Foley Sampler</h3>
            <p>
                The <strong>Foley</strong> mode, dock page and transport icon: sixteen sound-effect pads that you play
                live and that place themselves on the Timeline. <em>Why:</em> add footsteps, impacts and other effects
                in time with a picture or a song far faster than dragging files. See
                <a href="#timeline-foley">Foley Sampler</a>.
            </p>

            <h3 id="tab-ai">AI/SI Assistant</h3>
            <p>
                The <strong>AI/SI</strong> mode: a chat panel wired to a selectable AI/SI provider, plus mixing/mastering
                assistants, voice capture/command support, Suno-based music generation, and MCP for connecting external
                tool servers. See <a href="#ai">AI/SI Assistant &amp; Voice Control</a>.
            </p>

            <h3 id="tab-secure">Secure Storage</h3>
<p>Shows the OS-protected secret store (Windows DPAPI) that holds your API keys. Store, read (masked unless <em>Show values</em> is ticked) and delete secrets, run a round-trip self-test, or <em>Seal</em> the open project &ndash; an encrypted fingerprint of its name, tempo, track/clip and instrument counts &ndash; and later <em>Verify</em> whether it has changed.</p>
<h3 id="tab-settings">Settings</h3>
            <p>Startup configuration, working directories, visual theme selection, product Registration &amp; Updates, and <em>Show hints when hovering over controls</em> &ndash; a system-wide option (on by default, not stored in the project) that turns the rollover hint bubbles on or off everywhere in UDAW. See <a href="#settings">Settings &amp; Themes</a>.</p>
            <p><b>Where UDAW keeps its files.</b> The <em>Working Directories</em> group lets you choose the Projects, Audio library, SFX, Export and Recordings folders, plus two for disk space: <strong>Temporary files</strong> (scratch space used while rendering, exporting, downloading updates and viewing help) and <strong>Cache files</strong> (decoded and downloaded media UDAW can rebuild). If your C: drive is small, point both at a larger drive. Leave them empty for the defaults. Hover any control for a hint. Temporary and Cache changes take effect the next time UDAW starts.</p>
            <h3 id="workspace-controls">Menus, controls and the keyboard</h3>
            <p>Every part of UDAW looks and behaves the same way, and follows the visual theme you pick on the <strong>Settings</strong> tab (a theme change fades the window out and back in once the new look is ready).</p>
            <ul>
                <li><strong>Menus.</strong> Click a menu on the menu bar to open it; with one open, moving the pointer over the next menu name switches to it. Items with an arrow open a sub-menu when you point at them. Click an item to run it, or click anywhere else (or press <span class="kbd">Esc</span>) to close the menu. From the keyboard: <span class="kbd">Alt</span>+the underlined letter opens a menu, <span class="kbd">Up</span> / <span class="kbd">Down</span> move through the items, <span class="kbd">Right</span> / <span class="kbd">Left</span> enter and leave a sub-menu (or move to the next menu name), <span class="kbd">Enter</span> runs the highlighted item, and a letter picks the item with that underlined letter. Shortcut keys such as <span class="kbd">Ctrl+S</span> work whether or not the menu is open. Menu names that do not apply to the current tab are hidden, as before.</li>
                <li><strong>Right-click menus</strong> (Timeline, Mixer, Settings pages, the lyrics box and so on) are the same kind of menu and open at the pointer.</li>
                <li><strong>Drop-down lists.</strong> Click to open, click an entry to choose it, or click outside / press <span class="kbd">Esc</span> to leave the choice as it was. The mouse wheel over a closed list steps through its entries. A few lists (such as the AI/SI model) also let you type your own text.</li>
                <li><strong>Text boxes.</strong> Click to place the cursor, drag or hold <span class="kbd">Shift</span> to select, and use <span class="kbd">Ctrl+A</span>, <span class="kbd">Ctrl+C</span>, <span class="kbd">Ctrl+X</span> and <span class="kbd">Ctrl+V</span>. Letters and spaces you type in a box never trigger the global shortcuts.</li>
                <li><strong>Log and readout text.</strong> UDAW no longer shows a log window or read-only readout panels. Messages, status readouts and diagnostics are written to text files in the <em>logs</em> folder instead: the main log (<span class="kbd">udaw-YYYYMMDD.log</span>) and one <span class="kbd">ui-&lt;name&gt;.log</span> file per readout. <strong>Log &gt; Open Log...</strong> (also on the right-click menus that had it) opens the newest log file in your default text viewer.</li>
                <li><strong>Open / Save dialogs.</strong> Pick a drive or common folder from <em>Look in</em>, double-click a folder to enter it, type a path in the top box and press <span class="kbd">Enter</span>, and choose the file type from <em>Files of type</em>. Click a file (or <span class="kbd">Ctrl</span>/<span class="kbd">Shift</span>+click several where more than one is allowed) to put its name in the box, then <strong>Open</strong> / <strong>Save</strong>; type a pattern such as <span class="kbd">*.wav</span> and press <span class="kbd">Enter</span> to narrow the list. <strong>New folder</strong> creates a folder named like the text in the name box. Saving over an existing file asks first. <span class="kbd">Esc</span> cancels.</li>
                <li><strong>Knobs and sliders.</strong> Drag to change a value (hold <span class="kbd">Shift</span> for fine steps), use the mouse wheel, and double-click a knob to return it to its default.</li>
                <li><strong>Tabs.</strong> Click a tab to switch pages; with a tab strip selected, <span class="kbd">Left</span> / <span class="kbd">Right</span> move between tabs.</li>
                <li><strong>Check boxes and buttons</strong> take keyboard focus (<span class="kbd">Tab</span>) and are activated with <span class="kbd">Space</span>; a button marked as the default also answers <span class="kbd">Enter</span>.</li>
                <li><strong>Panes.</strong> Drag the thin divider between two panes (for example above the Timeline's docked editor) to give one more room.</li>
            </ul>
        </section>

        <!-- ============================================================ -->
        <section id="project-doc">
            <h2>4. Projects, Undo &amp; Recovery</h2>
            <div class="udaw-purpose">
                <p><b>Purpose</b> How a UDAW song is stored, and how you get back to a previous state.</p>
                <p><b>Why</b> Knowing the project format is one file, and that almost every edit is undoable, lets you experiment freely on the way to a finished mix.</p>
            </div>
            <p>
                A UDAW project is one document that embeds, in a single versioned text-based container,
                the audio graph, the mixer, the timeline (tracks/clips), the tracker module and the MIDI
                sequence. The format has grown by version number as features were added (graph
                embedding, mixer embedding, timeline embedding, tracker embedding, MIDI embedding);
                older project files continue to load, with newer sections simply absent/empty.
            </p>
            <p>
                The Project tab has an <b>Author's name</b> field next to the project Name. It is saved
                with the project and stamped into MP3 (ID3 artist), MP4, M4A, MKV, FLAC and WAV exports, and is also written as the Copyright field. Every MP3/MP4/M4A export also carries a &quot;Created by Ultimanium Digital Audio Workstation UDAW&quot; field a Version field holding the UDAW build version, and a Website field pointing to the UDAW page on ultimanium.com.
            </p>
            <p>
                <b>Settings &gt; General &gt; Export</b> sets the video <b>quality (bitrate)</b> and <b>size (maximum width)</b> used when a Timeline with video is exported as MP4 or MKV, the <b>audio quality (bitrate)</b> used for MP3, MP4, M4A and MKV exports, and shows the <b>Author</b> that is embedded in every exported file. <i>Automatic</i> picks a bitrate from the picture size and <i>Original size</i> keeps the source picture. These choices are saved in the project file and restored whenever that project is opened.
            </p>
            <h3>Undo / Redo</h3>
            <p>Undo and Redo, with Cut, Copy, Paste and the other editing commands of the tab you are on, are in the <b>Edit</b> menu on the menu bar. It shows the group for the current tab (Timeline, Tracker, Mixer or MIDI); on other tabs it offers the project-wide Undo and Redo.</p>
            <p>
                Almost every edit &ndash; timeline clip edits, mixer fader moves, tracker pattern edits,
                MIDI note edits, graph changes, and even AI/SI-driven changes (see
                <a href="#ai-chat">Chat &amp; Tool Commands</a>) &ndash; goes through a command object on
                a single undo stack, so it can be undone and redone uniformly across every tab. Rapid
                repeated edits of the same kind (for example dragging a fader) are merged into one undo
                step rather than flooding the stack. <strong>How to use it:</strong> don't be afraid to
                try a mix move, an AI/SI suggestion, or a tracker effect - <span class="kbd">Ctrl+Z</span>
                gets you back exactly where you were.
            </p>
            <h3>Autosave &amp; Crash Recovery</h3>
            <p>
                UDAW periodically autosaves the current project and, separately, keeps a
                disk-streaming recoverable container for anything currently being recorded, so an
                unexpected shutdown during a take does not lose the audio already captured &ndash; the
                take container's index can be rebuilt and the material recovered on next launch. If UDAW
                is closed uncleanly, relaunching it will offer the most recent autosave/recording to
                recover from - accept it to pick up exactly where you left off.
            </p>
        </section>

        <!-- ============================================================ -->
        <section id="timeline">
            <h2>5. Timeline &amp; Non-Destructive Editing</h2>
            <div class="udaw-purpose">
                <p><b>Purpose</b> Arrange, trim, and time-align recorded or imported audio into a song.</p>
                <p><b>Why</b> Non-destructive editing means every trim/move/fade is reversible and the original audio file on disk is never rewritten - you can be aggressive about editing without fear of damaging your source recordings.</p>
            </div>
            <p>
                The Timeline models tracks and clips non-destructively: a clip references a region of
                underlying audio rather than copying/mutating it, so trimming, moving and fading clips
                never rewrites the source file. A multiresolution waveform peak cache keeps scrolling
                and zooming responsive even on long files.
            </p>
            <p>
                The band above the Timeline holds the transport: <strong>Play</strong> (press again to
                stop), <strong>Stop</strong>, <strong>Record</strong> (opens the Recording view) and
                <strong>Loop</strong>, then the large <strong>Time</strong> and <strong>Bars</strong> readouts
                of the playhead, the <strong>BPM</strong> box, the <strong>Snap</strong> choice, the
                metronome and lane switches, and the master volume and meter at the right. Every track
                header shows a coloured avatar for the kind of track, its name and a sub-label, a
                <strong>volume</strong> slider and a <strong>pan</strong> knob (double-click either to reset
                it), and three buttons at its top right. <strong>M</strong> (mute)
                instantly silences that track and stays pressed-in and highlighted while the track is
                muted; click it again to un-mute. <strong>S</strong> (solo) plays only the soloed
                track(s) and silences the rest. The round <strong>R</strong> button arms an audio track for
                recording: the next recording is placed on the armed track (otherwise on the selected
                audio track). The same mute toggle is also on the track's right-click menu
                (<strong>Mute Track</strong>). Mute and solo are live monitoring toggles, separate from the
                Timeline's undo history, and take effect immediately even mid-playback. Each track has its
                own colour, shared by its header stripe, its clips and its waveform; MIDI clips show their
                notes as small dashes (time across, pitch up). A video channel shows a film glyph, and a
                <strong>BPM ?</strong> chip appears on the primary audio
                channel while its tempo is unknown &ndash; click it to enter the tempo.
            </p>
            <h3 id="timeline-transport">Transport buttons and status box</h3>
            <div class="udaw-purpose">
                <p><b>Purpose</b> Know what every icon in the strip above the Timeline does, and what the numbers beside it tell you.</p>
                <p><b>Why</b> The strip is where you control playback and switch whole parts of the workspace on and off; the icons are small, so each one also shows a hint when you rest the pointer on it. A lit icon means the thing it controls is currently on.</p>
            </div>
            <table class="udaw-table udaw-col-22">
                <tr><td>Go to start / Go to end</td><td>Move the playhead to the beginning, or to the end of the last clip. <em>Why:</em> restart a passage or jump to the tail without scrolling.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Play</td><td>Plays from the playhead; press again to stop (<span class="kbd">Space</span> does the same). Playing to the end rewinds the playhead to 0.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Stop</td><td>Stops playback.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Record</td><td>Opens the Recording view where you arm tracks and record takes. <em>Why:</em> capture vocals or instruments against what is already on the Timeline.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Loop</td><td>Repeats the loop region while playing (lit = on). <em>Why:</em> rehearse or edit a short passage over and over.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Video</td><td>Shows or hides the video playback panel (lit = shown). The sound of a video clip is its own audio lane and keeps playing when the panel is hidden. <em>Why:</em> give the lanes more room when you do not need to see the picture.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Editor</td><td>Shows or hides the editor docked under the Timeline lanes - piano roll, mixer, tracker, recording, AI/SI and Foley pages (lit = shown). <em>Why:</em> edit while still seeing the arrangement above.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Clip Stage</td><td>Shows or hides the Clip Stage, a launcher grid of patterns as scenes (lit = shown).</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Browser</td><td>Shows or hides the browser panel on the right: instruments, effects, samples and presets (lit = shown).</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Metronome</td><td>A click on every beat while the Timeline plays (lit = on). <em>Why:</em> keep time when recording without a drum track.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Lanes</td><td>Shows or hides the Markers, Tempo and Signature lanes under the ruler (lit = shown).</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Chords</td><td>Shows or hides the Chords lane - chord symbols over the Timeline (lit = shown).</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Foley</td><td>Shows the <a href="#timeline-foley">Foley Sampler</a> in the docked editor under the lanes, so you can play sound-effect pads while the Timeline stays in view (lit while the Foley page is showing). Click it again to hide the dock. <em>Why:</em> the quickest route to performing effects against the picture.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Delete Cache</td><td>Removes the files in the local cache folder that this project (and your recent projects) no longer use. <em>Why:</em> win back disk space; a progress bar shows the work.</td></tr>
            </table>
            <p>
                To the right of the icons are the large <strong>Time</strong> and <strong>Bars</strong>
                readouts of the playhead, the <strong>BPM</strong> box and the <strong>Snap</strong> choice. The
                <strong>Master Vol</strong> slider and its level meter sit in the tab row at the very top of the
                window, after the last tab and a separator, so they are always in reach. The status box at the right end of
                the <strong>menu bar</strong> shows two things:
            </p>
            <ul>
                <li><strong>FPS</strong> (when ticked in the Settings page that controls the screen mode) &ndash; how many times per second the busiest part of the display is being redrawn, how many draws per second in all, and whether UDAW is in a window or full screen. <em>Why:</em> if scrolling or playback looks jerky, this tells you whether the display is keeping up; a lower number while nothing moves is normal, because a surface only redraws when something on it changes.</li>
                <li><strong>Out</strong> &ndash; the audio output device sound is really coming out of, taken from what the audio engine actually opened, with the audio backend in brackets. It reads <em>Audio: not open</em> when the engine is closed. <em>Why:</em> when you hear nothing (or hear it from the wrong speakers), this is the first place to look - it shows what UDAW is really using, not just what you asked for.</li>
            </ul>

            <h3 id="timeline-modes">Modes: Arrange, Piano, Score, Inst, Pattern, Mix, Tracker, Rec, AI/SI and Foley</h3>
            <div class="udaw-purpose">
                <p><b>Purpose</b> Give the whole window to the editor you are working in, and get back to the arrangement in one click.</p>
            </div>
            <p>
                The bar under the transport picks what fills the page. <b>Arrange</b> is the Timeline; its
                <b>Editor</b> switch opens an editor underneath it, and the splitter between them resizes
                only the editor. <b>Piano</b> is the piano roll with a keyboard, a bar ruler, the chord lane
                and a velocity lane; <b>Score</b> shows MIDI parts as music notation; <b>Inst</b> is the drum
                machine; <b>Pattern</b> is the step sequencer for rhythm patterns; <b>Mix</b>, <b>Tracker</b>, <b>Rec</b>, <b>AI/SI</b> and <b>Foley</b> are the mixer, tracker, recording, AI/SI and Foley Sampler pages. In Arrange the <b>tool</b> icons choose what a drag does: Select, Range (drag to
                select a time range), Split (click a clip to cut it) and Erase (click a clip to delete it).
                The <b>Browser</b> switch shows the panel on the right with four tabs: <b>Instruments</b>
                (double-click to add a track, or double-click a tracker instrument to open it in the WAV editor; drag an instrument onto the Timeline to add it at the playhead, or drag a tracker module file onto the Timeline to be asked how to add it), <b>Effects</b> (double-click to add an
                effect to the selected track), <b>Samples</b> (your library folders - drag a sound onto the Timeline to
                add it at the playhead, or double-click it to open it in the WAV editor) and <b>Presets</b> (mix roles for the selected track). Every list in
                the panel has a vertical and a horizontal scroll bar, so a long name can always be read in full
                (hold <b>Shift</b> and use the wheel to scroll sideways).
            </p>
            <h3 id="timeline-foley">Foley Sampler</h3>
            <div class="udaw-purpose">
                <p><b>Purpose</b> Play sound effects on sixteen pads - by mouse or keyboard - and put them on the Timeline exactly where you hit them.</p>
                <p><b>Why</b> Foley is the craft of adding everyday sounds (footsteps, doors, impacts, risers) to a picture or a song. Dragging a file onto the Timeline for every single sound is slow and hard to time; performing the sounds live while the project plays is quicker, feels natural and lands them on the beat.</p>
            </div>
            <h4>Opening it</h4>
            <p>
                There are four ways, all showing the same pads: the <strong>Foley</strong> icon in the
                Timeline's transport strip (shows it in the dock under the lanes, so the Timeline stays in view);
                the <strong>FOLEY</strong> tab on the mode bar (gives the whole page to it); the
                <strong>Foley</strong> tab of the docked editor when the <strong>Editor</strong> switch is on; and
                <strong>Timeline &gt; Foley Sampler...</strong>. If the panel is too short to show everything,
                it gets a vertical scroll bar, like the other docked pages.
            </p>
            <h4>Setting up the pads</h4>
            <ol>
                <li>Click a pad to select it (its key letter is on the pad: <b>1 2 3 4 / Q W E R / A S D F / Z X C V</b>). The line to the right names the selected pad and its sound.</li>
                <li>Choose <strong>Load into pad...</strong> and pick a WAV or MP3 file. <em>Why:</em> each pad holds one sound, so load the sounds you will need before you perform.</li>
                <li><strong>Clear pad</strong> empties the selected pad.</li>
            </ol>
            <p>
                Your pad choices are remembered on this computer between sessions - they are not stored inside
                the project file, so every project can use the same set. The clips you place on the Timeline do
                refer to the sound files, so keep those files where they are.
            </p>
            <h4>Playing</h4>
            <p>
                Click a pad, or press its key while the Foley page is showing (and no text box has the
                keyboard focus), to hear it. Several pads can ring at once, hitting a pad again restarts it,
                and they sound on top of whatever the Timeline is playing. Pads play through the
                <a href="#audio-output">Output device</a> chosen in Settings, so they come out of the same
                speakers as everything else.
            </p>
            <h4>Getting sounds onto the Timeline - three ways</h4>
            <table class="udaw-table udaw-col-22">
                <tr><td>Pad key adds its sound at the playhead</td><td>Tick <em>A pad key adds its sound at the playhead</em>. Each pad <b>key</b> press puts that pad's sound on the selected audio lane (or a dedicated Foley lane if none is selected) at the playhead. If the playhead is inside a clip, the clip is split there and the sound goes between the two halves, pushing the rest later by the sound's length. When the Timeline is stopped the playhead moves to the end of the sound, so the next key follows on. <em>Why:</em> assemble a sequence of effects quickly, one key press each, with every step undoable.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Record hits while the Timeline plays</td><td>Tick <em>Record hits while the Timeline plays</em>, start playback, and hit pads in time with the picture or music. UDAW notes the playhead time of every hit (the counter shows how many). Choose <strong>Add hits to Timeline</strong> to lay them out as clips - one new track per pad used, each clip at the moment you struck it - or <strong>Discard hits</strong> to throw them away. <em>Why:</em> performing against moving picture gets the timing right in a way placing clips by hand rarely does.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Place pad at playhead</td><td>Drops the selected pad's sound at the playhead on a new track, without recording or playing it. <em>Why:</em> exact, deliberate placement of a single sound.</td></tr>
            </table>
            <div class="udaw-note">
                <strong>Tip:</strong> hits are recorded only while the Timeline is playing, and a key press that inserts straight onto the Timeline is not also kept as a recorded hit.
            </div>

            <h3 id="timeline-clip-icons">Clip icons</h3>
            <p>
                Each clip carries a small icon at the left end of its header that says what it is: a waveform
                for audio, piano-roll dashes for MIDI, a dot grid for a tracker module, a step grid for a
                pattern and a film frame for video. <b>Click the icon</b> to open that clip's editor: the
                <b>Piano</b> roll for a MIDI clip, the <b>Tracker</b> for a module, the <b>Pattern</b> editor for
                a pattern and the <b>Mixer</b> strip of the clip's track for audio. The editor opens beside the
                arrangement (or fills the page when you are not in Arrange). Video clips are edited on the
                Timeline itself, so their icon is only a label. Very narrow clips hide the icon so their name
                stays readable.
            </p>
            <h3 id="timeline-chords">Chords lane</h3>
            <p>
                Switch <b>Chords</b> on in the transport band to add a lane under the Markers lane.
                Double-click it to type a chord symbol such as Am7 or F#m; drag a chord to move it, drag its
                right edge to resize it, double-click to rename it and right-click to delete it. Every edit
                is undoable. In the Piano view, <b>Detect chords</b> names the chord of every bar from the
                notes of the clip and writes them into the lane.
            </p>
            <h3 id="timeline-score">Score</h3>
            <p>
                The Score draws the MIDI parts you pick in the parts list on a treble, bass or grand staff,
                rounded to the quantize value you choose, with rests, accidentals, the time signature, bar
                numbers, the chord symbols and the playhead. Click the staves to move the playhead. It is a
                reader: notes cannot be edited on the staves, a note that crosses a bar line is cut at the
                bar, and flagged notes are not beamed.
            </p>
            <h3 id="timeline-drums">Drum machine</h3>
            <p>
                The Inst page has a pad for every MIDI note from 0 to 127, shown 16 at a time as a 4 x 4 bank
                (it opens on notes 36 to 51, C1 at the bottom left). Scroll the range with the bar at the
                right of the pads, the mouse wheel over them, or by clicking or dragging in the overview strip at
                their left - it shows all 128 pads, the loaded ones lit, with the bank you are looking at
                outlined. Click a pad to play it - the lower you click, the harder it plays - or drop a WAV file
                on it; press the <b>+</b> in a pad's corner (or double-click an empty pad) to open the file
                chooser. Every pad that holds a sound gets its own <b>instrument panel</b> beside the pads, in
                a rounded container whose title bar takes the pad's colour. The icons down its left side
                <b>play</b> the sound, <b>stop</b> it, <b>load</b> a different WAV and <b>remove</b> the
                instrument from the kit. Beside them are the instrument's name, level, pan, mute, solo,
                <b>choke group</b> (pads of one group cut each other off, as an open and a closed hi-hat do)
                and play mode (<b>One-shot</b> plays the whole sample, <b>Gate</b> stops when the key is
                released), with a graph of the sound underneath. The arrow at the right of the title bar
                folds a panel into a slim vertical strip (arrow pointing right, the name written upright);
                collapsed strips sit side by side, and click an arrow to open one again. A connected MIDI keyboard plays the pads,
                and any note on MIDI channel 10 of a MIDI clip plays the pad on that note during Timeline
                playback. The kit is saved with the project, graphs included, and comes back when the
                project is opened (a sound whose file has moved still shows its graph, marked as missing).
                WAV files only for now.
            </p>
            <h3 id="timeline-patterns">Pattern editor</h3>
            <p>
                The <b>Pattern</b> page is a step sequencer for rhythm patterns. Each row is an instrument (a
                drum pad, shown with its note) and each column a step. <b>Click</b> a step to write a hit, drag
                to paint a run, <b>right-click</b> to erase, and turn the <b>wheel</b> over a hit to set how
                hard it is struck. Click an instrument's name to select the row (and hear it); click the dot
                beside it to mute it. <b>New</b> starts a pattern with a row for every pad that holds a sample;
                <b>Add row</b> adds another pad, <b>Remove row</b> takes the selected one away. Set the
                <b>Length</b> in steps (a beat at a time) and the resolution (1/8, 1/16 or 1/32 - changing it keeps
                every hit at the same moment in the music). <b>Fill</b>, <b>Euclid</b>, <b>&lt;</b> / <b>&gt;</b>
                (rotate), <b>Random</b> and <b>Clear</b> act on the selected row, or on every row when none is
                selected. <b>Play</b> loops the pattern through the drum machine, and every edit is undoable.
            </p>
            <p>
                A pattern remembers the instruments it uses - each row's note, name and sample - so opening it on
                a machine whose pads are empty loads those samples back when the files are still there. Patterns
                are saved <b>in the project</b>. <b>Save</b> and <b>Save as...</b> also write a single pattern as a
                preset file to share or reuse in another project, and <b>Load</b> brings one in.
            </p>
            <p>
                <b>Place</b> puts the pattern on the Timeline at the playhead as a clip (on the selected MIDI
                track, or a new one). Place it as many times as you like: edit the pattern afterwards and every
                copy follows, and a copy you have stretched keeps the length you gave it. Because a placed pattern
                is an ordinary clip, it takes part in the <b>Clip Stage</b> scenes (a scene is a Timeline region)
                like any other clip. Deleting a pattern leaves the clips already placed as plain MIDI clips.
            </p>            <h3 id="timeline-clip-stage">Clip Stage (clip launcher)</h3>
            <p>
                Tick <strong>Show</strong> under <strong>CLIP STAGE</strong> in the Timeline toolbar to open a
                launcher column between the track headers and the tracks. Its <strong>scenes</strong> are the
                Timeline's named regions: press <strong>+ Scene</strong> to add one (from the ruler range
                selection, else the loop region, else four bars from the playhead). Each scene is a rounded
                panel with its name on a title bar in the scene's colour and three icons down its left side:
                <strong>play</strong> launches the scene, <strong>stop</strong> returns the tracks it launched
                to the arrangement, and <strong>remove</strong> deletes the scene from the project (undoable).
                Right-click a scene panel to launch, rename, delete it or set its range to the current selection. Every track has one
                <strong>slot</strong> per scene showing a small preview of what that track plays inside the
                scene's range. Press a scene button to launch the scene on every track &ndash; a track with
                nothing in the range stops &ndash; or press one slot to launch just that track. A launched
                track loops its own slice of the scene while the playhead and the arrangement carry on
                underneath it; a green banner on the track reads <em>Clip Stage: &lt;scene&gt; (looping)</em>
                (or <em>Stopped by the Clip Stage</em>), and its slot shows a green outline with a progress bar.
                A launch lands on the grid chosen in the <strong>launch quantize</strong> list (none, 1 beat,
                1 bar, 2 bars or 4 bars) and waits, shown in amber, until then; launching while stopped starts
                playback. <strong>Back to arrangement</strong> (on any banner, or the toolbar button) ends every
                override and returns to the written arrangement; stopping playback does the same. Use the mouse
                wheel over the column to scroll through more than four scenes.
            </p>
            <p>
                Each channel has its own <strong>title band</strong> directly above that channel's
                own header and clip filename - not the bar-number ruler at the very top of the
                Timeline, which always shows only the numerical bar/beat position. It is drawn in a
                small, clearly legible font so it never gets confused with another channel's title
                or with the ruler's bar labels. Every effect you add to that channel's mixer strip -
                EQ, dynamics, modulation, VST2 plugins, and the rest of the insert palette -
                automatically appends its name here, so you always have a visual record, right on
                that channel, of what was added. This title is saved as part of the project (on the
                channel itself), so it is never lost between sessions. When you render an effect onto a
                range (Edit &rarr; Effects), that effect's name also appears in the title band at the start of the
                affected region, in the effect's colour, and that region's waveform is drawn in the same colour.
            </p>
            <p>
                The channel list has its own vertical scrollbar on the right edge of the Timeline
                once there are more channels than fit on screen. The bar-number ruler at the top
                stays fixed in place the whole time - it never scrolls out of view - only the
                channels underneath it move.
            </p>
            <p>
                Right-click anywhere on the Timeline and choose <strong>Open Browser Window...</strong>
                (also on the Timeline menu bar) to pop the Browser tab's sample tree out into its own
                separate window. The docked Browser tab is unaffected and picks its contents back up
                automatically when the floating window is closed. This is useful for keeping the
                Browser visible - on a second monitor, for example - while dragging samples straight
                onto the Timeline at the playhead; drag-and-drop from the floating window works exactly
                the same as from the docked tab. <strong>Right-click</strong> a file or folder in the tree for the Windows shell menu - the same Open, Open with, Send to, Copy, Delete, Rename and Properties choices Explorer offers (hold Shift for the extended entries).
            </p>
            <p>
                Zoom In/Out, Start of Timeline, End of Timeline, and Next/Previous Grid Position are
                grouped together under a single <strong>Navigation</strong> submenu (Timeline menu bar
                and right-click menu), separated into a zoom group, a jump-to-start/end group, and a
                grid-stepping group.
            </p>
            <p>
                The header column's width is adjustable: hover the boundary between it and the clip
                lanes until the cursor becomes a horizontal resize arrow, then left-drag to make the
                column wider or narrower. The new width is saved as part of the project (not a global
                UDAW setting), so each project reopens with its header column exactly as you left it.
            </p>
            <p>
                <strong>Video channels.</strong> A video file brought onto the Timeline gets its own
                <strong>VIDEO</strong> channel, tagged in its header, with its sound on a matching audio
                channel. The clip is drawn as a film strip with a <strong>VIDEO</strong> label at its
                start; the strip and label scroll with the rest of the channels, including while the
                Timeline follows the playhead during playback. Rest the mouse over a video clip and a
                small <strong>preview frame</strong> appears beside the pointer, showing the picture at
                that point of the clip together with its time, so you can find a moment without playing
                it. The preview appears the first time you hover a spot (a short pause while the frame is
                prepared) and needs a video format your system can decode. Video clips move, trim, split
                and fade like any other clip.
            </p>
            <p>
                <strong>Video Effects.</strong> Right-click a video channel and choose
                <strong>Video Effects</strong> (it is also in the <strong>Edit</strong> menu). The
                submenu is available only on a video channel, and <strong>Audio Effects</strong> is
                available only on audio channels. Effects are grouped under <em>Transitions</em> and <em>Effects</em>.
                <strong>Transitions:</strong> <em>Dissolve</em> (Cross, Additive, Non-Additive, Dither, Blur and
                Pixelate Dissolve, Dip to Black/White, Burn Away), <em>Iris</em> (Iris Open/Close, Square,
                Diamond, Oval and Cross Iris), <em>Wipe</em> (Wipe Left/Right/Up/Down, Clock, Barn Doors, Band,
                Venetian and Vertical Blinds, Checker, Diagonal, Pinwheel, Fan, Spiral, Zig-Zag, X, Random
                Blocks, Inset, Luma Wipe), <em>Motion</em> (Push, Slide, Split Open, Squeeze, Card Flip,
                Spin, Whip Pan, Ripple, Swirl and Zoom Blur Dissolve, Band Slide, Zoom In/Out, Glitch) and
                <em>Shape</em> (Heart, Star, Triangle, Pentagon, Hexagon).
                <strong>Effects:</strong> <em>Fade</em> (Fade In/Out, Flash, Blur In/Out), <em>Blur</em> (Box,
                Gaussian, Motion, Directional, Radial, Zoom, Lens, Prism, Tilt-Shift, Soft Focus, Mosaic),
                <em>Sharpen</em> (Sharpen, Unsharp Mask, Ultra Sharpen, Sharpen Edges, Edge Enhance, Detail
                Recovery, Texture Pop), <em>Colour</em> (black and white, sepia, grades, hue shift and cycle,
                exposure, gamma, auto levels, white balance, bleach bypass, cross process, teal and orange,
                split tone, false colour, thermal, night vision, contrast pop, colour compressor, cyanotype,
                retro palette, despill, film look and more), <em>Light</em> (Vignette, Glow, Bloom, Halation,
                Cinematic Haze, Light Rays, Lens Flare, Star Glow, Spotlight, Light Leak, Strobe, Flicker),
                <em>Stylize</em> (Sketch, Emboss, Edge Detect, Pencil, Cartoon, Oil Paint, Watercolor,
                Halftone, Anaglyph 3D, Abstraction, X-Ray, Dither, Shrink, Grow, JPEG Damage),
                <em>Texture</em> (Film Grain, Scanlines, Colour Noise, Film Damage, Old Film, Canvas, CRT),
                <em>Transform</em> (Mirror, Flip, Four-Way Mirror, Kaleidoscope, Tile, Rotate, Crop and Zoom,
                Keystone), <em>Warp</em> (Bulge, Dent, Lens Distortion, Fisheye, Swirl, Vortex, Ripple, Waviness,
                Magnify, Heat Haze, Underwater), <em>Glitch</em> (Glitch, RGB Split, Chromatic Aberration,
                Analog Damage, Digital Glitch, TV Static, Interlace Tear), <em>Revival</em> (Noise Reduction,
                Despeckle, Dead Pixel Fixer, Deband, Dehaze), <em>Beauty</em> (Soften Skin, Glamour Glow),
                <em>Generate</em> (Letterbox, Border, Gradient, Grid, Colour Bars, Checkerboard, Solid Colour,
                Clouds) and <em>Camera Motion</em> (Shake, Slow Zoom In/Out, Spin, Zoom Pulse, Pans, Ken Burns,
                Dutch Angle, Handheld, Bounce, Earthquake).
            </p>
            <ul>
                <li><strong>With a range marked</strong> (drag on the ruler): the video is split at both
                    edges of the range and the effect fills it. A transition pivots on the playhead when
                    the playhead is inside the range, otherwise on the start of the range.</li>
                <li><strong>No range, transition:</strong> put the playhead on the cut between two video
                    clips, or anywhere on a clip - the clip is split there - and the transition is laid
                    one second wide across the cut. Before the cut the incoming clip is held on its first
                    picture; after it the outgoing clip is held on its last picture.</li>
                <li><strong>No range, other effect:</strong> the effect covers the video clip under the
                    playhead.</li>
                <li>Effects show as bars along the bottom of the video channel (orange for transitions,
                    purple for effects; a white line marks a transition's cut) and are saved with the
                    project. While the playhead is inside an effect, the video pane shows the processed
                    picture (a preview of about ten pictures a second - it may lag on large videos);
                    the exported MP4/MKV is rendered picture by picture at full quality. The status bar
                    shows progress while an effect is applied and while a video is exported.</li>
                <li><strong>Playing with effects.</strong> When the video holds effects, pressing Play
                    first <strong>pre-renders</strong> the picture so the effects and transitions play
                    smoothly; the status bar shows the progress and an estimate of the time left, and the
                    audio is prepared at the same time. Playback starts as soon as both are ready, and
                    <strong>Stop</strong> cancels the wait. The pre-render is a lighter, silent preview
                    copy of the picture (at most 960 pixels wide); your video files and exports are not
                    changed. The rendered picture is <strong>kept in the cache</strong> and found again
                    automatically: as long as the video clips, their files and the effects are unchanged,
                    the next Play starts at once with no pre-render. Any change to a clip or an effect
                    renders a fresh copy, and old cached copies are cleaned up (the six most recent are
                    kept). While stopped, scrubbing shows a quick preview of the effect in the video pane.</li>
                <li><strong>Remove Video Effect at Playhead</strong> (last item of the submenu) removes the
                    effects under the playhead or touching the marked range. Applying and removing are
                    ordinary undoable edits. Effects are tied to their time on the Timeline: if you move
                    clips afterwards, remove and re-apply the effect. The sound is not changed by a video
                    effect.</li>
            </ul>
            <p>
                A <strong>BPM</strong> field sits in the Timeline toolbar next to the snap control. It
                reads and writes the exact same project tempo as the <a href="#tab-project">Project</a>
                tab's own tempo field - the same undo-tracked value, not a separate per-tab setting - so
                editing it from either tab keeps both in sync automatically. This tempo drives the
                Timeline's beat/bar grid (and therefore snap alignment and bar/beat markers), the
                <a href="#tab-tracker">Tracker</a>'s default speed, MIDI playback timing, and the tempo
                baked into any offline render/export - change it here when you want the whole project,
                not just one tab's display, to play at a different speed.
            </p>
            <p>
                Grid and snap settings control how clips (and the playhead, when you click/drag the
                ruler) align while dragging - turn snap on when you want clips to lock to the beat grid
                (e.g. lining up a drum loop), and off for freehand placement (e.g. nudging a vocal ad-lib
                slightly late for feel). The snap dropdown offers:
            </p>
            <table class="udaw-table udaw-col-22">
                <tr><th>Snap mode</th><th>Locks to</th></tr>
                <tr><td>Off</td><td>Nothing - free placement at the exact mouse/drop position.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Bar</td><td>Bar lines, using the project's tempo/time signature.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Beat</td><td>Beat lines (a quarter note in 4/4). The default.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>1/2, 1/4, 1/6, 1/8, 1/16 beat, Triplet</td><td>Sub-beat divisions, for lining
                    up faster material (hi-hat rolls, arpeggios) to the grid.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Seconds</td><td>Whole-second marks - useful when aligning to a fixed-length
                    video/animation cue rather than a musical grid.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Realtime</td><td>The finest grid available: the nearest single audio sample.
                    Edits track your exact input in real time rather than a musical subdivision - use
                    this when a beat/bar grid would fight you, e.g. aligning a spoken-word clip to a
                    precise waveform transient.</td></tr>
            </table>
            <p>
                The timeline has its own Play/Stop transport (<code>TTimelinePlayer</code>) that renders
                the current arrangement live rather than requiring an offline bounce to preview it, so you
                always hear exactly what the mix currently sounds like. While playing, the playhead line
                on the ruler tracks the actual audio position in real time (about 30 times a second), not
                just in coarse jumps. Reaching the end of the arrangement stops the transport
                automatically, just like a normal DAW - it will not run on playing silence forever, and
                the playhead snaps back to the very start (position 0) so the next press of Play starts
                the arrangement over rather than resuming from the end. The one exception is the
                <strong>region loop</strong> (set a loop start/length on the ruler and enable it): with
                looping engaged, playback wraps back to the loop start instead of stopping, for
                repeatedly auditioning one section. Manually pressing Stop leaves the playhead wherever
                it was, only reaching the end on its own triggers the rewind. See
                <a href="#mixer-volume">Playback Volume: Strip vs. Timeline Master</a> for how this tab's
                own master volume slider relates to the Mixer's master strip.
            </p>
            <h4>How to build an arrangement</h4>
            <p>
                To reuse an existing DAW arrangement, choose <strong>Import to Timeline</strong> and select
                an Ableton Live Set (<code>.als</code>) or FL Studio Project (<code>.flp</code>). UDAW creates
                ordinary, non-destructive audio tracks and clips for each referenced sample it can find and
                decode. The whole import is one Undo step. MIDI/pattern data, plug-ins, automation, and
                missing or unsupported audio are left untouched and reported in the Timeline log rather than
                guessed at. Pro Tools and Logic project files are not yet import formats.
            </p>
            <ol>
                <li>Add a track (audio, or drag in an imported WAV/MP3/MP4 to create one automatically).</li>
                <li>Drag the clip's edges to trim silence or unwanted material - this only changes what
                    region of the source is played, the file itself is untouched.</li>
                <li>Drag a clip left/right to move it in time; hold snap on to lock it to the grid.</li>
                <li>Duplicate/copy a clip to repeat a section (a chorus, a drum loop) without re-recording it.</li>
                <li>Add markers for song sections (intro/verse/chorus) to navigate a long arrangement quickly.</li>
                <li>Press Play to audition the arrangement live; adjust levels afterwards on the <a href="#tab-mixer">Mixer</a>.</li>
                <li><strong>Ctrl + left-drag</strong> highlights a region anywhere: on the Timeline lanes (even across clips, same range selection as the ruler drag), and as a visual highlight in the Piano Roll (grid-snapped), the Recording takes view and the Mixer automation lane. <strong>Shift + drag</strong> on empty Timeline space draws the rubber-band clip selection (this key is configurable in the code and may change).</li>
                <li>Left-drag on the ruler to make a time-range (section) selection spanning every track - highlighted with a tinted ruler strip and dashed boundary lines, independent of clip selection. Right-click the ruler for range actions: <strong>Split Clips at Range Edges</strong>, <strong>Delete Range</strong> (removes whatever falls inside, splitting first so partially-overlapped clips keep their outside portion), <strong>Fade In Range</strong> / <strong>Fade Out Range</strong> (a per-clip volume ramp over however much of the range covers a clip's head/tail - handy for building a crossfade by selecting the overlap and fading each side), and <strong>Set Loop to Selection</strong>.</li>
                <li><strong>Cut Range</strong> / <strong>Copy Range</strong> / <strong>Paste Range at Playhead</strong> (ruler right-click menu; also <strong>Cut Selection</strong> / <strong>Copy Selection</strong> / <strong>Paste Selection</strong> under Edit) record <em>everything</em> inside the selected range, not just the sound: each clip&#39;s audio for exactly that span (a clip that only partly overlaps contributes just its inside part), every effect rendered onto it with its name, colour and settings, the channel&#39;s title, and the markers and regions in the range. Cut and copy act on the selected channel(s) - or every channel when none is selected - and paste re-creates the lot at the playhead, starting on the channel that is active when you paste. Cutting across every channel also removes the range&#39;s markers and regions; cutting a single channel leaves those in place, since they belong to the whole timeline. Locked clips are never cut. Every step can be undone.</li>
                <li><strong>Clip Tempo (BPM)...</strong> (Edit menu and right-click) - makes audio recorded or downloaded at different speeds play together at the project tempo.
                    <ul>
                        <li><strong>What it does:</strong> you tell UDAW the tempo the audio <em>was made at</em> (the file's own tempo, <em>not</em> the tempo you want). Playback and every export then time-stretch that audio - pitch is kept - to the project's Timeline BPM (the BPM box on the Timeline/Project tab). A loop made at 100 BPM plays at 140 BPM in a 140 BPM project. Entering the same number as the project BPM changes nothing; entering 0 turns it off so the audio plays at its own speed.</li>
                        <li><strong>When to use it:</strong> whenever a channel's audio was not made at the project tempo - loops and samples from other packs, Suno/AI generations, imported songs. Audio that is already at the project tempo (or that you don't want changed) needs nothing. Audio with no Clip Tempo always plays and exports at its own native speed, even if the project is set to 140.</li>
                        <li><strong>How to use it, step by step:</strong> 1) Set the project tempo first (e.g. 140) in the Timeline BPM box - do this <em>before</em> Clip Tempo, because clip lengths are calculated from the tempo at the moment you apply it. 2) Click a clip (or the channel) that needs conforming. 3) Choose <strong>Clip Tempo (BPM)...</strong>. 4) Type the audio's original tempo, or type <em>auto</em> to detect it. 5) Repeat for each channel whose audio is at a different tempo - each channel can have its own original tempo (one at 100, another at 128, another at 174) and they will all lock to the same project tempo. Press Play or export to hear/render the result.</li>
                        <li><strong>Applies to the whole channel:</strong> Clip Tempo is applied to every audio clip on the channel(s) of the clip(s) you selected, or on the selected channel if no clip is selected, so the other clips on that channel don't export at the wrong speed. Clips you copy and paste do not carry the tempo over - set it again on the new channel.</li>
                        <li><strong>What changes on screen:</strong> the clip's length on the Timeline is rescaled to the stretched audio and its waveform is redrawn; the status bar and log report each clip's old/new tempo, stretch factor and length, and the new total time. The change is undoable. The stretch itself is built when you press Play or export. Stretch is supported between one quarter and four times the original speed.</li>
                        <li><strong>Baseline tempo (automatic):</strong> the first audio file imported into a project has its BPM detected automatically; that becomes the Timeline BPM and the clip's own tempo, and every clip added later conforms to it on Play and export. If the tempo cannot be detected (very short or rhythm-free audio) the Timeline BPM is left alone and a yellow <strong>?</strong> button appears at the bottom-right of the first audio channel's header - click it to type the tempo (or <em>auto</em>); the ? disappears once the tempo is known. Turn auto-detect off in Settings.</li>
                        <li><strong>Pitch or not (Settings):</strong> by default tempo matching keeps the pitch (phase vocoder). Tick <em>Conform clip tempo by resampling</em> in Settings to shrink/stretch the audio like tape instead, so pitch follows tempo. Play and export use the same setting.</li>
                        <li><strong>Not during playback:</strong> Clip Tempo cannot be changed while playing - stop playback first. If you change the project tempo afterwards, re-apply Clip Tempo (or set the project tempo first) so clip lengths stay in step.</li>
                    </ul>
                </li>
                <li><strong>Loop bar.</strong> The bottom strip of the ruler is the loop bar. Drag across empty strip to make a loop (it switches on and snaps to the grid), drag either edge to resize it, drag its body to move it, and double-click it to switch the loop on or off. A dashed guide line shows the grid position you are snapping to while you drag, trim or edit the loop.</li>
                <li><strong>Markers, regions, tempo and time signature (the lanes under the ruler).</strong> Double-click empty marker lane to add a marker; <strong>drag a marker flag</strong> to move it (snapped), click it to jump the playhead there, double-click it to rename it. Drag empty marker lane to make a region; drag a region's body to move it or either edge to resize it. Every move, resize and rename can be undone. <strong>Tempo lane (several tempos):</strong> the first point is the project tempo; double-click empty tempo lane to add a tempo change at that time (it starts at the tempo already in force). Drag a point up or down to change its tempo (one BPM per two pixels, hold <span class="kbd">Shift</span> for tenths) and, for any point after the first, left or right to move it (snapped). Double-click a point to type its tempo (the first point edits the project tempo), right-click a change to delete it, and use the mouse wheel over the lane to retune the tempo section under the pointer. The tempo line is a step line that rises and falls with the value. The ruler's bars and beats, the grid and snapping, the bar:beat position and the metronome all follow the tempo changes; audio clips stay at their position in time, and audio clips conform to the tempo in force where they start, and MIDI clips follow the tempo changes while playing. Tempo changes are saved in the project, come back when it is opened, and can be undone. <strong>Signature lane:</strong> double-click and type a time signature such as 3/4 or 6/8.</li>
                <li><strong>More Timeline keys</strong> (Timeline tab, not while typing): <span class="kbd">S</span> split the selected clip at the playhead; <span class="kbd">M</span> add a marker at the playhead; <span class="kbd">,</span> / <span class="kbd">.</span> jump to the previous / next marker; <span class="kbd">L</span> loop on / off; <span class="kbd">[</span> / <span class="kbd">]</span> set the loop start / end at the playhead; <span class="kbd">F</span> zoom to fit the whole project; <span class="kbd">Z</span> zoom to the highlighted range or the selected clips; <span class="kbd">+</span> / <span class="kbd">-</span> zoom in / out; <span class="kbd">Left</span> / <span class="kbd">Right</span> nudge the selected clips (or the playhead when nothing is selected) by one snap step, and <span class="kbd">Shift</span>+<span class="kbd">Left</span> / <span class="kbd">Right</span> by four steps. Markers are saved in the project and come back when it is opened.</li>
                <li><strong>Fixed keyboard shortcuts (Timeline tab, cannot be remapped):</strong> <span class="kbd">Delete</span> removes the active channel and its Mixer strip; <span class="kbd">Backspace</span> removes the selected clip; <span class="kbd">Insert</span> adds a new channel (which becomes the active channel, so Paste lands on it); <span class="kbd">Home</span> / <span class="kbd">End</span> jump to the start / end of the Timeline. Paste puts the copied clip on the active channel. They are ignored while you are typing in a text field, and Delete is ignored during playback.</li>
                <li><strong>Playback preparation and caching:</strong> pressing Play decodes each source and builds any tempo-stretched clips as a background job - the window stays responsive and the status bar names the current stage (decoding source n of m, time-stretching clip n of m). Decoded and stretched audio is kept between Plays, so pressing Play again starts immediately; only a clip whose source or tempo changed is redone. Stop cancels a preparation in progress.</li>
                <li><strong>MP3 / MP4 / M4A export and tempo:</strong> compressed exports are rendered at 48 kHz. When every audio clip has a Clip Tempo, the project BPM is written into the file (MP3 <code>TBPM</code> tag; MP4/M4A <code>tmpo</code> atom). If any audio clip has no Clip Tempo the tag is omitted, since that audio keeps its own tempo. After encoding, UDAW re-reads the saved file, aborts if its sample rate or length differs from the rendered mix, and logs the saved file's sample rate, frame rate (AAC/MP3 frames per second), duration and tempo.</li>
                <li>The same range selection also unlocks the <strong>Audio Effects</strong> submenu of the <strong>Edit</strong> menu (menu bar and right-click) once a range is active on an audio channel - it is disabled on a video channel, which has its own <strong>Video Effects</strong> submenu (the full alphabetical list is in the next item). Most entries offline-render the chosen DSP effect - the same processing the <a href="#tab-mixer">Mixer</a> tab's insert palette uses - over just the selected clips and swaps each clip onto the rendered result. Unlike ordinary trim/move/fade edits, applying an effect this way does write new audio (a fresh WAV file per affected clip, registered as a new source) but is still a normal, undoable Timeline edit.</li>
                <li><strong>Audio Effects menu (grouped):</strong> with a range selected, <strong>Edit &gt; Audio Effects</strong> (menu bar and right-click) is organised into category submenus - <strong>Volume &amp; Fades</strong> (Amplify, Normalize, Loudness Normalize, Fade In, Fade Out, Silence), <strong>Dynamics</strong> (Compressor, Multiband Compressor, Limiter, Expander, Gate, De-Esser, Transient Shaper), <strong>EQ &amp; Filters</strong> (Bass Enhance, Brighten, Presence Boost, Treble Boost, High-Pass, Low-Pass, Band-Pass, Notch), <strong>Distortion &amp; Saturation</strong> (Bit Crusher, Lo-Fi, Exciter, Foldback, Hard Clip, Soft Clip, Saturation, Tube Saturation), <strong>Modulation</strong> (Auto-Pan, Chorus, Flanger, Phaser, Tremolo, Ring Modulator, Vibrato), <strong>Delay &amp; Reverb</strong> (Delay, Ping-Pong Delay, Echo, Slapback Delay, Reverb, Hall, Room and Plate Reverb, Convolution Reverb), <strong>Pitch</strong> (Pitch Shift, Auto-Tune), <strong>Stereo</strong> (Balance, Make Mono, Stereo Widener, Swap Channels, Invert Phase), <strong>Restoration</strong> (Noise Reduction, DC Offset Remover, De-Hummer 50/60 Hz, De-Clicker, De-Clipper), <strong>Voice</strong> (Vocal Channel, Dialogue Leveler, Telephone) and <strong>Tools</strong> (Reverse, Split Stems, Audio to MIDI), with <strong>Undo Effect</strong> below them. The effects are described below by type. Each applies to the selected channel only, over just the selected range, and never cuts the clip.
                    <ul>
                        <li><strong>Level and dynamics:</strong> <strong>Amplify</strong> (asks for a gain in dB - positive boosts, negative cuts, limited to &plusmn;60 dB), <strong>Compressor</strong>, <strong>De-Esser</strong> (tames harsh &quot;s&quot; sounds), <strong>Expander</strong>, <strong>Gate</strong> (silences the signal below a threshold), <strong>Limiter</strong> (stops peaks going over -0.3 dB), <strong>Multiband Compressor</strong> (compresses low, mid and high bands separately), <strong>Transient Shaper</strong> (adds punch to attacks).</li>
                        <li><strong>Tone and filters:</strong> <strong>Bass Enhance</strong> (+6 dB low shelf at 100 Hz), <strong>Brighten (EQ)</strong>, <strong>DC Offset Remover</strong>, <strong>High-Pass Filter</strong> (removes rumble below 80 Hz), <strong>Low-Pass Filter</strong> (removes highs above 8 kHz), <strong>Notch Filter</strong> (cuts a narrow band at 60 Hz - mains hum), <strong>Presence Boost</strong> (+4 dB around 3 kHz for vocals and leads), <strong>Treble Boost</strong> (+6 dB high shelf at 8 kHz), <strong>Exciter</strong> (adds high-frequency harmonics).</li>
                        <li><strong>Stereo:</strong> <strong>Auto-Pan</strong>, <strong>Balance</strong> (asks for -100 left to +100 right; uses a constant-power pan law, so the centre setting is about 3 dB quieter), <strong>Make Mono</strong> (folds the selection to mono), <strong>Stereo Widener</strong>.</li>
                        <li><strong>Time and space:</strong> <strong>Chorus</strong>, <strong>Delay</strong>, <strong>Flanger</strong>, <strong>Phaser</strong>, <strong>Ping-Pong Delay</strong>, <strong>Reverb</strong>, <strong>Tremolo</strong>. Echo and reverb tails are cut off at the end of the selection.</li>
                        <li><strong>Distortion:</strong> <strong>Bit Crusher</strong>, <strong>Foldback</strong> (wave folder), <strong>Hard Clip</strong>, <strong>Saturation</strong>, <strong>Soft Clip</strong> (smooth, gentle limiting), <strong>Tube Saturation</strong> (warm, even-harmonic glow).</li>
                        <li><strong>Pitch:</strong> <strong>Pitch Shift</strong> (asks for semitones, -24 to +24; negative goes down, -12 is one octave).</li>
                        <li><strong>Restoration and creative tools:</strong> <strong>Noise Reduction...</strong> (learns the noise from the first 0.4 s of the selection, so start the selection on noise-only audio; you choose 1-40 dB of reduction), <strong>Auto-Tune...</strong> (type a scale such as &quot;C major&quot;, &quot;A minor&quot; or &quot;chromatic&quot; and each note is pulled to the nearest scale note) and <strong>Convolution Reverb...</strong> (choose a WAV impulse response of any real space or hardware unit; it is blended in at 30 % wet). All three show per-channel progress in the status bar and are undoable like every other range effect.</li>
                        <li><strong>Level tools (no audio rewritten):</strong> <strong>Normalize</strong> peak-normalizes each selected clip to -0.3 dBFS with a plain gain adjustment; <strong>Reverse</strong> flips playback direction for each selected clip.</li>
                        <li><strong>Fades and silence:</strong> <strong>Fade In</strong> ramps the level from silence to full across the selection and <strong>Fade Out</strong> from full to silence, wherever the selection sits inside the clip (audio outside it is untouched); they are rendered like the other effects and drawn in the Fade In / Fade Out background colours. (The ruler's right-click <strong>Fade In Range</strong> / <strong>Fade Out Range</strong> are the older clip-edge fades and still only act on clips whose start or end lies inside the range.); <strong>Silence</strong> mutes just the selected stretch. <strong>Undo Effect</strong> restores the original sound (from the untouched imported file) over the selected range on the selected channel(s) and removes the effect label for that part; imported files are never modified, and the restore itself can be undone. To change or remove one particular effect, click its name in the channel's title band (left or right button), or simply click inside the effect's coloured region on the clip (a plain click never moves the clip &ndash; a clip only moves once you actually drag it): exactly the section that effect covers is highlighted on that channel and a small menu opens with two choices. <strong>Edit Effect...</strong> shows the settings that effect was applied with (for example <em>thresh=-18;ratio=3</em> for a compressor, or the gain for Amplify); change any value and press OK, and the original sound is put back over that section and the effect is applied again with your new settings. Effects without settings (Fade In, Fade Out, Silence) offer only Undo. <strong>Undo Effect</strong> puts the original sound back over that section. The original is taken from the untouched source of the clip; if no original was kept for it, the same stretch of time is taken from your <strong>Master</strong> channel instead (the channel named &quot;Master&quot; &ndash; use Rename Channel to name one), as long as a single audio clip there covers the whole section. Every clip in the selection is checked first; if any of them cannot be restored (for example the original file has been moved or deleted and there is no Master channel covering it, the clip is locked, or the effect changed the timing, as Warp does), nothing is changed and a message explains why the effect cannot be undone. Edit and Undo both restore every effect inside the highlighted section, and both can be reversed with Edit &rarr; Undo.</li>
                    </ul>
                    Applying several effects to different (or overlapping) ranges keeps every one - each keeps its own coloured span, waveform colour and name label on the clip, starting where its selection started. Every effect has its own colour, editable under <strong>Settings &gt; Colors</strong> (&quot;Effect: Amplify&quot;, &quot;Effect: Bass Enhance&quot;, and so on; the fade colours are &quot;Fade in: Background&quot; and &quot;Fade out: Background&quot;).</li>
            </ol>

            <h3 id="timeline-advanced">Stems, Audio to MIDI, Warp Markers and the Metronome</h3>
            <div class="udaw-purpose">
                <p><b>Purpose</b> The tools that most often send people to a second application: separating a mix, turning a recording into notes, locking loose audio to the grid and playing to a click.</p>
                <p><b>Why</b> They are built in, work offline, and every result lands on the Timeline as ordinary undoable clips.</p>
            </div>
            <table class="udaw-table udaw-col-22">
                <tr><th>Tool</th><th>How</th></tr>
                <tr><td>Echo</td><td>Repeating echoes with a darkening tail; the right channel's echo is 1.5 times later than the left for a wide stereo spread. Also available as <b>Slapback Delay</b> (a single short repeat).</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Swap Channels / Invert Phase</td><td>Swap Channels trades left and right; Invert Phase flips the polarity of the selection, useful to fix a mis-wired microphone or to cancel a phase clash.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Ring Modulator / Vibrato</td><td>Ring Modulator multiplies the sound by a 440 Hz tone for metallic, robotic colours; Vibrato gently wobbles the pitch (about 5.5 times a second).</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Vocal Channel</td><td>A ready-made voice chain in one step: rumble filter below 85 Hz, a cut at 300 Hz to clear mud, a lift around 3.5 kHz for presence, extra air above 11 kHz and gentle compression.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Dialogue Leveler</td><td>Slowly rides the level of speech toward a steady target so quiet phrases come up and loud ones come down. It leaves silence and room noise alone instead of boosting them.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Loudness Normalize</td><td>Measures the selection's overall loudness and raises or lowers it to -16 LUFS (a common streaming level), never letting the peaks pass full scale.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>De-Hummer 50 / 60 Hz</td><td>Removes mains hum and its overtones with narrow notches; pick the one matching your electricity supply.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>De-Clicker / De-Clipper</td><td>De-Clicker finds short pops and clicks and redraws the audio across them. De-Clipper rebuilds the flattened tops of overloaded (clipped) waves, then keeps the result under full scale.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Band-Pass / Telephone</td><td>Band-Pass keeps only a band around 1 kHz. Telephone gives the narrow 300 Hz to 3.4 kHz sound of a phone line with a touch of handset distortion.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Wah-Wah / Auto-Filter</td><td><b>Audio Effects &gt; Tone &amp; Filters</b>. Wah-Wah sweeps a resonant band up and down like a guitar pedal; Auto-Filter slowly opens and closes a low-pass filter for a pulsing, breathing sound.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Vowel Filter (Ah, Eh, Ee, Oh, Oo)</td><td>Shapes the sound with the resonances of a human mouth so any instrument seems to speak the chosen vowel. Level is kept constant.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Tilt EQ (Brighter / Darker)</td><td>Tips the whole tonal balance toward the highs or the lows with one move, 3 dB either way, the way a tone knob does.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Haas Widener / Mono Bass / Vocal Remover</td><td><b>Audio Effects &gt; Stereo &amp; Bass</b>. Haas Widener delays the right side by a few milliseconds for a wide feel without changing tone. Mono Bass makes everything below 120 Hz identical in both speakers, which tightens a mix and suits vinyl and club systems. Vocal Remover cancels what is panned dead centre (usually the lead vocal) while keeping the bass.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Tape Wow &amp; Flutter / AM Radio / Fuzz</td><td><b>Audio Effects &gt; Creative</b>. Tape adds warm saturation with the slow pitch drift of an old machine. AM Radio gives a band-limited, slightly noisy broadcast sound. Fuzz is a heavily driven, asymmetric guitar-pedal distortion.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Octave-Up Rectifier / String Ensemble / Stutter</td><td>Octave-Up Rectifier folds the waveform to add a note an octave above. String Ensemble thickens a sound with three gently moving copies. Stutter repeats short slices of the sound four times every quarter second for a glitch effect.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Multi-Tap Delay / Tape Delay</td><td><b>Audio Effects &gt; Time &amp; Space</b>. Multi-Tap Delay adds four evenly spaced, fading echoes. Tape Delay is a repeating echo that gets darker, softer and slightly unsteady on each pass.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Parallel Compression</td><td><b>Audio Effects &gt; Dynamics</b>. Blends the original with a heavily compressed copy, so quiet detail comes up while the punch of the loud parts stays.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Level Rider / Dialog Rider</td><td><b>Audio Effects &gt; Dynamics</b>. Slowly turns the volume up and down, like a hand on a fader, so the selection stays near a steady level (within 6 dB; 9 dB for Dialog Rider). Dialog Rider listens only to the speech range and holds still during silence, so room noise is not pumped up.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>True-Peak Limiter</td><td>A look-ahead limiter that also catches the peaks between samples, so the finished file stays below -1 dBTP and does not clip after conversion to MP3 or streaming formats.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>De-Popper (Plosive Remover)</td><td><b>Audio Effects &gt; Restoration</b>. Finds the low-frequency thumps of &ldquo;p&rdquo; and &ldquo;b&rdquo; sounds on a voice recording and turns only the lows down for that instant.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Adaptive Noise Reduction</td><td>Reduces steady background noise without needing a noise sample: it finds the quietest moment in your selection and learns the noise from there.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Oversampled Saturation</td><td><b>Audio Effects &gt; Distortion &amp; Saturation</b>. Warm saturation computed at four times the sample rate, which avoids the harsh, gritty overtones ordinary digital distortion can produce.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Fade curves</td><td><b>Audio Effects &gt; Volume &amp; Fades</b>. Fade In and Fade Out in S-Curve, Equal Power (for crossfades), Exponential and Logarithmic shapes.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Loudness &amp; Phase Report</td><td><b>Audio Effects &gt; Tools</b>. Measures the marked range and lists, in the Timeline log: integrated, momentary and short-term loudness (LUFS), sample and true peak, RMS, stereo correlation (to spot phase problems), the balance between the middle and the sides, and any DC offset. Nothing in the project is changed.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Voice Isolation / Music Separator</td><td><b>Audio Effects &gt; Voice</b>. Voice Isolation keeps the voice and lowers steady background (music pads, hum, room tone). Music Separator does the opposite and keeps the background. They work by measuring which parts of the sound move like speech, sit in the voice range and are centred between the speakers; they are not an AI/SI model, so busy mixes will not separate as cleanly as a single voice over steady noise.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Split Speech / Music</td><td><b>Audio Effects &gt; Tools</b>. Select a clip and two new tracks are added, one with the speech and one with the music/background, so you can balance or process them separately. Undoable like any edit.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Ducker</td><td><b>Mixer &gt; Add insert &gt; Ducker (sidechain)</b>. Lowers a track whenever another track is loud, for example music that dips under a voice-over. Type the strip number of the track to listen to into the <b>sidechain</b> row; <b>range</b> is how far it dips (-12 dB by default), <b>attack</b>, <b>hold</b> and <b>release</b> shape the movement.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Apply mix role</td><td>Right-click the Mixer and choose <b>Apply mix role</b> for a ready starting chain on the selected track: Dialogue, Voice-over, Music, Sound Effects, Ambience or Master. Each effect is added as a normal insert you can change, bypass or remove.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Effect curves</td><td>The parameter window of an EQ, filter, compressor, gate, expander, ducker or limiter insert now shows a live graph below the list: the frequency response of EQs and filters, or the input-to-output curve of dynamics effects. It redraws as you change values.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Loudness and Phase Meters</td><td><b>Mixer &gt; Loudness and Phase Meters...</b> (also on the Timeline menu). A live window for the master output: momentary, short-term and integrated loudness against the -23 and -14 LUFS targets, peak and true peak, and a phase-correlation bar that turns red when the left and right channels cancel each other.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Foley Sampler</td><td><b>Timeline &gt; Foley Sampler...</b> or the <b>Foley</b> icon button beside the transport controls, which opens the Foley page under the Timeline so you can play it while the lanes stay in view (it scrolls when the panel is short). Sixteen pads that each hold a sound (WAV or MP3). Hit them with the mouse or the keyboard block 1234 / QWER / ASDF / ZXCV. Tick <b>Record hits while the Timeline plays</b>, perform the sounds against your picture or music, then choose <b>Add hits to Timeline</b> to lay each hit out as a clip at the moment you struck it. <b>Place pad at playhead</b> drops a single sound where the playhead is. The pads are remembered between sessions. Pad sounds are heard through the system output while you play them.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Ambisonics report</td><td>When the clip you measure with <b>Loudness &amp; Phase Report</b> has four or more channels (first-order Ambisonics, AmbiX order), the report adds the level of each channel and the direction the sound is coming from (angle, height and how much it is one source versus diffuse).</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Hall / Room / Plate Reverb, Lo-Fi</td><td>Reverb presets of different size and decay; Lo-Fi reduces the bit depth and sample rate for a gritty, vintage sound.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Stereo Fixer / Mid-Side Balance</td><td><b>Audio Effects &gt; Stereo &amp; Bass</b>. Stereo Fixer corrects a polarity-flipped channel and an uneven left/right level in one step. Mid/Side Balance trims the centre versus the sides (the default narrows the sides by 3 dB for a firmer centre).</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Headphone Crossfeed</td><td>Feeds a softened copy of each ear into the other, as speakers do, to ease the extreme separation of hard-panned mixes on headphones.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>LFE Filter / Sub Bass Synth</td><td>LFE Filter keeps only the low end (steep 24 dB per octave roll-off from 120 Hz) for a subwoofer channel. Sub Bass Synth adds a clean tone an octave below the bass it hears.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Frequency Shifter</td><td><b>Audio Effects &gt; Creative</b>. Moves every frequency up or down by the same number of hertz, giving inharmonic, metallic or swirling tones.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Auto-Wah / Rotary Speaker</td><td>Auto-Wah sweeps a resonant filter with how hard you play. Rotary Speaker imitates a spinning cabinet with slow pitch and level movement that differs between left and right.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Vocoder</td><td><b>Audio Effects &gt; Creative</b>. The classic robotic-voice effect: the shape of the voice is imposed on a buzzing synthetic tone, so speech and singing stay understandable but sound distorted and mechanical. It keeps the original loudness.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Robot Voice / Megaphone / Underwater</td><td>Character treatments for voices: Robot Voice (metallic buzz), Megaphone (narrow, driven) and Underwater (muffled with a slow wobble).</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Tape Stop</td><td>The end of the selection slows down as if the tape machine were switched off, winding to a halt over about 1.5 seconds.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Trance Gate</td><td>Chops the sound on and off in time (8 times a second) with soft edges to avoid clicks.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Vinyl Crackle / Comb Filter</td><td>Vinyl Crackle rolls off the top end and adds clicks and surface hiss. Comb Filter adds a very short repeat that creates a hollow, ringing, pitched tone.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Peak Normalize</td><td><b>Audio Effects &gt; Volume &amp; Fades</b>. Raises or lowers the selection so its loudest peak sits at -1 dBFS.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Split Stems</td><td>Select a clip, then choose <b>Audio Effects &gt; Tools &gt; Split Stems (Drums/Bass/Vocals/Other)</b>. Four new tracks are added, one per stem. This is classical signal processing (percussive/harmonic separation, a bass crossover and a centre-channel mask for vocals), not a neural network: the four stems always add back to the original, but separation is less clean than dedicated AI/SI tools on dense mixes.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Audio to MIDI</td><td>Select a clip, then <b>Audio Effects &gt; Tools &gt; Audio to MIDI...</b> and type <b>mono</b> (a melody or bass line) or <b>poly</b> (chords). The notes are written to a new Piano Roll clip using the project tempo, with velocity taken from the audio level.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Warp markers</td><td>Select an audio clip. <b>Warp: Add Marker at Playhead</b> pins a point; <b>Warp: Snap Marker to Nearest Beat</b> moves the marker nearest the playhead onto the beat grid; <b>Warp: Auto-Detect Beats to Grid</b> finds the drum hits or note starts and pins each one that is close to a beat. <b>Warp: Apply Markers</b> time-stretches the audio between markers (pitch preserved) into a new file and swaps it into the clip, keeping the clip's length. Undo restores the original audio; <b>Warp: Clear Markers</b> discards pending markers.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Metronome</td><td>Tick <b>Metronome</b> beside the tempo field on the Timeline tab. An accented click plays on every beat during playback and follows loops and seeks. It is a monitoring aid and is never included in exports.</td></tr>
            </table>
            <h3 id="timeline-video">Video: Import, Edit and Export</h3>
            <p>
                UDAW treats a video the way it treats sound: non-destructively. Your original file is never changed. The picture sits on its own <strong>VIDEO</strong> channel, its sound on a matching audio channel, and everything you do - cuts, effects, transitions, tempo, new music underneath - is saved as instructions in the project and only becomes a finished picture when you export. This section follows a clip from import to the final file, for two common jobs: <em>personal video</em> (holiday, family, social clips) and <em>production work</em> (a film, advert, trailer, game cut-scene or music video scored with the DAW's audio).
            </p>

            <h4>1. Bringing a video in</h4>
            <table class="udaw-table udaw-col-22">
                <tr><th>Way in</th><th>How</th></tr>
                <tr><td>Import menu</td><td>Use <b>Import to Timeline &gt; MP4 / M4A File...</b> (Timeline menu or the Timeline right-click menu) and pick the file. The same command brings in sound-only M4A/MP4 files.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Drag and drop</td><td>Drag a video file from the Browser panel onto the Timeline. It lands where you drop it.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Add a channel first</td><td>Use the Timeline's add-channel menu and choose <b>Video track</b> to create an empty video channel, then drop clips onto it. Extra video channels let you layer clips (see <em>Stacking</em> below).</td></tr>
            </table>
            <p><strong>Supported files:</strong> <code>.mp4</code>, <code>.m4v</code>, <code>.mov</code>, <code>.mkv</code> and <code>.webm</code>. Common video and sound formats play and import; if your system cannot decode a particular format, installing a codec pack such as K-Lite extends the list. A file the system cannot display still imports its sound, but its video pane stays blank.</p>
            <p><strong>What happens on import:</strong></p>
            <ul>
                <li>The <b>picture</b> is placed on a <b>VIDEO</b> channel.</li>
                <li>The <b>sound</b> is decoded and placed on its own audio channel at exactly the same start time, so picture and sound begin together. The silent padding that encoders add to the start of a file is removed, so the sound starts on the first real sample.</li>
                <li>A file with <b>several audio streams</b> (for example commentary, a second language or a surround mix) gets <b>one audio channel per stream</b>, so you can keep the one you want and mute or delete the rest.</li>
                <li>Large files are handled without loading them whole into memory, and the decoded sound is kept in the project's cache so the next Play is quick.</li>
                <li>A damaged stretch of sound becomes silence of the same length, so everything after it stays in time with the picture.</li>
            </ul>

            <h4>2. Looking at the picture</h4>
            <ul>
                <li><b>Video pane.</b> A pane beside the Timeline shows the picture in step with the sound while it plays and when you scrub. It appears whenever a video channel holds a clip. Use the <b>Video</b> button in the transport strip to hide or show it; hiding it does not silence the clip's sound.</li>
                <li><b>Hover preview.</b> Rest the mouse over any video clip and a small frame appears with its time, so you can find a moment without playing. The first hover on a spot takes a moment while the frame is prepared.</li>
                <li><b>Film strip.</b> Each video clip is drawn as a film strip labelled <b>VIDEO</b>, scrolling with the other channels, including while the Timeline follows the playhead.</li>
                <li>The picture is always silent; the sound you hear comes from the audio channels, so picture and mix can never fight each other.</li>
            </ul>

            <h4>3. Editing the clip</h4>
            <table class="udaw-table udaw-col-22">
                <tr><th>Edit</th><th>What it does to the final clip</th></tr>
                <tr><td>Move, trim, split, fade</td><td>Work like any other clip. Trimming the front or end of a clip shortens what is shown; splitting at the playhead makes two clips you can move, delete or put effects between.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Reverse</td><td>The clip plays backwards in the export. In the live pane a reversed clip is shown as a stepped preview of about ten pictures a second; the exported file is smooth.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Tempo / conform</td><td>When a clip is stretched to the project tempo, the picture is sped up or slowed down by the same amount so it stays in step with its sound. In the live pane this is also shown as a stepped preview; the export is smooth.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Mute</td><td>A muted video channel or clip is left out of the export entirely (it shows black, or reveals whatever is beneath it). Muting an audio channel removes only that sound.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Stacking</td><td>When video clips overlap on the Timeline, <b>the clip on the higher video channel is shown</b>. Use extra video channels for cutaways, titles and inserts over a base shot. Where no clip is under the playhead the picture is black.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Undo</td><td>Every video edit, including adding and removing effects, is an ordinary undoable edit and is saved with the project.</td></tr>
            </table>

            <h4>4. Effects and transitions - how they change the final clip</h4>
            <p>
                Effects are the heart of video editing in UDAW. <b>The picture in the pane while you edit is a preview; the exported file is rendered picture by picture, at full quality, with every effect and transition applied exactly where you put it.</b> An effect changes only the picture - the sound is never altered by a video effect, so your music and dialogue stay exactly as mixed.
            </p>
            <p><strong>Applying one:</strong> right-click a video channel (or open the <b>Edit</b> menu) and choose <b>Video Effects</b>. The submenu is only available on a video channel, just as <b>Audio Effects</b> is only available on audio channels.</p>
            <ul>
                <li><b>Mark a range</b> by dragging on the ruler, then choose an effect: the clip is split at both edges and the effect fills exactly that range. Use this to grade one scene, soften a few seconds, or add a flash on a beat.</li>
                <li><b>No range, effect:</b> the effect covers the whole video clip under the playhead.</li>
                <li><b>No range, transition:</b> put the playhead on the cut between two video clips (or anywhere on a clip, which splits it there) and choose a transition. It is laid one second wide across the cut: before the cut the incoming shot holds its first picture, after it the outgoing shot holds its last, so the change is smooth even if the clips were trimmed tight.</li>
                <li>Effects appear as bars along the bottom of the channel - <b>orange for transitions, purple for effects</b>, with a white line marking the cut of a transition. <b>Remove Video Effect at Playhead</b> (last item of the submenu) removes the effects under the playhead or touching the marked range.</li>
            </ul>
            <p><strong>What the groups are for:</strong></p>
            <table class="udaw-table udaw-col-22">
                <tr><th>Group</th><th>Typical use - and how it changes the result</th></tr>
                <tr><td>Transitions</td><td>Join two shots. <em>Dissolve</em> (cross, dip to black or white) is the gentle general-purpose change; <em>Iris</em>, <em>Wipe</em> and <em>Shape</em> are graphic and playful; <em>Motion</em> (push, slide, spin, whip pan, zoom, glitch) adds energy for music videos and montages.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Fade</td><td>Fade in at the start of a film, fade out at the end, flashes and blur-ins for dramatic beats.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Colour</td><td>Set the mood of a scene: black and white or sepia for nostalgia, teal and orange or bleach bypass for a cinematic look, white balance, exposure and levels to repair footage shot in poor light, thermal, night vision and false colour for effect.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Light</td><td>Vignette, glow, bloom, haze, light rays, lens flare, light leaks, strobe and flicker - polish and atmosphere, or flashes in time with the music.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Blur and Sharpen</td><td>Soften a distracting background, hide a detail, add a dreamy look, or rescue soft footage with sharpen, unsharp mask and detail recovery.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Stylize and Texture</td><td>Turn footage into a sketch, cartoon, oil paint, halftone or 3D anaglyph; add film grain, scanlines, old-film damage or a CRT look for retro and lo-fi styles.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Transform and Warp</td><td>Mirror, flip, kaleidoscope, tile, rotate, crop and zoom, keystone; bulge, fisheye, swirl, ripple, heat haze and underwater distortion.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Glitch</td><td>RGB split, chromatic aberration, digital glitch, TV static and interlace tear - ideal for electronic music and title stings.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Revival</td><td>Repair: noise reduction, despeckle, dead-pixel fixer, deband and dehaze for old or poorly lit footage.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Beauty</td><td>Soften skin and glamour glow for portraits and interviews.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Generate</td><td>Letterbox bars, borders, gradients, grids, colour bars, checkerboards, solid colours and clouds - backgrounds, a cinematic frame, or test cards.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Camera Motion</td><td>Shake, handheld, earthquake, bounce, slow zoom in and out, pans, Ken Burns, dutch angle and spin - add life to a still, steady shot.</td></tr>
            </table>
            <p><strong>Combining effects.</strong> Effects can sit on the same clip in different ranges: a colour grade over the whole clip, a vignette on the last few seconds, a transition at the cut. Effects are tied to a time on the Timeline and do not follow a clip when you move it - after moving clips, remove and re-apply the effect.</p>

            <h4>5. Playing with effects</h4>
            <p>
                When the video holds effects, pressing <b>Play</b> first <b>pre-renders</b> the picture so effects and transitions play smoothly. The status bar shows progress and an estimate of the time left while the sound is prepared at the same time; playback starts when both are ready and <b>Stop</b> cancels the wait. The pre-render is a lighter, silent preview copy (at most 960 pixels wide) - your files and exports are not changed. It is <b>kept and reused</b>: as long as the clips, their files and the effects are unchanged, the next Play starts at once. Any change renders a fresh copy. While stopped, scrubbing shows a quick preview of the effect in the video pane.
            </p>

            <h4>6. Adding and mixing the DAW audio</h4>
            <p>This is where UDAW differs from a video editor: the whole studio is behind your picture.</p>
            <ul>
                <li><b>Score it.</b> Drop music, loops, MIDI parts, tracker modules or AI-generated music on audio channels beneath the picture. Set the project tempo and use snap so cuts, effects and music hits land on the beat.</li>
                <li><b>Keep, replace or blend the original sound.</b> Keep the video's own audio channel for dialogue and ambience, mute it to replace the soundtrack entirely, or turn it down and layer music over it.</li>
                <li><b>Add Foley and effects.</b> Use the Foley Sampler to play sound effects against the picture in time, then add the hits to the Timeline as clips.</li>
                <li><b>Record a voice-over or live instruments</b> while the picture plays, then edit and mix the take like any other channel.</li>
                <li><b>Mix and master.</b> Use the Mixer's levels, pan, effects and automation, and the mixing and mastering assistants, on all of it, so dialogue, music and effects sit together.</li>
                <li><b>Use a range.</b> Mark a range on the ruler to export just one scene or a short social clip.</li>
            </ul>

            <h4>7. Exporting the finished clip</h4>
            <ol>
                <li>Choose <b>Timeline &gt; Export &gt; MP4...</b> or <b>MKV...</b> (also in the Timeline right-click menu). When the Timeline holds video these are the only formats offered, because other formats cannot carry a picture.</li>
                <li>Pick a name and location. The picture and your <b>complete mix</b> - every unmuted audio channel with its effects, automation and master level - are written together as one file.</li>
                <li>The status bar and Jobs show progress while the picture is rendered frame by frame with every effect and transition applied.</li>
            </ol>
            <table class="udaw-table udaw-col-22">
                <tr><th>What you get</th><th>Detail</th></tr>
                <tr><td>Picture size</td><td>The largest video clip used. <b>Settings &gt; General &gt; Export &gt; Video size (max width)</b> can reduce it; <i>Original size</i> keeps the source.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Frame rate</td><td>Follows the first video clip.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Quality</td><td><b>Video quality (bitrate)</b> and <b>Audio quality (bitrate)</b> in the same Settings group; <i>Automatic</i> chooses a bitrate to suit the picture size. The choices are saved with the project.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Layers and gaps</td><td>The higher video channel wins where clips overlap; gaps are black; muted channels and clips are left out.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Range</td><td>With a range selected, only that range is exported.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Author and tempo</td><td>The <b>Author</b> name and the project tempo are written into the file.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Sound</td><td>48 kHz, at the audio quality you chose.</td></tr>
            </table>

            <h4>8. Two worked examples</h4>
            <p><strong>Personal video - a holiday clip with music.</strong></p>
            <ol>
                <li>Import the clips (or drag them in from the Browser) and put them one after another on the video channel.</li>
                <li>Trim the dull parts and split where you want a change of scene.</li>
                <li>Put the playhead on each cut and add a <em>Cross Dissolve</em>; mark the sunset shot and add a warm <em>Colour</em> grade; add <em>Fade In</em> at the start and <em>Fade Out</em> at the end.</li>
                <li>Mute the video's own audio if it is only wind noise, add a music clip on an audio channel, and lower it under any speech you want to keep.</li>
                <li>Export as MP4 for your phone or social media.</li>
            </ol>
            <p><strong>Production work - a trailer or music video scored in UDAW.</strong></p>
            <ol>
                <li>Set the project tempo to the music first, so cuts and effects can snap to the beat.</li>
                <li>Lay the main picture on video channel 1; put titles, cutaways and inserts on channels above it.</li>
                <li>Cut on the beat; use <em>Push</em>, <em>Whip Pan</em> or <em>Glitch</em> transitions on the big hits and <em>Dip to Black</em> between scenes. Give the whole film one <em>Colour</em> look plus a light <em>Film Grain</em> or <em>Vignette</em> for a consistent feel.</li>
                <li>Score the sound: music, effects, Foley and voice-over on separate channels; automate levels so dialogue always sits on top; finish with the mastering assistant.</li>
                <li>Export a range to check a scene quickly, then the whole piece as MP4 or MKV at <i>Original size</i> and a high video quality.</li>
            </ol>

            <h4>9. Good to know</h4>
            <ul>
                <li>Your original video file is never modified; delete an export and export again at any time.</li>
                <li>The live preview is for editing and may lag on very large pictures; the export is always full quality.</li>
                <li>A video effect never changes the sound; use <b>Audio Effects</b> on the audio channels for that.</li>
                <li>Moving a clip does not move the effects placed on it - remove and re-apply them afterwards.</li>
                <li>Video editing and export are available on Windows. Only MP4 and MKV exports can carry a picture.</li>
                <li>You are responsible for having the right to use the footage and music you import and publish.</li>
            </ul>
            <h3 id="export-dither">Export quality: dither</h3>
            <p>Every Timeline export applies TPDF dither before the mix is rounded to 24-bit (or 16-bit for raw PCM), so quiet passages and fade tails stay smooth instead of gaining rounding distortion. There is nothing to configure.</p>
        </section>

        <!-- ============================================================ -->
        <section id="recording">
            <h2>6. Recording</h2>
            <div class="udaw-purpose">
                <p><b>Purpose</b> Capture a live vocal or instrument performance directly into the project.</p>
                <p><b>Why</b> Recording writes to a crash-safe container as it happens and offers punch/loop/takes tooling so a performance doesn't have to be perfect in one pass to end up sounding great.</p>
            </div>
            <p>
                The Recording tab captures audio to a recoverable, disk-streaming take container (so a
                take is safe even if UDAW is interrupted mid-recording), with support for:
            </p>
            <ul>
                <li><strong>Punch in/out</strong> and <strong>loop recording</strong> over a defined range - use punch-in to redo just one bad phrase without re-recording a whole take; use loop recording to keep trying a difficult section, capturing a new take on every pass.</li>
                <li><strong>Pre-roll</strong> and <strong>count-in</strong> before recording starts - pre-roll plays a bit of existing material first so a singer/player has context; count-in gives a metronome lead-in.</li>
                <li><strong>Latency compensation</strong>, so recorded audio lines up with the timeline despite device round-trip latency - you never need to manually nudge a take to fix timing caused by your audio interface.</li>
                <li><strong>Input monitoring</strong> while armed, so you can hear yourself (optionally through effects) while tracking.</li>
                <li><strong>Takes and comping</strong> &ndash; record multiple passes of the same section and pick/combine the best parts of each into one final performance.</li>
            </ul>
            <p>A <strong>Commit to Timeline</strong> command turns a finished take/comp into ordinary timeline clips, ready for the same trim/move/fade editing described in <a href="#timeline">Timeline &amp; Non-Destructive Editing</a>.</p>
            <p>Comping controls: swipe across any take to assign that time range to it (the range is split as needed, so you can build a comp from many short swipes); <b>Reset Comp</b> returns to take 1 for the whole lane; <b>Commit Comp to Timeline</b> adds one undoable clip per comp segment on the destination track.</p>
            <h4>Typical vocal-recording workflow</h4>
            <ol>
                <li>Arm the vocal track and enable input monitoring to check levels.</li>
                <li>Set a 1-2 bar pre-roll/count-in so the singer has a run-up.</li>
                <li>Record several takes of a difficult line using loop recording.</li>
                <li>Open the takes view and <b>swipe</b>: drag horizontally across a take row to make that stretch of the comp play from that take. The comp lane under the takes shows which take plays where.</li>
                <li>Commit the comp to the timeline and continue editing/mixing as normal.</li>
            </ol>
        </section>

        <!-- ============================================================ -->
        <section id="mixer">
            <h2>7. Mixer &amp; Routing</h2>
            <div class="udaw-purpose">
                <p><b>Purpose</b> Balance levels/pan, route signal through buses, apply effects (including hosted VST2 plugins), and hear the result live against Timeline playback.</p>
                <p><b>Why</b> Every sound source in a UDAW project - Timeline clips, Tracker channels, MIDI instrument output - can pass through the same mixer, so this is the one place you control how the whole song sits together.</p>
            </div>
            <p>
                The mixer models channel strips, sends, buses, aux channels, VCA groups and a master
                bus, with routing validated to reject cycles (so you cannot accidentally route a bus into
                itself and hang the audio engine). Its real-time summing processor runs each strip's
                insert chain (hosting Graph nodes and VST2 plugins, including external sidechain routing)
                plus pre/post-fader sends, pan/width and fader gain, with full metering.
            </p>
            <h4>Anatomy of a channel strip</h4>
            <table class="udaw-table udaw-col-22">
                <tr><th>Control</th><th>What it's for</th></tr>
                <tr><td>Fader</td><td>Overall level of this strip in the mix.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Pan / Width</td><td>Stereo placement and stereo image width.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Mute / Solo</td><td>Silence a strip, or isolate it to check it in isolation.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Inserts</td><td>A serial effect chain (EQ, compressor, a hosted VST2 plugin, ...) applied to just this strip - see <a href="#dsp">DSP &amp; Effects</a> and <a href="#mixer-plugins">VST2 Plugin Hosting</a>.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Sends</td><td>Split some/all of the signal off to a bus (e.g. a shared reverb) without removing it from the direct mix.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Bus / Aux / VCA</td><td>Group multiple strips so one fader (VCA) or one shared effect (Aux) controls all of them at once - e.g. a "Drums" bus for the whole kit, a "Reverb" aux fed by sends from several tracks.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Link track</td><td>On an audio-track strip, points the strip at a Timeline track (see <a href="#mixer-volume">Playback Volume</a> below) so this strip's own fader/pan/inserts/sends actually shape what that track sounds like during Timeline playback.</td></tr>
            </table>
            <p>
                The mixer's insert palette exposes the full internal DSP effect suite &ndash; see
                <a href="#dsp">DSP &amp; Effects</a> below &ndash; alongside hosted VST2 plugins. A common
                example: put a compressor insert on a bass strip with the kick drum wired in as an
                <strong>external sidechain</strong> source, so the bass ducks slightly every time the kick
                hits - a classic "pumping" dance-mix effect, built entirely from Graph nodes.
            </p>

            <h3 id="mixer-plugins">VST2 Plugin Hosting</h3>
            <div class="udaw-purpose">
                <p><b>Purpose</b> Use third-party VST2 effect plugins (.dll) on a mixer strip alongside UDAW's own built-in effects.</p>
                <p><b>Why</b> UDAW's own DSP suite covers most mixing needs without any external dependency, but a VST2 plugin the recipient may not have installed can still be loaded and processed for as long as that plugin's .dll is present on the machine running UDAW.</p>
            </div>
            <p>
                UDAW hosts VST2-ABI effect plugins directly - the classic 32-/64-bit Windows VST2 "shell
                DLL" format many DAWs and hardware-modelled effects still ship in. Only audio effect
                plugins are supported (audio in, audio out); VST instruments are not hosted. CLAP and
                VST3 effect plug-ins are hosted as well - see below.
            </p>
            <table class="udaw-table udaw-col-22">
                <tr><th>Action</th><th>How</th></tr>
                <tr><td>Load a plugin</td><td>Select a strip, click <b>Add VST...</b> on the Mixer tab and choose the .dll. UDAW probes the file first and refuses anything that isn't a usable VST2 audio effect, with the reason shown if it's rejected.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Drag-and-drop</td><td>Drag a .dll from the Browser tab straight onto a mixer strip to add it as an insert, the same way a sample is dragged onto the Timeline.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Edit parameters / open its GUI</td><td>Double-click the insert's dot on the strip, or select it and click <b>Edit VST...</b>, to open the plugin's own parameter list or native editor window.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Automate a parameter</td><td>A VST2 insert's parameters appear alongside built-in effect parameters in the strip's automation parameter list - see <a href="#mixer-automation">Automation Lanes</a>.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Save/reload plugin state</td><td>The plugin's full state (its native chunk, or a per-parameter dump if it doesn't support chunks) is saved with the project and restored automatically when the project reopens - as long as the same plugin .dll is available on that machine.</td></tr>
            </table>
            <p>
                Because a project's VST2 inserts reference an external .dll by path, a project that uses
                them is only fully portable to another machine that has the same plugin installed at a
                compatible path; a UDAW project built entirely from internal effects has no such
                dependency (see <a href="#dsp">DSP &amp; Effects</a>).
            </p>

            <h3 id="mixer-clap-vst3">CLAP and VST3 Plug-in Hosting</h3>
            <p>
                <b>Add VST...</b> also accepts <strong>.clap</strong> and <strong>.vst3</strong> files (plain files or VST3 bundle folders). UDAW loads the module, checks it exposes an audio effect, and inserts it like any other effect: bypass, reorder and remove work the same way, and the plug-in's saved state travels with the project. If a plug-in ever faults while processing, that insert falls back to dry pass-through instead of taking the audio engine down. Not yet available for CLAP and VST3: the plug-in's own editor window and parameter automation (VST2 keeps both), and instrument plug-ins.
            </p>

            <h3 id="mixer-plugin-scan">Scanning for Plug-ins</h3>
            <p>
                In the Timeline's right-hand panel, open the <b>Effects</b> tab and double-click <b>Scan for plug-ins...</b>. UDAW searches the standard VST2 and VST3 folders, shows progress on the status bar, and lists every usable effect plug-in under <b>Plug-ins</b> (results are remembered, so later scans only re-check what changed). Select a track, then double-click a plug-in in the list to add it to that track's mixer strip. Instrument plug-ins and files that are not usable effects are skipped.
            </p>

            <h3 id="scripts-python">Python Scripts</h3>
            <p>
                <b>Scripts &gt; Run Python script...</b> runs a Python 3 script that can drive UDAW (add tracks, edit clips, mix) through the same tools the AI/SI assistants use, so every change is undoable. Python 3 must be installed, and <b>expose UDAW</b> must be switched on in the AI/SI settings. Script output appears in the AI/SI log and on the status bar; choose Run again to stop a running script. <b>Open scripts folder</b> opens the folder holding the generated <code>udaw.py</code> helper: <code>from udaw import UDAW; u = UDAW(); u.tools(); u.call("tool_name", arg=value)</code>.
            </p>

            <h3 id="python-plugins">Python Plug-ins: Write Your Own Effects</h3>
            <p>
                A <b>Python plug-in</b> is a small text file that becomes a real effect on a Timeline lane, a bus or a Tracker channel. It can process the sound, show its own window with knobs, sliders, meters and pictures, be automated from the Mixer like any other insert, and its settings are saved <b>in your project</b>, which points at the script file and loads it again whenever the project opens. There is nothing to buy and nothing to install besides Python: if you can describe an effect in a few lines of code, you can have it on a guitar, a vocal or a whole mix.
            </p>
            <h4>Why use one?</h4>
            <ul>
                <li><b>Re-amp a clean guitar.</b> A DI recording sounds the same however you play it; a drive plug-in lets you try a crunch, a lead and a fuzz against the mix after the fact, and automate the drive for a solo. The bundled example does exactly this.</li>
                <li><b>A tool that does not exist.</b> A mid/side balance, a tape-style wobble, a telephone filter, a bit-crusher for one drum bus, a gate keyed to your own rules - if no stock effect does it, write it in ten minutes.</li>
                <li><b>Repeatable and shareable.</b> The whole effect is one file. Send it to a collaborator, keep it in a folder of favourites, or open an old project years later and hear exactly the same processing.</li>
                <li><b>Learn by doing.</b> Every control you add moves a number you can see in the code, which makes it a gentle way to understand filters, distortion, envelopes and delays.</li>
                <li><b>Project-specific fixes.</b> A de-hum for one noisy room, a high-pass tuned to one singer, a ducker with exactly the timing this song needs.</li>
                <li><b>Look inside your sound.</b> A custom-drawn meter, transfer curve or spectrum on the plug-in window shows what an effect is doing while it does it.</li>
            </ul>
            <h4>What you need</h4>
            <p>
                <b>Python 3.14 or newer</b> from python.org (tick <i>Add python.exe to PATH</i> in the installer). Plug-ins use only what Python ships with; if you also have <code>numpy</code> installed (<code>pip install numpy</code>) a plug-in may use it for heavy maths such as vocoding or reverb; <code>up.HAS_NUMPY</code> tells a script whether it is there. UDAW finds Python by itself and tells you plainly if it is missing or too old. A faulty script can never stop or crash UDAW: at worst the plug-in passes the sound through untouched and says why.
            </p>
            <h4>The Plugins menu</h4>
            <p>
                The <b>Plugins</b> menu lists every plug-in UDAW can use, found automatically: your Python plug-ins (every script in the plug-ins folder, including sub-folders) and the VST2 / VST3 effects found by the last scan. New files appear in the menu by themselves within a few seconds. Choose a <b>Python plug-in</b> and its <b>window opens</b>, ready to try, listen to and add to the mixer; choose a VST entry and it is put on the strip selected in the <b>Mixer</b> tab.
            </p>
            <table class="udaw-table udaw-col-22">
                <tr><th>Menu item</th><th>What it does</th></tr>
                <tr><td>Python Plug-ins</td><td>Your scripts; choosing one opens its window. A script in a sub-folder of the plug-ins folder is listed under a sub-menu named after that folder, so you can keep <i>Dynamics</i>, <i>Creative</i>, <i>Utilities</i> apart.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>VST 3 Plug-ins / VST 2 Plug-ins</td><td>Effects found by the plug-in scan. A long list is sorted into sub-menus by maker when there are several makers, otherwise into alphabetical groups such as <i>A - D</i>, <i>E - K</i>, so no menu ever grows taller than the screen.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Load VST 2 Plug-in... / Load VST 3 Plug-in...</td><td>Pick a plug-in file yourself (for one that lives outside the standard folders) and put it on the selected strip.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Open Python Editor...</td><td>The plug-in editor described below.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>New Python Plug-in...</td><td>Opens the editor with a small working template.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Open Example: Guitar Overdrive / Distortion</td><td>Opens the bundled example in the editor.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Rescan Plug-ins</td><td>Searches the VST folders again (with progress on the status bar) and refreshes the menu.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Open Plug-ins Folder</td><td>Opens the folder your Python plug-ins live in (choose it under <b>Settings &gt; Working Directories &gt; Python plug-ins folder</b>; the choice is remembered). UDAW keeps its helper module and the bundled plug-ins there. Projects point at the script files, so if you change the folder, move your plug-ins with it.</td></tr>
            </table>
            <h4>The editor</h4>
            <p>
                <b>Plugins &gt; Open Python Editor...</b> opens the plug-in editor: your script on top, <b>Problems</b> and <b>Output</b> below. The toolbar:
            </p>
            <table class="udaw-table udaw-col-22">
                <tr><th>Button</th><th>What it does</th></tr>
                <tr><td>New / Open / Save / Save As</td><td>Start from the template, open a script, save it (<b>Ctrl+N</b>, <b>Ctrl+O</b>, <b>Ctrl+S</b>, <b>Ctrl+Shift+S</b>). Closing with unsaved changes asks first.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Validate (F6)</td><td>Checks the script without needing any audio: the file is read for syntax errors, loaded, its parameters and window are checked (duplicate names, controls bound to a parameter that does not exist, controls hanging off the window, <b>controls that overlap - the warning names both controls and their lines</b>, a missing <code>author</code>), and a test signal is run through <code>process()</code> to catch crashes, silence, non-finite numbers and code too slow for real time. Results are listed under <b>Problems</b> as errors, warnings and notes; click a line to jump to it.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Run (F5)</td><td>Validates, then starts the plug-in, opens its window and starts <b>Listen</b> so you hear it straight away (see below).</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Stop</td><td>Stops the running plug-in.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Add to lane + Add to Mixer</td><td>Choose a Timeline lane or bus, or a Tracker channel, then press <b>Add to Mixer</b>: the plug-in goes on that strip with <b>the settings currently shown in its window</b>.</td></tr>
            </table>
            <p>
                Anything the script prints appears under <b>Output</b>, together with load errors - the first place to look when something does not behave.
            </p>
            <h4>Hear it before you add it</h4>
            <p>
                Every plug-in window (from the Plugins menu or the editor's Run) has a <b>Hear it</b> row. <b>Listen</b> plays program material through the plug-in on your speakers, so you judge the sound before it goes anywhere near a lane:
            </p>
            <table class="udaw-table udaw-col-22">
                <tr><th>Control</th><th>What it does</th></tr>
                <tr><td>Listen</td><td>Starts and stops the audition (it starts by itself when the window opens from the menu or from Run). The audition takes the speakers, so it stops Timeline playback first, and Timeline playback in turn ends the audition.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Source</td><td><b>Built-in guitar phrase</b>: a clean plucked-string riff that needs no files, the fallback that is always available. <b>Highlighted Timeline range</b>: the area you selected on the Timeline (up to 30 seconds, the lanes summed without their effects), played in a loop through the plug-in. With nothing highlighted UDAW says so and uses the guitar phrase.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Bypass</td><td>Plays the same material without the plug-in, to compare before and after while you turn the knobs.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Add to Mixer</td><td>When you like it, puts the plug-in with the settings you chose on the strip selected in the Mixer tab. The insert points at the script file, so the file must be saved (the editor offers to save it first).</td></tr>
            </table>
            <p>
                Also in the window: <b>About</b> (name, version and <b>the author</b>), <b>Log</b>, <b>Defaults</b> and <b>Close</b>. The controls <b>scale with the window</b> when you resize it.
            </p>
            <h4>On the mixer</h4>
            <p>
                A Python plug-in is an ordinary insert. It sits in the strip's insert chain, can be bypassed, reordered and removed, and takes part in Undo. <b>Double-click</b> it to open its window; the settings are kept in the project when you press <b>Save settings</b> or close the window. While the project plays, the window is connected to the plug-in that is playing, so what you move is what you hear and meters show the real signal. Its parameters appear in the Mixer's <b>automation</b> list (named after the plug-in's parameters) and can be drawn as automation lanes or driven by modulators. Exports always wait for the plug-in, so a bounce is exact even if the script is slow. The project <b>remembers every Python plug-in it uses</b> (the script's location and its settings, on Timeline lanes and Tracker channels alike). When the project opens, each plug-in is located, loaded and put back on its Mixer strip, together with any plug-in windows you left open.
            </p>
            <div class="udaw-note">
                The project does <b>not</b> contain a copy of the script: the insert <b>points at the script file</b> and loads it from there each time the project opens or plays. Keep the file where it is (or add the plug-in again from its new place). Edit the file and the insert uses the new version the next time it starts. If the file is missing when the project opens, UDAW lists the missing scripts and the insert passes the sound through unchanged until the file is back. Because the project holds only the path, give a plug-in that a collaborator needs a shared location (or send the <code>.py</code> file with the project).
            </div>
            <h4>Writing a plug-in</h4>
            <p>
                A plug-in is a class that derives from <code>udaw_plugin.Plugin</code>. Everything is optional except what you want it to do:
            </p>
            <div class="udaw-code">import udaw_plugin as up

class Plugin(up.Plugin):
    name = "Tremolo"
    author = "Your Name"            # shown in the About box
    description = "Wobbles the level, like an old amplifier."

    def setup(self):                      # 1. declare the settings
        self.add_param("rate",  "Rate",  min=0.5, max=12, default=4, unit="Hz", log=True)
        self.add_param("depth", "Depth", min=0, max=100, default=60, unit="%", decimals=0)

    def ui(self, ui):                     # 2. describe the window
        ui.window(220, 150, "Tremolo")
        ui.knob("k_rate",  20, 20, param="rate")
        ui.knob("k_depth", 120, 20, param="depth")

    def prepare(self, sample_rate, channels, max_frames):
        self.lfo = up.LFO(sample_rate, 4.0)   # 3. get ready (filters, delay lines ...)

    def process(self, block):             # 4. the audio
        self.lfo.freq = self.p.rate
        depth = self.p.depth * 0.01
        for i in range(block.frames):
            g = 1.0 - depth * (0.5 + 0.5 * self.lfo.tick())
            for ch in range(block.channels):
                block.out[ch][i] = block.inp[ch][i] * g</div>
            <h4>Parameters</h4>
            <p>
                Parameters are the plug-in's settings. They are saved in the project, can be automated, and are read in <code>process()</code> as <code>self.p.name</code>.
            </p>
            <table class="udaw-table udaw-col-22">
                <tr><th>Call</th><th>Meaning</th></tr>
                <tr><td><code>add_param(id, name, min, max, default, unit="", log=False, step=0, decimals=2)</code></td><td>A number. <code>log=True</code> gives a logarithmic scale (frequencies, times; needs <code>min &gt; 0</code>). <code>step</code> snaps to multiples.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>add_param(..., kind="int")</code></td><td>A whole number.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>add_bool(id, name, default=False)</code></td><td>An on/off switch; <code>self.p.id</code> is <code>True</code> or <code>False</code>.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>add_choice(id, name, ["A", "B", ...], default=0)</code></td><td>One of a list; <code>self.p.id</code> is the chosen index.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>self.set_param(id, value)</code></td><td>Changes a parameter from inside the plug-in (the control follows).</td></tr>
            </table>
            <h4>Audio</h4>
            <p>
                <code>process(self, block)</code> is called for each buffer. <code>block.inp[ch]</code> is what comes in, <code>block.out[ch]</code> is what you write (it starts as a copy of the input, so a channel you do not touch passes through). <code>block.frames</code>, <code>block.channels</code>, <code>block.sample_rate</code> and <code>block.peak()</code> are available. Because Python is slower than compiled code, keep loops tight: put values you use per sample into local variables, avoid creating objects inside the loop, and prefer simple formulas such as <code>up.soft_clip(x)</code> over many function calls. The Validate button reports how much of the real-time budget your <code>process()</code> uses.
            </p>
            <table class="udaw-table udaw-col-22">
                <tr><th>Helper</th><th>What it is</th></tr>
                <tr><td><code>up.db_to_lin(db)</code>, <code>up.lin_to_db(x)</code>, <code>up.clamp</code>, <code>up.lerp</code>, <code>up.midi_to_hz</code>, <code>up.rgb</code></td><td>Small conversions.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>up.Biquad(kind, sr, freq, q, gain_db)</code></td><td>Filters: lowpass, highpass, bandpass, notch, peak, lowshelf, highshelf, allpass. <code>f.tick(x)</code> filters one sample; <code>f.set(...)</code> retunes it.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>up.OnePole</code>, <code>up.DCBlocker</code></td><td>Gentle one-pole filter; DC-offset remover (asymmetric distortion makes offset).</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>up.Smoother</code></td><td>Glides a value to its target to avoid clicks when a knob moves.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>up.EnvelopeFollower</code></td><td>Follows the level with separate attack and release - the heart of gates and compressors.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>up.DelayLine</code>, <code>up.LFO</code></td><td>Interpolating delay (echo, chorus, flange); sine / triangle / saw / square oscillator.</td></tr>
            </table>
            <h4>Timing and heavy plug-ins</h4>
            <p>
                UDAW sends each buffer to the plug-in and waits briefly for the answer. If the plug-in is ever late, that buffer plays <i>dry</i> instead of stalling the music; the window's status line shows the percentage of late buffers. If an effect is too heavy for real time, set <code>buffered = True</code> on the class: results then arrive one buffer late (a tiny, constant delay) and the effect always keeps up. Plug-ins that want numpy set <code>use_numpy = True</code>.
            </p>
            <h4>Building the window</h4>
            <p>
                The <code>ui(self, ui)</code> method places controls with <code>ui.&lt;type&gt;(id, ..., x, y, w, h)</code>, in pixels. Leave <code>ui()</code> out and UDAW builds a plain window with one control per parameter. Controls sit directly on the window with transparent backgrounds, and they stretch with the window when it is resized, so design at a comfortable size. A control placed wholly on a group or panel automatically becomes part of it, so it takes the frame's background and moves with it (no filled rectangles around knobs). Place controls so they do not overlap: Validate warns about every overlapping pair by name (a control lying wholly on a group or panel is fine). Every type of UDAW's own control set can be created:
            </p>
            <table class="udaw-table udaw-col-22">
                <tr><th>Call</th><th>Control</th></tr>
                <tr><td><code>ui.label</code>, <code>ui.status</code></td><td>Text; a status line.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>ui.button</code> (<code>toggle=True</code> for an on/off button), <code>ui.check</code></td><td>Buttons and check boxes. Bound to an on/off parameter they follow it; otherwise the plug-in gets a <code>click</code> / <code>change</code> event.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>ui.knob</code>, <code>ui.slider</code> (<code>vertical=True</code>), <code>ui.eq_slider</code></td><td>Value controls. Bound with <code>param=</code> they take range, default, logarithmic scale, resolution and unit from the parameter.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>ui.meter</code> / <code>ui.progress</code></td><td>A level bar. Drive it from <code>process()</code> with <code>self.ui_set("id", value=level)</code>.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>ui.combo</code>, <code>ui.list</code>, <code>ui.list_view</code>, <code>ui.tree</code></td><td>Drop-down, list, table with columns, expandable tree.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>ui.edit</code>, <code>ui.memo</code></td><td>A text box; a multi-line text area (read-only or editable).</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>ui.group</code>, <code>ui.panel</code>, <code>ui.recess</code>, <code>ui.collapse</code>, <code>ui.scroll_box</code>, <code>ui.bevel</code>, <code>ui.shine</code>, <code>ui.splitter</code></td><td>Containers and decoration. Give a control <code>parent="id"</code> to place it inside one (its position is then relative to the container).</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>ui.tabs</code>, <code>ui.tab_strip</code></td><td>Pages: <code>ui.tabs("t", ["Tone", "About"], ...)</code> then <code>parent="t#0"</code> / <code>"t#1"</code> to fill a page.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>ui.scroll_bar</code></td><td>A scroll bar with change events.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>ui.canvas</code></td><td>A surface you draw yourself (below).</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>ui.add(type, id, ...)</code></td><td>Any of the above by name.</td></tr>
            </table>
            <p>
                <b>Every property is reachable.</b> Extra named arguments set the control's property of the same name, written either way: <code>font_size=14</code>, <code>bold=True</code>, <code>font_color="#8a93a3"</code>, <code>color="#16181d"</code>, <code>hint="Shown on hover"</code>, <code>read_only=False</code>, <code>Decimals=1</code>, <code>visible=False</code>. Colours are <code>"#rrggbb"</code> or <code>(r, g, b)</code>. The script can change controls while it runs: <code>self.ui_set("id", caption="...", value=0.5, visible=True)</code>, and react to controls that have no parameter by defining <code>on_&lt;id&gt;(self, event, value)</code> or <code>on_event(self, id, event, value)</code>.
            </p>
            <h4>Drawing your own surface</h4>
            <p>
                A <code>ui.canvas</code> is redrawn about 30 times a second (or on <code>self.repaint(id)</code>) by calling <code>paint_&lt;id&gt;(self, canvas)</code> or <code>paint(self, id, canvas)</code>. Draw with:
            </p>
            <table class="udaw-table udaw-col-22">
                <tr><th>Draw command</th><th>Result</th></tr>
                <tr><td><code>fill_rect</code>, <code>rect</code>, <code>round_rect</code>, <code>ellipse</code>, <code>circle</code></td><td>Filled and outlined shapes.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>line</code>, <code>polyline</code>, <code>polygon</code>, <code>arc</code></td><td>Lines, paths and filled polygons.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>gradient(x, y, w, h, top, bottom, horizontal)</code></td><td>A colour blend.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>plot(values, x, y, w, h, lo, hi, color)</code>, <code>grid(...)</code></td><td>A curve scaled into a box (waveforms, transfer curves, spectra); a ruled grid.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>text(x, y, string, color, size, bold, align, width)</code></td><td>Text, left / centre / right aligned.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>push_clip</code> / <code>pop_clip</code>, <code>alpha</code></td><td>Restrict drawing to a box; set transparency.</td></tr>
            </table>
            <p>
                With <code>mouse=True</code> (the default) the canvas also reports the mouse to <code>on_mouse(self, id, kind, x, y, button)</code> - kinds <i>down, move, up, wheel, double</i> - so you can build draggable curves, XY pads or step sequencers.
            </p>
            <h4>Saving extra data</h4>
            <p>
                Parameter values are saved automatically. For anything else (a preset number, a list of steps), return plain data from <code>get_state()</code> and read it back in <code>set_state(state)</code>; it is saved with the project too. Other hooks: <code>reset()</code> (clear filter memory when playback restarts), <code>on_param(id, value)</code> (a setting changed), <code>on_timer()</code> (about 20 times a second while the window is open).
            </p>
            <h4>The bundled example: Guitar Overdrive &amp; Distortion</h4>
            <p>
                <b>Plugins &gt; Open Example</b> opens a complete plug-in made for guitar recordings, and it exercises every part of the system: parameters of all kinds, per-sample audio, a tabbed window with knobs, a combo box, check boxes, level meters and a memo, a custom-drawn transfer curve with a live waveform, presets that move many settings at once, and saved extra state. Signal flow: noise gate, tight (high-pass before the clipper), drive, one of six voicings through one to three cascaded clipping stages, tone low-pass, bass / mid / presence, a cabinet filter, DC block, dry/wet mix, output level and a soft output limiter.
            </p>
            <table class="udaw-table udaw-col-22">
                <tr><th>Voicing</th><th>Character</th></tr>
                <tr><td>Clean Boost</td><td>Soft, nearly transparent push - thickens a clean part and drives the next stage.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Overdrive</td><td>A pushed tube-like stage with a touch of asymmetry: warm, dynamic, responds to picking.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Crunch</td><td>Different knees for the two halves of the wave: rhythm-guitar grit.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Distortion</td><td>Hard knee, tight and aggressive - rock and metal.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Fuzz</td><td>Heavy, uneven and raw.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Metal</td><td>An almost square-wave clipper: the most extreme voicing. At full Drive there is about 100 dB of gain into the clipper.</td></tr>
            </table>
            <table class="udaw-table udaw-col-22">
                <tr><th>Control</th><th>Why you would touch it</th></tr>
                <tr><td>Gate</td><td>High-gain sounds amplify hum and hiss between notes; raise it until the gaps are silent but note tails still ring.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Tight</td><td>Cuts low frequencies <i>before</i> the clipper so palm-muted low strings stay clear instead of turning to mud.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Drive</td><td>How hard the clipper is pushed. Automate it for a solo that gets dirtier as it climbs.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Stages</td><td>One to three clippers in series: every extra stage clips what the last one already clipped, for a denser, more compressed, far more distorted sound.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Tone / Bass / Mid / Presence</td><td>Shape the result after the distortion, where it matters.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Cabinet</td><td>Rolls off the fizz above about 5 kHz and the rumble below 80 Hz, as a speaker would.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>2x anti-alias</td><td>Less harsh digital fizz on heavy settings, for more CPU.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Mix</td><td>Blend with the dry guitar: parallel drive keeps the pick attack of a clean take under a heavy tone.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Output limiter</td><td>A soft ceiling so even the heaviest setting can never go over 0 dBFS.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Preset</td><td>Clean Boost, Blues Overdrive, Classic Crunch, Hard Rock, Metal Wall, Fuzz Face - each moves many controls at once.</td></tr>
            </table>
            <h4>The bundled plug-ins</h4>
            <p>
                Three finished plug-ins ship with UDAW, all written by <b>Daniel J. Hobson, Australia</b>. They appear in the Plugins menu by themselves, open in the editor to be read and changed, and between them show most of what the system can do. The Vocoder and Reverberator &amp; Chorus use numpy (<code>pip install numpy</code>) so they stay light on the processor; without it they pass the sound through and say so in their Log.
            </p>
            <table class="udaw-table udaw-col-22">
                <tr><th>Plug-in</th><th>What it is for</th></tr>
                <tr><td>Guitar Overdrive &amp; Distortion</td><td>Re-amp a clean guitar: six voicings from a clean boost to a metal wall, cascaded stages, gate, tone stack, cabinet filter.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Vocoder</td><td>Make a voice talk through a built-in synthesiser. Choose a sawtooth, pulse, detuned choir or noise carrier, a note and a chord; 8 to 32 bands, attack and release, formant shift, brightness and a sibilance control so consonants stay clear. Presets include Classic Robot, Choir Pad, Deep Giant and Whisper. It needs no side-chain: put it straight on the vocal lane. Latency is about 21 ms.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Reverberator &amp; Chorus</td><td>A stereo chorus (one to four modulated voices, rate, depth, delay, mix) feeding an algorithmic reverb (eight combs and four all-pass filters with damping, pre-delay and stereo width). Room, Hall, Plate and Cathedral types set sensible starting points. Use it to thicken a thin part and place it in a room in one step.</td></tr>
            </table>
            <p>
                <b>Free for every user.</b> These three plug-ins are included for all UDAW users as working demonstrations of how to program your own plug-ins. Use them, open them in the editor, study them and adapt them to learn the system - at no extra cost. You may share your own plug-ins, and changed versions of these, with other users free of charge, but they may not be sold or distributed for commercial gain. <b>Want to contribute?</b> If you would like to contribute plug-ins, ideas or other work to UDAW, please contact <b>udaw2026@gmail.com</b>. Please write properly: say who you are, give a verifiable name and contact details, and explain clearly what you would like to contribute. Spam, unsolicited offers and anything irrelevant to UDAW will be ignored without reply.
            </p>
            <h4>More ideas for your own plug-ins</h4>
            <table class="udaw-table udaw-col-22">
                <tr><th>Plug-in</th><th>Why it is worth writing</th></tr>
                <tr><td>Tape wobble / lo-fi</td><td>A slow <code>LFO</code> modulating a <code>DelayLine</code> plus a high-cut and a little noise gives a worn-tape character no stock effect matches exactly.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Mid / side balance</td><td>Two knobs and ten lines turn a stereo recording's centre or sides up and down - handy for vocal focus or widening.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Smart gate for a drum bus</td><td>Combine an <code>EnvelopeFollower</code> with your own hold and release rules, and show the gate's state with a meter.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Level and spectrum display</td><td>Use a <code>canvas</code> to draw what you need on every lane: peak history, stereo balance, a rough spectrum.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Ducker</td><td>Lower a pad when a note is detected on the same channel, with exactly the shape you want.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Step-gate / trance gate</td><td>A canvas of clickable steps (<code>on_mouse</code>) that chops the signal in time.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Teaching tool</td><td>A filter or clipper with the maths in plain sight - change a line, hear the difference.</td></tr>
            </table>
            <h4>If something goes wrong</h4>
            <table class="udaw-table udaw-col-22">
                <tr><th>What you see</th><th>What to do</th></tr>
                <tr><td>"Python 3.14 or newer was not found"</td><td>Install the current Python from python.org with <i>Add python.exe to PATH</i> ticked, then try again.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>The insert passes sound through unchanged</td><td>The script did not load or stopped. Open the editor, press <b>Validate</b>, and read <b>Problems</b> and <b>Output</b>.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Status line says some blocks are "late"</td><td>The script is too heavy for real time. Simplify the per-sample loop, set <code>buffered = True</code>, or use numpy.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>A control does not appear</td><td>Validate lists controls placed outside the window or hung off a parent that does not exist.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Validate says two controls overlap</td><td>The message names both controls and the script lines that created them; move or resize one so they do not cover each other.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Listen cannot start</td><td>Another sound (Timeline playback) is using the speakers, or the plug-in instance runs at a different sample rate than the output device - close the window and open it again.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>A change to the file does not affect the song</td><td>The project holds its own copy: add the plug-in to the lane again.</td></tr>
            </table>

            <h3 id="tracker-to-timeline">Tracker Modules on the Timeline</h3>
            <p>
                After importing a module on the Tracker tab, UDAW offers to add it to the Timeline. <b>Yes</b> places it as a clip drawn as notation (its notes, time across and pitch up); the tracker plays it when the Timeline reaches the clip, following the Timeline tempo. <b>No</b> converts it once to an ordinary audio clip. <b>Cancel</b> keeps it in the Tracker only. Playback is stopped automatically before a module is replaced.
            </p>

            <h3 id="pianoroll-zoom">Piano Roll View</h3>
            <p>
                Use the mouse wheel over the piano roll to zoom the time axis in and out around the pointer; hold <b>Shift</b> and use the wheel to scroll along it. The <b>Track</b> list picks which track of a MIDI file you are editing (the other tracks are shown dimmed behind it), and the note range widens automatically so every note is visible. Importing a MIDI file also places it on the Timeline.
            </p>

            <h3 id="mixer-modulation">Modulators</h3>
            <div class="udaw-purpose">
                <p><b>Purpose</b> Make a fader, pan or plug-in parameter move by itself - a slow LFO on a pad's pan, a random wobble, a step pattern, or a level that follows another channel.</p>
                <p><b>Why</b> Modulation is evaluated inside the mixer engine on every audio block, so what you hear live is exactly what exports, and it is saved with the project.</p>
            </div>
            <table class="udaw-table udaw-col-22">
                <tr><th>Source</th><th>Menu</th></tr>
                <tr><td>LFO</td><td><b>Mixer &gt; Modulation &gt; Add LFO modulator...</b> - type a shape (sine, triangle, saw, square, sh) and a rate in Hz.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Smooth random</td><td>A new random target every so often, glided between.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Steps</td><td>A rate plus up to 16 values from -1 to 1 that repeat.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Envelope follower</td><td>Follows the level of the selected strip, so one channel can move another.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Macro</td><td>A fixed knob value from -1 to 1.</td></tr>
            </table>
            <p>Then select the strip to affect and choose <b>Route modulator to selected strip...</b>, typing <code>modulator# target amount</code>: <code>1 fader 3</code> swings the fader &plusmn;3 dB with modulator 1, <code>2 pan 0.5</code> swings pan, and <code>1 i1:p4 0.3 0.5</code> swings parameter 4 of insert 1 (a VST2 plug-in) by &plusmn;0.3 around 0.5. <b>List modulators and routes</b> shows everything; <b>Remove all modulators</b> clears it.</p>

            <h3 id="mixer-layout">The Mixer controls</h3>
            <p>The controls at the top of the Mixer tab are arranged in four rows: strip selection, routing and link; level, pan, mute, solo and the automation parameter and mode; the insert palette and plug-in buttons; and the strip-width and meter-hold settings, with the automation lane beneath.</p>

            <h3 id="mixer-automation">Automation Lanes</h3>
            <div class="udaw-purpose">
                <p><b>Purpose</b> Record or draw a mixer parameter's changes over time instead of setting it once.</p>
                <p><b>Why</b> A mix rarely stays static - a fader riding down for a chorus, a filter opening up over a build - and automation captures that as part of the project instead of a manual move you have to repeat every time you play the song.</p>
            </div>
            <p>
                Each strip has an automation mode (Off / Read / Touch / Latch / Write) selected next to its
                controls. <b>Write</b> records every move of the selected parameter (including a hosted
                VST2 plugin's own parameters) into its automation lane while the transport runs; <b>Touch</b>
                and <b>Latch</b> only record while a control is actively being moved (Touch reverts once
                released, Latch keeps the last written value); <b>Read</b> plays existing automation back
                without recording; <b>Off</b> ignores the lane entirely. The automation view below the
                strip controls draws the selected parameter's curve directly - click to add a point, drag
                to reshape it.
            </p>

            <h3 id="mixer-editing">Strip Reordering &amp; Editing</h3>
            <div class="udaw-purpose">
                <p><b>Purpose</b> Keep the mixer's strip order matching how you think about the mix (e.g. drums together, busses on the right).</p>
            </div>
            <p>
                Each strip is a vertical channel: name and colour (a strip takes the colour of the
                Timeline track it is linked to), <b>In</b> and <b>Out</b> choosers, its effect list with
                <b>+ FX</b> (its menu also has a <b>Python Plug-ins</b> sub-menu listing your scripts &ndash; pick one to put it on that strip) and <b>+ Send</b> beneath it, a pan slider, the fader level and peak readouts,
                the fader with its meter, and <b>M</b> (mute), <b>S</b> (solo) and <b>R</b> (arm for
                recording) at the bottom. Click an effect to edit it, right-click it to move or remove it,
                and click a send to remove it. Double-click the fader or the pan slider to reset it.
                Drag a strip by its name area onto another strip to reorder it - purely cosmetic, it never
                changes routing. The toolbar above the strips has <b>Add</b> (track, bus, aux or VCA; the
                right-click menu has the rest, including <b>Remove strip</b> - the master strip cannot be
                removed), <b>Strip tools</b> (the detailed strip, automation and meter settings),
                Undo / Redo, strip width and scroll buttons (the mouse wheel also scrolls the strips). Every
                structural edit - adding/removing a strip, an insert, a send, or changing routing/the
                Timeline track link - is undoable from the Mixer tab's own Undo/Redo, sharing the
                project's single undo stack with every other tab.
            </p>

            <h3 id="mixer-metering">Metering</h3>
            <div class="udaw-purpose">
                <p><b>Purpose</b> See level, not just hear it - catch clipping and check loudness against a target.</p>
            </div>
            <p>
                Every strip shows peak level (dBFS, per channel) and momentary/integrated loudness (LUFS,
                per BS.1770). Meter hold time and decay rate are adjustable so a peak stays visible long
                enough to read before falling back to the live level.
            </p>

            <h3 id="mixer-volume">Playback Volume: Strip vs. Timeline Master</h3>
            <div class="udaw-purpose">
                <p><b>Purpose</b> Understand which volume control is actually shaping what you hear at any given moment.</p>
                <p><b>Why</b> UDAW has two independent volume stages that are easy to confuse: the Timeline tab's own master volume, and the Mixer's per-strip faders (and master strip).</p>
            </div>
            <p>
                A Timeline track plays two different ways depending on whether it's linked to a mixer
                strip (see <b>Link track</b> above). An <b>unlinked</b> track is summed directly by the
                Timeline player using only its own track-level gain/pan/mute/solo - the Mixer tab has no
                effect on it. A <b>linked</b> track (an audio-track strip whose Link track points at it)
                is instead rendered dry and handed to that strip, so from that point on the strip's own
                trim, fader, pan, inserts (including VST2 plugins), sends and bus/VCA routing are what
                actually reach the output - the track's own gain/pan controls no longer apply, exactly as
                on a hardware mixer once a channel is patched in. Either way, the Timeline tab's own
                <b>master volume</b> slider is a final trim applied on top of everything (linked and
                unlinked tracks alike) right before the sound card - it is not the same control as the
                Mixer's master strip fader, and riding one does not move the other. Editing a clip, a
                track, or a linked strip's settings while the transport is running takes effect on the
                very next audio block, matching a normal DAW.
            </p>
        </section>

        <!-- ============================================================ -->
        <section id="dsp">
            <h2>8. DSP &amp; Effects</h2>
            <div class="udaw-purpose">
                <p><b>Purpose</b> Shape the tone, dynamics and space of a sound.</p>
                <p><b>Why</b> Every effect below is implemented internally, so a UDAW project built only from these never depends on a plugin the recipient doesn't have installed - what you hear while mixing is exactly what plays back for anyone else who opens the project. A project that also uses a hosted <a href="#mixer-plugins">VST2 plugin</a> insert does depend on that plugin being present on the machine that opens it.</p>
            </div>
            <p>Every effect in this suite is implemented internally &ndash; no third-party plugin format or DSP library is used to build it. The suite covers:</p>
            <table class="udaw-table udaw-col-22">
                <tr><th>Category</th><th>Effects</th><th>Typical use</th></tr>
                <tr><td>Dynamics</td><td>Compressor, expander, gate, de-esser, and a true lookahead brickwall limiter (a dedicated lookahead peak-detection design, separate from the compressor-style limiter preset)</td><td>Even out a vocal's level (compressor), remove hiss/bleed between phrases (gate/expander), tame harsh "s" sounds on vocals (de-esser), catch peaks with zero overshoot on a master bus (lookahead limiter).</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Modulation</td><td>Chorus, flanger, phaser, tremolo, auto-pan</td><td>Thicken a thin guitar/synth (chorus), add sweeping movement (flanger/phaser), rhythmic volume/stereo motion (tremolo/auto-pan). Chorus is a rich three-voice ensemble with a smooth, warm top end; the flanger has a softened, musical feedback; the phaser sweeps evenly across the spectrum and moves independently left and right for a wide stereo image.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Distortion</td><td>Saturation, tube saturation, soft and hard clipping, wavefolding, bitcrusher</td><td>Warm up a bass or drum bus (saturation), aggressive guitar/synth tone (clipping/wavefolding), vintage warmth on vocals and mixes (tube saturation), gentle peak rounding (soft clip), lo-fi/retro texture (bitcrusher). Saturation and wavefolding are processed at double resolution so heavy drive stays clean and musical instead of harsh and gritty; hard clip and bitcrusher deliberately keep their raw, edgy character.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Space</td><td>Freeverb-style reverb; tempo-syncable ping-pong/stereo delay/echo</td><td>Give a dry recording a sense of room/hall (reverb); bounce a rhythmic echo between left/right locked to the song's BPM (ping-pong delay).</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Tone</td><td>Graphic EQ; a fully parametric multi-band EQ (peaking/shelf/cut bands with adjustable frequency, gain and Q per band)</td><td>Cut muddy low-mids, add air on top, carve space for competing instruments; use the parametric EQ when you need a precise, surgical cut/boost rather than the graphic EQ's fixed bands.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Character &amp; repair</td><td>Stereo widener/imager, transient shaper, exciter/enhancer, pitch shifter</td><td>Make a stereo mix feel wider without phase issues (widener), punch up or soften drum attacks independent of overall level (transient shaper), add sizzle/presence to a dull track (exciter), correct or creatively shift a part's pitch (pitch shifter).</td></tr>
            </table>
            <p>
                Each effect is a node type in the <a href="#graph">Audio Graph</a> and can be dropped
                into a mixer insert chain (the common case - see <a href="#mixer">Mixer &amp; Routing</a>)
                or wired freely in the Graph tab, including external sidechain inputs (for example, a
                compressor ducking on a separate track's signal). Use the <a href="#tab-dsp">DSP tab</a>
                to audition an effect or check a signal's metering/loudness before committing it to a
                mixer insert. On a mixer strip, double-click an insert's slot to open its parameter
                editor and adjust it after the fact - and when you first add an insert, the same editor
                opens immediately so you can set its starting values instead of getting a fixed preset.
                Per-effect settings can be saved as a named preset and reloaded onto any other insert of
                the same kind.
            </p>
        </section>

        <!-- ============================================================ -->
        <section id="graph">
            <h2>9. Audio Graph</h2>
            <div class="udaw-purpose">
                <p><b>Purpose</b> Wire sources, effects and utility nodes into a custom signal-processing chain.</p>
                <p><b>Why</b> Mixer inserts are the common case, but some processing (parallel compression, multi-tap delays, custom sidechain routing) needs a graph you design yourself - the Graph tab is where that happens, and the exact same engine underpins every mixer insert slot.</p>
            </div>
            <p>
                The audio graph is UDAW's node engine: sources, effects and utility nodes connected into
                a processing graph. Graph changes are validated (rejecting illegal feedback loops,
                channel-count mismatches, and dangling connections) and then swapped into the running
                audio engine atomically and lock-free, so editing the graph never glitches or clicks the
                audio that is currently playing. Graphs have their own versioned text serializer and can
                be embedded inside a project, a mixer insert slot, or saved standalone for reuse across
                projects.
            </p>
            <h4>How to use it</h4>
            <ol>
                <li>Add nodes (gain, pan, filter, one of the <a href="#dsp">DSP effects</a>, a bus/meter/output node, ...).</li>
                <li>Connect them by dragging from one node's output to another's input.</li>
                <li>Use <strong>Build + analyze</strong> to validate the graph and see the compiled topology drawn with live meter-tap colouring.</li>
                <li>Use <strong>Play graph</strong> to audition it standalone, or save it into a mixer insert slot to use it on a real track.</li>
            </ol>
        </section>

        <!-- ============================================================ -->
        <section id="tracker">
            <h2>10. Tracker Module</h2>
            <div class="udaw-purpose">
                <p><b>Purpose</b> Compose chiptune/tracker-style music by typing notes and effect commands directly into a pattern grid.</p>
                <p><b>Why</b> Trackers let you build precise, sample-accurate rhythmic and melodic patterns faster than mouse-drawn MIDI for many electronic/chiptune styles, and UDAW's tracker engine is fully compatible with the classic MOD/XM/IT format family.</p>
            </div>
            <p>
                UDAW includes a native tracker engine &ndash; patterns of rows containing note, instrument,
                volume and effect columns, arranged into a sequence/order list, alongside sample-based
                instruments. The playback engine implements the classic tracker effect set: arpeggio,
                portamento, vibrato, tremolo, note/volume slides, position jump, pattern break, set
                speed/BPM, note delay/cut, and retrigger. The pattern grid is a real design-time paintbox
                control in the Tracker tab, with a clickable order/sequence strip that follows playback
                live.
            </p>
            <p>
                <strong>Modules on the Timeline:</strong> when you import a MOD, XM or IT/MPTM module onto the
                Timeline you can keep it <em>live</em> (Yes) or render it once to a plain audio clip (No).
                A live module clip shows its waveform like any other clip and plays together with the
                other clips. Its own tempo is read from the module and used as the clip&rsquo;s Source BPM,
                so the Timeline tempo (and any tempo changes) conforms it, the same as other tempo-matched
                clips. As the first audio on an empty Timeline it sets the Timeline tempo to the module&rsquo;s
                tempo. If the module file is edited, the clip is rebuilt from the changed file the next
                time it loads.
            </p>
            <h4>Editing in the pattern grid</h4>
            <p>
                You edit directly in the pattern grid &ndash; there are no separate entry boxes. Click a cell (the click also
                picks which part of the cell you are on: note, instrument, volume, effect letter or effect value), or move with the
                arrow keys, <span class="kbd">Tab</span>, <span class="kbd">Home</span>/<span class="kbd">End</span> and
                <span class="kbd">PgUp</span>/<span class="kbd">PgDn</span>. The cursor glides smoothly from cell to cell, and the cell under the mouse glows.
            </p>
            <table class="udaw-table udaw-col-22">
                <tr><th>Part of the cell</th><th>How to enter it</th></tr>
                <tr><td>Note</td><td>Play the piano keys on your keyboard: <span class="kbd">Z S X D C V G B H N J M</span> are C to B of the current octave, <span class="kbd">Q 2 W 3 E R 5 T 6 Y 7 U</span> the octave above. <span class="kbd">F1</span>/<span class="kbd">F2</span> change the octave, <span class="kbd">`</span> enters a note-off and <span class="kbd">\</span> a note-cut. The cursor steps down a row after each note.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Instrument</td><td>Notes are entered with the instrument chosen in the <em>Instrument</em> box beside the BPM (it lists every instrument in the module); you can also type the number as hexadecimal digits.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Volume, effect value</td><td>Type hexadecimal digits (0-9, A-F).</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Effect letter</td><td>Type the effect's letter.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Any part</td><td><span class="kbd">Delete</span> clears it.</td></tr>
            </table>
            <p>
                Editing is only possible while playback is stopped or paused &ndash; the grid, the Pattern and Cell menus and the channel Effect buttons all refuse edits while the module is playing. Every edit can be undone.
                Notes, instruments, volumes, effect letters and effect values each have their own colour, and the colours of the grid,
                the channel headers, the level lamps and the sequence strip are all set under <em>Settings &gt; Colors</em> (the &ldquo;Tracker:&rdquo; entries).
            </p>
            <h4>Channel headers</h4>
            <p>
                Each channel has a header showing its channel number with three controls: a <b>Mute</b> speaker button, an <b>Effect</b> button, and a
                <b>level lamp</b>. The lamp lights during playback and goes from green through amber to red with that channel&rsquo;s loudness. Mute silences a channel
                (you can do this while playing). The Effect button puts a whole-channel effect on every note the channel plays: with no effect set it offers a list of
                effects, then opens the effect&rsquo;s editor so you can set it up; with an effect set, it offers <em>Edit effect</em>, <em>Replace effect</em> and <em>Remove effect</em>.
                The channel&rsquo;s mute state and effect are saved with the project.
            </p>
            <h4>Transport buttons</h4>
            <p>
                The icon strip above the sequence has <b>Back to the beginning</b>, <b>Play</b>, <b>Pause</b>, <b>Stop</b>, <b>Go to the last pattern</b> and <b>Record</b>.
                Pause holds the position and Play carries on from the same place; Stop returns to the beginning. Go to the last pattern shows the
                module&rsquo;s last pattern (and jumps there while playing). Record opens the Recording view.
            </p>
            <h4>Anatomy of a pattern row</h4>
            <table class="udaw-table udaw-col-22">
                <tr><th>Column</th><th>What it does</th></tr>
                <tr><td>Note</td><td>The pitch to play (or a note-off) at this row.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Instrument</td><td>Which sample-based instrument to trigger.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Volume</td><td>Per-row volume (or a volume-column effect, e.g. a quick fade).</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Effect</td><td>A classic tracker command - see below.</td></tr>
            </table>
            <h4>Common effect commands</h4>
            <table class="udaw-table udaw-col-22">
                <tr><th>Effect</th><th>What it's for</th></tr>
                <tr><td>Arpeggio</td><td>Rapidly cycle between 2-3 notes on one channel to fake a chord.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Portamento</td><td>Slide smoothly from one pitch to another.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Vibrato / Tremolo</td><td>Periodic pitch or volume wobble.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Note/volume slide</td><td>Gradual pitch bend or volume ramp across a row's duration.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Position jump / Pattern break</td><td>Control song structure - jump to another position in the order list, or end the current pattern early.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Set speed / BPM</td><td>Change playback tempo mid-song.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Note delay / cut, retrigger</td><td>Fine timing tricks - delay a note's start within a row, cut it short, or rapidly re-trigger it (classic "roll" effects).</td></tr>
            </table>
            <h4>Exporting an instrument</h4>
<p>On the Tracker's Instruments page, <em>Export WAV/PCM/FLAC...</em> saves the selected sample for use elsewhere. The bit depth comes from the <em>Write format</em> box on the Supported SFX page. WAV files also carry the instrument's loop points and root note (a standard <code>smpl</code> chunk) plus a comment listing everything applied to it &ndash; loop mode, volume, pan, fadeout and volume/pan envelopes. Raw PCM and FLAC exports get a matching <code>.txt</code> sidecar with the same settings.</p>
<h4>How to build a tracker song</h4>
            <ol>
                <li>Load or record sample instruments (drum hits, bass one-shots, synth stabs).</li>
                <li>Click a cell and play notes on the keyboard into the pattern grid, one channel at a time (drums first is a common approach).</li>
                <li>Use effect commands for movement (slides, vibrato) instead of drawing automation.</li>
                <li>Chain patterns together in the order/sequence strip to build verse/chorus structure.</li>
                <li>Press Play - the sequence strip follows along live so you always know which pattern is sounding.</li>
                <li>Mix the tracker's channels on the same <a href="#tab-mixer">Mixer</a> used by every other part of the project.</li>
            </ol>

            <h3 id="tracker-import">Import / Export (MOD, XM, MPTM/IT)</h3>
            <p>
                UDAW reads and writes several classic tracker formats natively, with all of them mapped
                onto the same internal tracker engine, so a module imported from any of these formats is
                immediately editable with UDAW's own pattern editor and mixer:
            </p>
            <table class="udaw-table udaw-col-22">
                <tr><th>Format</th><th>Notes</th></tr>
                <tr><td><strong>.mod</strong> (ProTracker / SoundTracker)</td><td>15- and 31-sample modules, Amiga period pitch model, full Exy effect mapping, best-effort writer.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><strong>.xm</strong> (FastTracker 2)</td><td>Packed patterns, sample keymaps, volume/pan envelopes, fadeout, 8/16-bit delta PCM, unpacked writer.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><strong>.mptm / .it</strong> (OpenMPT / Impulse Tracker)</td><td>Instrument and sample modes, IT2.14/IT2.15 compressed-sample decompression, IT-core writer.</td></tr>
            </table>
            <p>Import/Export buttons for each format live directly on the Tracker tab - use Import to bring in an existing classic module to remix, and Export to share a UDAW-composed tracker song in a format any tracker-compatible player can open.</p>
                    <p>For dedicated tracker-module editing, visit and support <a href="https://www.modplug.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">ModPlug</a> and its tracker development. UDAW uses modules in your projects and provides focused editing tools. The Tracker tab also provides this link at its bottom.</p>
        </section>

        <!-- ============================================================ -->
        <section id="codecs">
            <h2>11. File Formats &amp; Codecs</h2>
            <div class="udaw-purpose">
                <p><b>Purpose</b> Get audio into and out of UDAW in the format you need.</p>
                <p><b>Why</b> Because every codec is implemented internally, UDAW has no missing-codec failure mode - if a format is listed here, it works the same on every machine UDAW runs on.</p>
            </div>
            <p>All codecs below are implemented from scratch &ndash; UDAW does not call out to system or third-party codec libraries.</p>

            <h3 id="codec-wav">WAV / RF64</h3>
            <p>
                Full RIFF and RF64 (64-bit WAV, for files over 4&nbsp;GB) support: PCM 8/16/24/32-bit and
                float 32/64-bit, mono or multichannel, with a reader, writer, streaming player source, and
                header sniffing to auto-detect format on open. Use WAV as your working/interchange format
                when you need lossless quality - for stems, samples, and anything you plan to edit further.
            </p>

            <h3 id="codec-flac">FLAC</h3>
            <p>
                A from-scratch FLAC decoder (stream/header sniffing, frame decode) for import - FLAC
                files can be brought onto the <a href="#tab-timeline">Timeline</a> the same way as
                WAV/MP3/MP4. UDAW can also write FLAC: use <strong>Export &rarr; FLAC</strong> in the Timeline menu (from-scratch lossless encoder).
                To save just a highlighted range, drag-select it on the ruler and use <strong>Edit &rarr; Save Selection as WAV / PCM / FLAC...</strong> (main Timeline menu or right-click Edit menu).
            </p>

            <h3 id="codec-mp3">MP3</h3>
            <p>
                A from-scratch MP3 decoder and encoder. The decoder uses a binary Huffman decode tree
                (rather than a naive canonical-index table) so it correctly handles real-world encoder
                output, not just conformance tones. The encoder supports both constant and variable
                bitrate, long-block encoding, and round-trips its own output. ID3 tags are read/written.
                Use MP3 for smaller files when sharing a mix, or for importing existing MP3 material
                (including Suno-generated audio - see <a href="#ai-suno">Music Generation</a>) onto the
                timeline.
            </p>

            <h3 id="codec-mp4">MP4</h3>
            <p>
                Open and import <code>.mp4</code>, <code>.m4a</code>, <code>.m4v</code>, <code>.mov</code>, <code>.mkv</code> and <code>.webm</code> files. Sound plays and imports to the Timeline sample-accurately: AAC (including HE-AAC and surround), MP3 and uncompressed audio are decoded exactly, the silent padding that encoders add at the start and end of a file is removed so a clip begins on the first real sample, and surround channels keep their standard order. A damaged section of a file becomes silence of the same length instead of shortening the clip, so everything after it stays in time. Large video files are handled without loading the whole file into memory. Matroska (MKV/WebM) files and less common audio formats (Opus, Vorbis, AC-3, DTS, FLAC and others) are read with the media codecs installed in Windows; installing a codec pack such as K-Lite extends the list. MP4/M4A and MP3 export from the Timeline are encoded by the Windows system audio encoders (Windows only), always at 48 kHz. Use <strong>Import to
                Timeline</strong> to bring audio in from an MP4/M4A/MKV file the same way as WAV/MP3/FLAC. If the file also contains <strong>video</strong>, the picture is placed on its own <strong>Video</strong> channel at the same start time, separate from the sound's audio channel. While the Timeline plays, a video pane beside the Timeline shows the picture in step with the audio (the pane appears whenever a video channel holds a clip; the picture is silent because the sound plays from the audio channel). Video is displayed with the video support installed in Windows; a file whose video format Windows cannot display still imports, but its pane stays blank. <strong>Exporting video:</strong> when the Timeline holds video, <strong>Timeline &gt; Export &gt; MP4...</strong> or <strong>MKV...</strong> (also in the Timeline popup menu) saves the picture and the mixed sound together as one file. Where several video clips overlap, the clip on the higher video channel is shown; gaps are black; muted channels and clips are left out; and a range selection exports just that range. The picture size is that of the largest video clip used and the frame rate follows the first clip. Only MP4 and MKV are offered while video is present, since other formats cannot carry a picture. Progress appears in the status bar and Jobs. See <a href="#timeline-video">Video: Import, Edit and Export</a> for the full walk-through.
            </p>
        </section>

        <!-- ============================================================ -->
        <section id="midi">
            <h2>12. MIDI &amp; Piano Roll</h2>
            <div class="udaw-purpose">
                <p><b>Purpose</b> Compose with notes on a piano-roll grid, record live from a real MIDI controller, and hear either played back by UDAW's own built-in synth - all mixed into the Timeline like any other track.</p>
                <p><b>Why</b> Because playback runs through an internal synth by default, a MIDI part in a UDAW project always sounds the same for everyone who opens it - no missing VST instrument, no "it sounded different on my machine" - while still supporting a real external keyboard/controller for input and a real external synth/module for monitoring when you have one.</p>
            </div>
            <p>
                UDAW reads and writes Standard MIDI Files, and maintains its own sequence with a tempo
                map. The Piano Roll tab supports note editing plus scale and chord helper tools (so you
                can, for example, constrain note entry to a chosen key, or stamp down a full chord with
                one action instead of clicking each note). Playback runs through an internal 64-voice
                polyphonic synth engine that behaves as an ordinary UDAW audio source. A MIDI clip placed
                on the <a href="#tab-timeline">Timeline</a> (on a MIDI track) plays back as part of the
                normal Timeline transport, mixed alongside recorded and tracker audio on the same
                <a href="#tab-mixer">Mixer</a> - not only from the Piano Roll tab's own Play button.
            </p>
            <h4>How to use it</h4>
            <ol>
                <li>Draw notes directly on the piano-roll grid, or import a Standard MIDI File to start from an existing arrangement.</li>
                <li>Use the scale/chord helpers to stay in key or quickly place harmony.</li>
                <li>Adjust the tempo map if the song needs tempo changes over time.</li>
                <li>Press Play to hear it through the internal synth immediately - no extra setup.</li>
                <li>Mix the synth's output on the Mixer exactly like any other track.</li>
            </ol>

            <h3 id="midi-devices">Recording from a Real MIDI Device</h3>
            <div class="udaw-purpose">
                <p><b>Purpose</b> Play a real MIDI keyboard/controller into UDAW and have the performance land as an editable, non-destructive clip on the Timeline.</p>
                <p><b>Why</b> Playing notes in with a real keyboard is faster and more musical than mouse-drawing every note on the piano-roll grid, and recording it non-destructively means the performance can still be quantized, transposed or otherwise edited after the fact, exactly like an imported MIDI file.</p>
            </div>
            <p>
                <strong>How to use it:</strong> on the <strong>Settings</strong> tab's <strong>MIDI
                Devices</strong> group, pick your controller from the <strong>Input device</strong>
                dropdown (and, optionally, an external synth/module from <strong>Output device</strong>
                for hardware monitoring) - <strong>Refresh list</strong> re-scans if you plug something in
                after opening Settings. Back on the <strong>MIDI</strong> tab, choose <em>Transport &gt;
                Record from MIDI device</em> from the menu to arm and start; playing the controller is
                audible immediately through the internal synth (and the external output device, if one is
                selected) as you play. Choosing the same menu item again stops the recording and commits
                whatever was captured onto the Timeline as one undoable step, on the selected MIDI track
                (or a new one). <em>Transport &gt; MIDI Panic</em> sends "all notes off" to silence any
                stuck note immediately.
            </p>
            <div class="udaw-note">
                Device selection is remembered by name, not by a numeric slot, since which slot a driver
                assigns a device can change across a reboot or a different USB port - so the saved choice
                still finds the right device next time, and simply shows nothing selected if that device
                isn't currently plugged in.
            </div>
        </section>

        <!-- ============================================================ -->
        <section id="ai">
            <h2>13. AI/SI Assistant &amp; Voice Control</h2>
            <div class="udaw-purpose">
                <p><b>Purpose</b> Use an external AI/SI provider to answer questions, make validated edits, suggest mix/master moves, generate new music, and take voice commands - all as an optional layer on top of a DAW that works fully offline without it.</p>
                <p><b>Why</b> Every AI/SI-driven action goes through the same safe, undo-aware command path a mouse-driven edit uses (see <a href="#project-doc">Projects, Undo &amp; Recovery</a>), and destructive actions always ask for confirmation first - so using the assistant never puts your project at more risk than editing it by hand.</p>
            </div>
            <p>
                The AI/SI tab hosts a chat panel that can be pointed at different AI/SI providers
                (OpenAI, Claude, or DeepSeek), selected through a provider factory so the rest of the
                application does not care which one is active. Each provider is optional and can be
                individually enabled by saving an API key for it; with none configured, UDAW is a
                complete, fully functional DAW with no AI/SI features engaged at all.
            </p>
            <p>
                A horizontal splitter sits between the MCP server list and the AI/SI log below it -
                drag it up or down to give the log more or less room, the same way you would resize
                any docked panel.
            </p>

            <h3 id="ai-keys">Getting API Keys</h3>
            <div class="udaw-purpose">
                <p><b>Purpose</b> Know exactly where each provider's key comes from and what to paste into UDAW, so you're not guessing which website to sign up on.</p>
                <p><b>Why</b> "AI/SI provider" and "Suno" are not accounts you already have just because you use UDAW - each is a separate third-party service with its own signup, billing and key page. UDAW never creates, sells or proxies these keys; it only stores whatever you paste in, in your operating system's own secure credential store (DPAPI on Windows), and sends it straight to that provider's API over HTTPS.</p>
            </div>
            <p>For every provider below, the steps in UDAW are the same: on the <strong>AI/SI tab</strong>, pick the provider from the <strong>Provider</strong> dropdown, paste the key into the <strong>API key</strong> field, and press <strong>Save</strong> (then <strong>Test</strong> to confirm it works). Leaving a provider's key blank simply leaves that provider disabled - nothing else in UDAW requires it.</p>
            <table class="udaw-table udaw-col-30">
                <tr><th>Provider</th><th>Where to get a key</th><th>Notes</th></tr>
                <tr><td>OpenAI / ChatGPT</td><td><a href="https://platform.openai.com/api-keys" target="_blank" rel="noopener">platform.openai.com/api-keys</a></td><td>Requires an OpenAI Platform account (separate from a ChatGPT Plus subscription) with billing/usage set up. Create a "secret key" and paste it in as-is.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Claude</td><td><a href="https://console.anthropic.com/settings/keys" target="_blank" rel="noopener">console.anthropic.com/settings/keys</a></td><td>Requires an Anthropic Console account with billing configured. Create an API key on the Console; it is separate from a personal claude.ai login.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>DeepSeek</td><td><a href="https://platform.deepseek.com/api_keys" target="_blank" rel="noopener">platform.deepseek.com/api_keys</a></td><td>Requires a DeepSeek Platform account with credits/billing. Create a key on the API Keys page.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Suno</td><td>Create an account at <a href="https://sunoapi.org" target="_blank" rel="noopener">sunoapi.org</a>, then get your key at <a href="https://sunoapi.org/api-key" target="_blank" rel="noopener">sunoapi.org/api-key</a></td><td>Suno itself has no public API, so UDAW uses the third-party SunoAPI.org service (separate signup, paid credits). A <strong>suno.com</strong> subscription by itself is not an API key. Full steps: <a href="#ai-suno">Music Generation (Suno)</a>.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Luma</td><td>Create an account and key at <a href="https://platform.lumalabs.ai/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">platform.lumalabs.ai</a></td><td>Luma generates <strong>video and images only</strong> (Ray / Photon) - it has no music, lyrics or sheet-music service, so choosing Luma hides the music controls (Recordings, Lyrics and Styles) and shows video/image controls instead. Use <strong>Test (credits)</strong> to verify the key. A finished Luma video is placed automatically on a new <strong>VIDEO</strong> channel of the <a href="#timeline">Timeline</a> at the playhead, where you can move, trim, split and fade it like any other clip.</td></tr>
            </table>
            <div class="udaw-note">
                Keys are billed by that provider, not by UDAW. Treat a pasted key like a password: anyone with it can spend against your account. Select the provider and press the AI/SI tab's <strong>Delete</strong> button to remove its stored key from secure storage, and rotate/revoke the key on the provider's own dashboard if you ever suspect it leaked.
            </div>

            <h3 id="ai-cost">AI/SI Cost: Consumer Plans vs. Your Own API Key</h3>
            <div class="udaw-purpose">
                <p><b>Purpose</b> Understand what AI/SI inside UDAW costs compared with a consumer chat subscription, so you can pick the provider and model that suit how much you use it.</p>
                <p><b>Why</b> Chat plans (ChatGPT Plus/Pro, Claude Pro/Max) are flat monthly fees, roughly $20, $100 or $200, with message caps and rolling usage windows. UDAW uses each provider's API with your own key, which is billed per token used, with no monthly fee from UDAW, no mark-up and no message cap other than your own budget.</p>
            </div>
            <table class="udaw-table udaw-col-30">
                <tr><th>Model (API, per million tokens in / out)</th><th>Rate, September 2026</th><th>Approx. cost of 1M tokens in + 250k out</th></tr>
                <tr><td>DeepSeek V4.1 Flash</td><td>$0.30 / $1.20 (half off-peak)</td><td>about $0.60</td></tr>
                <tr><td>OpenAI GPT-5.6 Luna</td><td>$0.20 / $1.20</td><td>about $0.50</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Claude Sonnet 5</td><td>$2 / $10</td><td>about $4.50</td></tr>
            </table>
            <p>Use a low-cost model for routine questions and quick edits, and switch provider on the <strong>AI/SI tab</strong> to a premium model only when a task needs it. Because the assistant reads your live tracks and mixer, you do not spend tokens describing the project.</p>
            <div class="udaw-note">
                Rates and plan prices are set by the providers and change often; the figures above are indicative. Check the current pricing yourself:
                <ul>
                    <li><a href="https://chatgpt.com/pricing" target="_blank" rel="noopener">ChatGPT plans (Plus, Pro)</a></li>
                    <li><a href="https://claude.com/pricing" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Claude plans (Pro, Max)</a></li>
                    <li><a href="https://openai.com/api/pricing/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">OpenAI API rates</a></li>
                    <li><a href="https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/about-claude/pricing" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Claude API rates</a></li>
                    <li><a href="https://api-docs.deepseek.com/quick_start/pricing" target="_blank" rel="noopener">DeepSeek API rates</a></li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <h3 id="ai-compose">AI/SI Composition: Samples and Loops Before MIDI</h3>
            <div class="udaw-purpose">
                <p><b>Purpose</b> Understand what the AI/SI actually puts on a track when it composes or revises an arrangement, and how to make it use generated MIDI on purpose when that is what you want.</p>
                <p><b>Why</b> Real recorded samples and loops from your own catalogue sound like finished production; generated MIDI performance data is a starting point that still needs an instrument, a patch and a performance. Defaulting to real audio means an AI/SI-composed arrangement is closer to "done" on the first pass.</p>
            </div>
            <p>
                When the AI/SI composition/arrangement engine (see <a href="#ai-chat">Chat &amp; Tool
                Commands</a> and the <code>SuggestMix</code>/song-specification pipeline) builds or
                revises a track, it always tries a matching <strong>sample or loop</strong> from your
                instrument/sample catalogue first - scored the same way instrument matching already
                works (exact id, then category/role/tag match). A resolved loop is tiled end-to-end to
                fill each section (the last tile trimmed to the section's exact length); a resolved
                one-shot sample is placed once per section at its own natural length. Only when no
                catalogue sample/loop is a good match, or a track is explicitly pinned to real
                performance data, does the track fall back to (or stay on) generated MIDI notes through
                the internal synth.
            </p>
            <p>
                <strong>How to keep a track on MIDI on purpose</strong> - ask for it explicitly in the
                request or revision instruction (for example "use a MIDI arpeggio for the lead" or "make
                the pad track MIDI so I can edit the notes"); the assistant sets that track's
                <code>requireMidi</code> flag, which tells the arrangement engine to skip sample/loop
                substitution for it entirely and always generate notes, matching the AIPlan.md rule this
                behaviour implements: <em>"MIDI interfacing should only be used when requested."</em>
                A track with no catalogue match and no explicit MIDI request is never silently dropped or
                silently defaulted to a synth patch you didn't ask for - it is left unresolved and
                reported, the same "never silently remove the track" guarantee instrument resolution
                already gives you.
            </p>

            <h3 id="ai-chat">Chat &amp; Tool Commands</h3>
            <p>
                <strong>How to use it:</strong> pick a provider from the dropdown, paste in an API key and
                press <strong>Save</strong>, then type a message and press <strong>Send</strong>. The
                assistant can describe your project (it is told to call read-only tools like
                <code>GetProjectInfo</code>/<code>GetTracks</code>/<code>GetMixerState</code> to ground its
                answer in the real current state rather than guessing), and it can also perform actions -
                for example "lower the drums by 2 dB" results in a structured <code>SetGain</code> command
                that runs through the normal mixer command path, so it is undoable exactly like a manual
                fader move. Structural or harder-to-undo actions (adding a track, changing tempo,
                exporting, generating music) are flagged as <strong>destructive</strong> and UDAW asks you
                to confirm before running them.
            </p>
            <table class="udaw-table udaw-col-30">
                <tr><th>Tool</th><th>What it does</th></tr>
                <tr><td><code>GetProjectInfo</code>, <code>GetTracks</code>, <code>GetTrackInfo</code>, <code>GetMixerState</code>, <code>GetSelection</code></td><td>Read-only - let the assistant see real project state.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>AnalyzeAudio</code></td><td>Run loudness/level analysis on a track.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>SetGain</code>, <code>SetPan</code>, <code>SetMute</code>, <code>SetSolo</code></td><td>Ordinary, reversible mixer moves.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>AddTrack</code>, <code>MoveClip</code>, <code>SetTempo</code>, <code>AddMarker</code>, <code>CreateAutomation</code></td><td>Structural edits - some flagged destructive (confirmation required).</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>SuggestMix</code></td><td>Ask for mixing suggestions - see below.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>GenerateMusic</code></td><td>Request a Suno music generation - see <a href="#ai-suno">Music Generation</a>.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>ExportProject</code></td><td>Render/export the project - destructive, requires confirmation.</td></tr>
            </table>

            <h3 id="ai-mixmaster">Mixing &amp; Mastering Assistants</h3>
            <p>
                <strong>Purpose:</strong> get concrete, applied mixing/mastering suggestions instead of
                just prose advice. <strong>How to use it:</strong> press <strong>Analyze Mix</strong> (or
                the mastering equivalent) to run the current arrangement through offline loudness/level
                analysis; the assistant turns the result into specific suggestions (e.g. "reduce the bass
                by 1.5 dB", "raise the master ceiling to -1 dBTP"). Use <strong>Preview</strong> to hear
                the suggestions applied live, <strong>Compare</strong> to A/B against the untouched
                original, <strong>Apply</strong> to keep the change (it stays on the undo stack like any
                other edit), or <strong>Reject</strong> to discard it - nothing is changed until you
                explicitly apply it.
            </p>

            <h3 id="project-startup">Startup, recovery and recent projects</h3>
            <p><strong>Settings</strong> has separate General, Colours and Diagnostics pages. Secure Storage, MP3, MP4, Graph, DSP, Supported SFX (formerly WAV), Advanced DSP and Scope are in Settings. The General page also sets <strong>Timeline refresh (per second)</strong> &ndash; how often the Timeline redraws while playing (5, 10, 15, 20, 25, 30, 35 or 40; default 20). Lower values use less CPU, which can help audio and video playback on slower machines. Open Log from the main or popup menus. Colours controls backgrounds for clips with effects and fade-in/fade-out regions; choose coloured areas (default), curves, or both. A colour you change under Settings &gt; Colors for an effect shows at once on every clip that carries that effect, selected or not - the effect's span is tinted behind the waveform and the wave itself is drawn in that colour.</p>
            <p>UDAW starts on the <strong>Timeline</strong> and, unless you have turned it off, shows the <a href="#welcome">Welcome screen</a> over it. It reopens the last successfully opened or saved project. If that project has an unclean-shutdown marker, UDAW automatically loads its newest available recovery candidate and marks recovered autosaved changes as unsaved. Recovery is limited to data already written to an autosave; it cannot restore edits made after the last snapshot. Missing or unreadable project data produces an error and leaves a new project available.</p>
            <p><strong>Project &gt; File &gt; Recent Projects</strong> lists the last ten distinct opened or saved projects, newest first. Reopening a project moves it to the top. The list survives restarts, and opening a recent entry uses the same recovery manager. Creating a new project clears the automatic last-project target until you save or open a named project.</p>
            <p>The startup splash uses the saved visual theme from its first display. An animated circular activity indicator appears alongside the current startup stage and its progress bar. The animation continues while Windows startup work is busy; it does not imply a measured percentage.</p>
            <p>UDAW saves its normal window dimensions and maximized state on close, then restores them on the next startup. Closing while minimized preserves the previous normal/maximized state. Restored dimensions are constrained to the current screen's working area.</p>

            <h3 id="ai-suno">Music Generation (Suno)</h3>
            <p>Generate finished music from a description or custom lyrics, then import the returned takes as editable WAV clips. Suno access is optional. UDAW connects to a third-party API provider, not suno.com directly - a <strong>suno.com</strong> subscription by itself is not an API key and does not unlock this feature.</p>
            <div class="udaw-note">
                By default the Suno panel is <strong>configured automatically</strong>: API type, base URL, model and callback are already filled in for the managed SunoAPI.org contract, all greyed out. The only thing you need to supply is your own API key.
            </div>
            <ol>
                <li>Get a managed API key: create an account at <a href="https://sunoapi.org" target="_blank" rel="noopener">sunoapi.org</a>, add credits, then open <a href="https://sunoapi.org/api-key" target="_blank" rel="noopener">sunoapi.org/api-key</a> and copy your API key (another dashboard using the same <code>apibox.erweima.ai</code> contract also works). The provider's site and pricing are its own and can change - if a link has moved, start from the <a href="https://docs.sunoapi.org" target="_blank" rel="noopener">documentation</a>.</li>
                <li>Open the <strong>AI/SI tab</strong>, choose <strong>Suno</strong> on the <strong>Provider</strong> row, paste the key into <strong>API key</strong> and press <strong>Save</strong>. UDAW stores it in the operating system's secure storage. Nothing else on the panel needs to change.</li>
                <li>Press <strong>Test</strong>. Success shows the provider's credit balance. HTTP errors, a website page instead of JSON, and invalid quota responses fail the test - most often a missing or wrong key. Check the AI/SI log for the result. Test does not generate music.</li>
                <li>Enter a music description in <strong>Suno prompt</strong>, optionally select <strong>Instrumental</strong>, and press <strong>Generate</strong>. To supply your own lyrics instead, enable <strong>Custom lyrics/style</strong>: the single-line prompt field is replaced by a multi-line <strong>Lyrics</strong> box sized to hold a full song, with a live character counter, and the <strong>Style / BPM</strong> and <strong>Title</strong> fields appear alongside it (both required in custom mode). Suno's own limit on custom-mode lyrics is 5000 characters - UDAW enforces this both as you type (further input is blocked past the limit) and again when Generate is pressed, so a paid submission never gets rejected by the provider for being too long. In custom instrumental mode, style and title are still required but the lyrics box may be left blank.</li>
                <li>Watch the status bar or <strong>Jobs</strong> tab for submission, provider status, and take-by-take download and decoding. Generation runs in the background. <strong>Cancel</strong> stops local processing at the next cancellation check; an active network request can take up to its 30-second timeout. Remote work may continue and charges may still apply.</li>
                <li>The first finished take goes to the audio track selected when Generate was pressed, at the snapped playhead position captured then. Alternative takes go to separate lanes at the same position. If no valid selected track remains, UDAW creates a new lane. Clip insertion is undoable. Switching projects cancels/detaches the old job so it cannot import into the replacement project.</li>
            </ol>
            <p><strong>Model:</strong> the <strong>Model</strong> dropdown lists every value SunoAPI.org's managed <code>/api/v1/generate</code> endpoint actually accepts - <code>V6</code>, <code>V6_WILD</code> and <code>V6_MINI</code> are the current tier (<code>V6</code> is the provider's own recommended default and UDAW's default selection); <code>V5_5</code>, <code>V5</code>, <code>V4_5PLUS</code>, <code>V4_5ALL</code>, <code>V4_5</code> and <code>V4</code> are listed as deprecated by the provider and marked accordingly - only pick one of those if your account is specifically still pinned to that older tier.</p>
            <p><strong>Advanced (self-hosted): </strong>check <strong>Advanced (self-hosted)</strong> to unlock the API type, base URL, model and callback fields and configure them by hand - for example to point at your own <a href="https://github.com/gcui-art/suno-api" target="_blank" rel="noopener">gcui-art/suno-api</a> proxy fronting your own suno.com session, or a managed account with a non-default base URL or model. The self-hosted adapter uses <code>/api/generate</code> or <code>/api/custom_generate</code>, then <code>/api/get?ids=...</code>; a self-hosted proxy that does not require a key can leave the API key field blank, since its Suno session cookie stays on the proxy server, not in UDAW. Unchecking Advanced restores automatic configuration.</p>
            <p><strong>About the callback URL:</strong> the managed API's <code>callBackUrl</code> field is mandatory in its request schema, but UDAW never hosts a webhook - it polls <code>/api/v1/generate/record-info</code> for the result instead. In automatic configuration UDAW fills this with an inert placeholder on the reserved <code>example.com</code> domain that is never actually called; only fill in a real callback URL here if your own external tooling depends on receiving that provider's webhook independently of UDAW.</p>
            <p><strong>Completion and retries:</strong> UDAW waits for completed audio, not streaming previews, up to ten minutes total. The paid generation submission itself is sent exactly once and is never automatically retried, so a submit failure never risks a duplicate charge - after a timeout, cancellation or connection failure there, check the provider's task history before pressing Generate again. Once a generation is submitted, the background status poll that watches for it to finish is allowed one automatic retry if a single poll request drops due to a transient network hiccup (a dropped packet, a momentary DNS blip) - not on an actual error response from the provider - so a brief connectivity wobble part-way through a ten-minute wait doesn't abort an already-paid-for job.</p>
            <p><strong>Audio and timing:</strong> this integration downloads finished full-mix MP3 takes and converts them locally to WAV. Cached WAVs remain available if timeline import fails. BPM in the prompt is a suggestion; automatic grid warping, leading-silence removal, remote WAV conversion and stem-separation jobs are not included. The proxy's <code>/api/concat</code> endpoint joins extensions; it is not a stem-separation endpoint.</p>
            <p>Provider selection and (in Advanced mode) URL, model and callback settings are saved when you Test or Generate. See the <a href="https://github.com/gcui-art/suno-api#api-reference" target="_blank" rel="noopener">self-hosted API reference</a> and the <a href="https://docs.sunoapi.org/suno-api/generate-music" target="_blank" rel="noopener">managed provider documentation</a> for their server setup and account requirements.</p>
            <div class="udaw-note">
                Generated takes are downloaded to a local cache folder before the finished WAV is imported onto the Timeline - and that WAV is <strong>not</strong> deleted afterwards (only the intermediate MP3 is). Since this is paid, provider-billed audio, use <strong>Open Suno Cache Folder...</strong> - on the AI/SI menu and on the AI/SI tab's right-click menu - to open that folder directly and copy your takes somewhere safe. The folder is created automatically the first time you use this, even before you've generated anything.
            </div>

            <h3 id="ai-suno-upmatch">Taking a UDAW Mix to Suno (Up-Match Workflow)</h3>
            <p>If you also use Suno directly at suno.com, you can carry your UDAW work over to it and use Suno to build something better on top of your own material. Please read the first point carefully, because it sets what is and is not possible.</p>
            <div class="udaw-note">
                <strong>Important:</strong> Suno does not offer a supported way to publish a finished file straight onto your Suno profile, and UDAW does not try to bypass that (no scripted logins or private endpoints). The workflow below uses Suno's own <em>upload audio</em> features, which create a <em>new</em> Suno track from your audio. That new track is what appears in your Suno library and, if you make it public, on your profile. Your original mix is used as the starting point, not posted unchanged. Suno's website and rules change over time, so where the steps below name a Suno button, look for the closest current equivalent.
            </div>

            <h4>Step 1 - Decide what to send</h4>
            <ul>
                <li><strong>Whole song:</strong> use the normal export (Timeline menu &rarr; Export).</li>
                <li><strong>Just a section</strong> (a verse, a hook, a riff you want Suno to develop): on the Timeline ruler, drag from the start to the end of the section so it is highlighted. Suno works best with a short, clean, focused piece, so a single strong idea is usually better than an entire arrangement.</li>
            </ul>

            <h4>Step 2 - Prepare the mix in UDAW</h4>
            <ol>
                <li>Play the section and make sure nothing is clipping (watch the Out L/R meters). Leave a little headroom rather than pushing the master to the limit.</li>
                <li>Check every channel you want included is un-muted, and that clips with a different tempo have their <strong>Clip Tempo (BPM)</strong> set so the mix really is at the project tempo.</li>
                <li>Keep the clip reasonably short (well under Suno's upload length limit, which is a few minutes at most and can change). Trim or highlight a smaller range if needed.</li>
            </ol>

            <h4>Step 3 - Save the audio file</h4>
            <ol>
                <li><strong>For a highlighted section:</strong> with the range still highlighted, choose <strong>Edit &rarr; Save Selection as WAV...</strong> (or <strong>as FLAC...</strong>) from the main Timeline menu, or from the right-click Edit menu. These items are greyed out until a range is highlighted. Suno accepts WAV and MP3 uploads, so WAV is the safe choice; use FLAC only if you want a smaller lossless file for your own archive.</li>
                <li><strong>For the whole song:</strong> use Timeline &rarr; Export &rarr; WAV (or MP3 if you need a smaller file).</li>
                <li>Pick a folder and a clear file name, then press Save. Progress appears in the status bar at the bottom and in Jobs, and a "Saved ..." line appears in the Timeline log when the file is finished. Do not close the project until it says so.</li>
                <li>Only the highlighted range is written, with all channels mixed, and with your fades, gains, tempo conforming and mixer settings applied - exactly what you hear when you play it.</li>
            </ol>

            <h4>Step 4 - Upload it in Suno (in your web browser)</h4>
            <ol>
                <li>Sign in at suno.com with your own account.</li>
                <li>Open Suno's <strong>Create</strong> page and use its audio upload option (look for <em>Upload Audio</em> or the equivalent), then choose the file you saved in Step 3.</li>
                <li>Wait for the upload to finish. Suno may ask you to confirm you own the rights to the audio. Only upload material you created or are licensed to use.</li>
                <li>Use the upload as the starting point: choose <strong>Cover</strong> to reinterpret it in a new style or with new vocals/lyrics, or <strong>Extend</strong> to have Suno continue it past its end. Add a style description and, if you want, lyrics, then create.</li>
                <li>Listen to the results. Suno normally returns more than one version. Keep the ones you like; open a version's menu to make it public if you want it to show on your profile.</li>
            </ol>

            <h4>Step 5 - Bring the improved version back into UDAW</h4>
            <ol>
                <li>Download the version you like from Suno (as MP3 or WAV) to a folder on your computer.</li>
                <li>In UDAW, use <strong>Timeline &rarr; Import</strong> (MP3 or WAV) to add it as a new clip on its own channel. You can then trim it, apply Clip Tempo, layer it with your original tracks, apply effects, mix and master it, and export again.</li>
                <li>You can repeat the loop as often as you like: export a section, up-match it in Suno, import the result, refine it in UDAW.</li>
            </ol>

            <h4>Doing the same from inside UDAW (API route)</h4>
            <p>If you have set up the Suno API key as described under <a href="#ai-suno">Music Generation</a>, the <strong>AI/SI tab</strong> can send recorded reference audio to Suno for <strong>Cover</strong> or <strong>Extend</strong> without leaving UDAW, and the finished takes are downloaded and placed on the Timeline for you. That route goes through the third-party API provider, not suno.com, so those takes belong to that service's account and will <strong>not</strong> appear on your suno.com profile. Use the browser workflow above whenever you specifically want the result on your own suno.com profile.</p>

            <h4>Tips for better results</h4>
            <ul>
                <li>Send a clean, well-balanced, moderately loud mix; heavy limiting or distortion tends to confuse the result.</li>
                <li>A short section with a clear rhythm and melody gives Suno more to hold on to than a dense full arrangement.</li>
                <li>Describe the style you want in words as well as supplying audio; the more specific the description, the closer the result.</li>
                <li>Generate several versions and compare them against your original inside UDAW before choosing.</li>
                <li>Keep the original exported file. Suno's output is a reinterpretation, so the original is your reference.</li>
            </ul>
            <div class="udaw-note">
                Rights and terms: you are responsible for owning or licensing anything you upload, and for following Suno's terms of service and its rules on public sharing and commercial use. UDAW only writes an ordinary audio file to disk; it does not upload anything to Suno on your behalf in the browser workflow.
            </div>
            <h4 id="ai-suno-lyrics-menu">Lyrics box: Clear and Save as .txt</h4>
            <p>Right-click inside the <strong>lyrics box</strong> (shown when Custom mode is ticked) to open a small menu:</p>
            <ul>
                <li><strong>Save as .txt...</strong> &ndash; opens a Save dialog (it starts in your export folder) and writes the lyrics to a plain UTF-8 text file. Use this to keep a safe copy of your lyrics outside UDAW, for example before clearing the box or before closing the project. If a file with that name already exists you are asked before it is replaced.</li>
                <li><strong>Clear</strong> &ndash; removes all the lyrics from the box. Because this cannot be undone, UDAW first asks <em>"Clear all lyrics?"</em> and only clears if you answer <strong>Yes</strong>; answering No leaves the lyrics untouched. Save a copy first if you want to keep them.</li>
            </ul>
            <p>Both items are greyed out while the box is empty.</p>
            <h3 id="ai-voice">Voice Commands</h3>
            <p>
                <strong>Purpose:</strong> control UDAW hands-free while you're focused on playing/singing.
                <strong>How to use it:</strong> press the voice-record control to capture a few seconds of
                microphone input; UDAW transcribes it (on Windows, via the system speech recognizer) and
                either performs a direct action it recognizes (e.g. "add a marker", "set tempo to 120") or,
                if it isn't a recognized command, forwards the transcript to the AI/SI chat provider as a
                normal message.
            </p>

                        <h3 id="ai-luma">Video &amp; Image Generation (Luma)</h3>
            <p>Luma is set up like Suno: pick <strong>Luma (video/image)</strong> on the AI/SI Selection tab, paste your API key (create one at <a href="https://platform.lumalabs.ai/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">platform.lumalabs.ai</a>), press <strong>Save</strong>, then use the <strong>Generation</strong> tab.</p>
            <ul>
                <li><strong>Test (credits)</strong> checks your key and shows your remaining Luma credit balance.</li>
                <li><strong>Media</strong> switches between Video (Ray models) and Image (Photon models). Only the controls Luma accepts for that type are shown: video offers aspect ratio, resolution (540p-4k), duration (5s/9s), loop, and start and end frame image URLs; image offers aspect ratio, JPG/PNG format, an image reference, a style reference, a character reference and a modify-image reference (each with a 0.0-1.0 weight where Luma supports one). Luma only accepts public HTTPS image URLs, not local files.</li>
                <li><strong>Generate</strong> submits the job, shows live progress in the status bar and Jobs tab, and saves the finished .mp4/.jpg/.png into your Recordings folder under <em>Luma</em>. <strong>Cancel</strong> stops local work, but Luma may still charge for a job already submitted.</li>
            </ul>
            <p><b>What Luma does not do:</b> Luma's API produces video and images only - there is no music, lyrics, instrumental or sheet-music service, so those controls (and the Recordings and Lyrics and Styles tabs) are hidden when Luma is selected. Use Suno for music. A generated video is placed on its own <strong>VIDEO</strong> channel of the Timeline (a blue VIDEO tag in the channel header) at the playhead, as an ordinary non-destructive clip, so you can edit it on the Timeline - move, trim, split, fade and delete - with full undo, and the AI/SI assistant and MCP clients can do the same through <code>SplitClip</code>, <code>DeleteClip</code>, <code>SetClipFade</code> and <code>MoveClip</code>.</p>

            <h4>Music Tabular Only (practice PDF)</h4>
            <p>With <strong>Luma</strong> selected, tick <strong>Music Tabular Only</strong> on the Generation tab, describe the music in the prompt box (song or style, instrument, key, difficulty - e.g. <em>12-bar blues in A for guitar, beginner</em>) and press <strong>Generate</strong>. Luma itself cannot write music, so nothing is sent to Luma and no Luma credits are used: UDAW asks your Claude, OpenAI or DeepSeek assistant (whichever has a saved key, in that order) for the music laid out as tablature - guitar and bass tab, drum grids and chord names, with bar numbers, sections and practice tips - and renders it to a <strong>PDF</strong> in a monospaced font so the columns line up for study and practice. The file is saved automatically to <code>AI/SI\Rendered</code> inside your Recordings folder (Settings &gt; Recordings folder), named from your prompt plus the date and time, and <strong>Open</strong> shows it. Progress appears in the status bar. Untick the option to go back to Luma video/image generation. The PDF is produced by UDAW itself; it is text-based tablature, not engraved standard notation, and the AI/SI's arrangement should be checked by ear.</p>

            <h3 id="ai-mcp">Model Context Protocol (MCP)</h3>
            <div class="udaw-purpose">
                <p><b>Purpose</b> Let UDAW's AI/SI assistant use tools from external MCP servers (a sample librarian, a documentation server, a project-management server, ...) in the same conversation as the built-in DAW tools - and, in the other direction, let an external MCP-aware client drive UDAW itself through its own safe command API.</p>
                <p><b>Why</b> MCP (Model Context Protocol) is the emerging standard for connecting an AI/SI assistant to external tools without hand-writing a new integration for each one. UDAW treats every MCP tool call as informational context for the model rather than a direct DAW mutation, so connecting an unfamiliar third-party MCP server can never bypass UDAW's own edit-validation and confirmation rules.</p>
            </div>

            <h4>Consuming external MCP servers (the common case)</h4>
            <ol>
                <li>On the AI/SI tab, fill in <strong>MCP name</strong> (a short label you choose) and
                    <strong>MCP command line</strong>. UDAW picks the transport from what you type:
                    anything starting with <code>http://</code> or <code>https://</code> is treated as an
                    MCP-over-HTTP ("Streamable HTTP") server: UDAW POSTs each JSON-RPC message to that URL, advertising
                    <code>Accept: application/json, text/event-stream</code>, and understands a reply that is either plain JSON or a
                    single SSE <code>message</code> event. Not supported: a server-push (GET) notification stream, and servers that
                    <em>require</em> an <code>Mcp-Session-Id</code> header - those fail the Test button with a clear HTTP status);
                    anything else is launched as a local child process that speaks MCP over stdio,
                    newline-delimited JSON-RPC on its stdin/stdout (e.g.
                    <code>mcp-fs-server --root C:\Samples</code>).</li>
                <li>Press <strong>Test</strong>. UDAW starts the transport, performs the MCP
                    <code>initialize</code> handshake (protocol version <code>2024-11-05</code>,
                    identifying itself as client "UDAW"), then calls <code>tools/list</code> and logs the
                    server's name/version and every tool's name and description to the AI/SI log. This is a
                    one-shot connect-list-disconnect check, not a live connection - use it to confirm a
                    server is reachable and see what it offers before relying on it in a real conversation.
                    A server that never answers within its timeout (10 seconds by default) fails the test
                    rather than hanging the UI.</li>
                <li>Press <strong>Add</strong> to save it to your configured server list, written to a
                    UDAW-wide config file (<code>mcp_servers.cfg</code> in your UDAW config directory,
                    independent of any open project) so it persists across restarts and across every
                    project you open. Remove it the same way when you no longer need it.</li>
                <li>Once added, every chat conversation automatically connects to every configured server
                    and tells the assistant what tools each one offers - the assistant can then call one of
                    those tools mid-conversation (e.g. "search the sample library for a snare") and the
                    result is fed back as context. <strong>MCP tool results never directly change your
                    project</strong> - if you want the AI/SI to act on what it found, ask it to do so via an
                    ordinary DAW tool command (see <a href="#ai-chat">Chat &amp; Tool Commands</a>), which
                    still goes through the normal validated, undo-safe path.</li>
            </ol>
            <div class="udaw-note">
                A misbehaving or unreachable MCP server never blocks the DAW or the UI - it just
                contributes no tools for that conversation ("that tool is unavailable"), exactly like a
                built-in AI/SI provider with no key configured.
            </div>
            <p><strong>Authentication and environment:</strong> an HTTP MCP server that needs a login takes a
                <strong>bearer token</strong>: type it in the <strong>HTTP auth token</strong> box next to the MCP
                command line before pressing <strong>Add</strong>. UDAW stores the token in the operating system's
                secure credential store (never in <code>mcp_servers.cfg</code>, never in the log), keyed by the
                server name, and sends it as <code>Authorization: Bearer &lt;token&gt;</code> on every request,
                including <strong>Test</strong>. A stdio server can still be given extra environment variables
                (an <code>env=NAME1=VAL1;NAME2=VAL2</code> line in <code>mcp_servers.cfg</code>, edited with UDAW
                closed) to pass a credential without putting it on the command line.</p>
            <p><strong>Resources and prompts:</strong> UDAW's MCP client can already fetch a server's
                <code>resources/list</code>/<code>resources/read</code> and
                <code>prompts/list</code>/<code>prompts/get</code> at the protocol level, but the AI/SI chat
                only surfaces a connected server's <strong>tools</strong> today - resources and prompts are
                not yet pulled into the conversation automatically.</p>

            <h4>Exposing UDAW as an MCP server (advanced)</h4>
            <p>
                UDAW can optionally expose its own safe DAW command API (the same tool table listed in
                <a href="#ai-chat">Chat &amp; Tool Commands</a>) as an MCP server of its own, so an
                external MCP-aware client - Claude Desktop, a local model, another application - can drive
                UDAW directly over HTTP/JSON-RPC (POST only, one request per connection, replies are plain JSON - no SSE
                stream; the local listener is plain HTTP on loopback, and remote agents use the separate
                HTTPS listener described under <a href="#ai-mcp-remote">Remote access over TLS</a>). It speaks MCP revisions <code>2024-11-05</code>, <code>2025-03-26</code> and <code>2025-06-18</code>
                (it echoes the client's requested revision when it supports it), answers <code>initialize</code>,
                <code>ping</code>, <code>tools/list</code> and <code>tools/call</code>, replies <code>202 Accepted</code> to
                notifications, and answers <code>405</code> to GET. It exposes <strong>tools only</strong> - no resources or prompts. This is <strong>off by default</strong> and binds to <code>127.0.0.1</code>
                (loopback only, not reachable from the network) unless you change the bind address
                yourself.
            </p>
            <ol>
                <li>On the AI/SI tab, check <strong>Expose UDAW as an MCP server</strong> and set the port
                    (default <code>8765</code>) and bind address (default <code>127.0.0.1</code>; leave
                    it unless you specifically understand the exposure you're creating by changing it).</li>
                <li>Leave <strong>Allow destructive tools</strong> unchecked to only permit the read-only
                    tools from the table below from external clients - a safe default for letting something
                    look at your project without being able to change it.</li>
                <li>Check it if you want an external client to also be able to run structural/destructive
                    tools; even then, each destructive call additionally requires the external client to
                    pass an explicit <code>confirm: true</code> argument, so a client can't destructively
                    mutate the project through one blind call.</li>
                <li>UDAW generates a <strong>bearer token</strong> the first time you start the server, keeps it in the secure credential store, and shows it in the AI/SI log each time the server starts. Give it to the external client; every request must carry <code>Authorization: Bearer &lt;token&gt;</code> or UDAW answers <code>401 Unauthorized</code>. UDAW refuses to start the server on any address other than <code>127.0.0.1</code> without a token.</li>
                <li>Every call an external client makes - destructive or not - runs through the exact same
                    dispatch/undo-stack path as a chat-driven or mouse-driven edit, so it always lands on
                    the undo stack and is subject to the same validation.</li>
            </ol>
            <h4 id="ai-mcp-remote">Remote access over TLS (agents outside your network)</h4>
            <p>The local listener stays on <code>127.0.0.1</code>. To let an AI/SI agent that is <em>not</em> on your
                network connect, tick <strong>HTTPS (TLS) for remote agents</strong> on the MCP tab: UDAW then opens a
                second, encrypted listener (TLS 1.2+, default port <code>8766</code>) <em>alongside</em> the local one,
                so local and remote access are both available at once. It <strong>always requires the bearer token</strong>
                (UDAW refuses to start the HTTPS listener without one) and uses the same tool table, destructive-tool
                switch and <code>confirm: true</code> rule as the local listener.</p>
            <ol>
                <li>Provide a PEM <strong>certificate</strong> and <strong>private key</strong> in the
                    <strong>Cert (PEM)</strong> / <strong>Key (PEM)</strong> boxes. A CA-signed certificate (for example
                    Let's Encrypt) is what AI/SI providers will trust; a self-signed one only works for your own tests.</li>
                <li>Make the port reachable from the internet: a router port-forward to this PC, or a tunnel
                    (Cloudflare Tunnel, ngrok) that gives you a public https URL and its own certificate.</li>
                <li>Tick <strong>Expose UDAW as an MCP server</strong>. The AI/SI log shows the token and the HTTPS port.</li>
                <li>Press <strong>Test local</strong> (checks the local listener) and <strong>Test TLS / URL</strong>
                    (blank = the local https listener; or type your public <code>https://host:port/mcp</code>).
                    The report lists each stage - connect, TLS handshake and certificate, token, MCP handshake, tool
                    list - and confirms that a request <em>without</em> the token is refused. Tick
                    <strong>Allow self-signed certificate</strong> only when testing your own self-signed certificate.</li>
            </ol>
            <p><strong>Which AI/SI providers can use it</strong> (checked against the providers' documentation on 2026-09-21;
                the same table is shown on the MCP tab):</p>
            <table class="udaw-table">
                <tr><th>Provider</th><th>Remote MCP support</th><th>What it needs</th></tr>
                <tr><td>Claude (Anthropic)</td><td>Yes &ndash; Messages API "MCP connector" (beta)</td><td>Public https URL, Streamable HTTP or SSE, token via <code>authorization_token</code>, tools only. Local, LAN and stdio servers cannot be used.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>OpenAI</td><td>Yes &ndash; Responses API remote MCP tool</td><td>Public URL, Streamable HTTP or SSE, token via the <code>authorization</code> field; asks for approval unless <code>require_approval</code> is <code>never</code>.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>DeepSeek</td><td>No hosted connector in its API</td><td>Only the separate DeepSeek-Harness agent app can use an https MCP endpoint, as a client.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Suno, Luma</td><td>Not applicable</td><td>Generation services, not tool-using agents.</td></tr>
            </table>
            <p>The provider's servers make the connection, so the endpoint must be publicly reachable and its certificate
                must chain to a public CA. UDAW itself is not yet verified against these providers' live connectors &ndash;
                use <strong>Test TLS / URL</strong> from outside your network first.</p>

            <p>The full set of DAW tools UDAW exposes, whether called by its own chat assistant or by an
                external MCP client connected to <strong>Expose UDAW as an MCP server</strong>:</p>
            <table class="udaw-table udaw-col-30">
                <tr><th>Read-only</th><th>Reversible edit</th><th>Destructive (requires <code>confirm: true</code> from external MCP clients)</th></tr>
                <tr><td><code>GetProjectInfo</code>, <code>GetTracks</code>, <code>GetTrackInfo</code>, <code>GetMixerState</code>, <code>GetSelection</code>, <code>AnalyzeAudio</code>, <code>SuggestMix</code>, <code>FindSample</code>, <code>GetAIProviders</code>, <code>TransportStop</code></td>
                    <td><code>SetGain</code>, <code>SetPan</code>, <code>SetMute</code>, <code>SetSolo</code>, <code>AddMarker</code>, <code>CreateAutomation</code>, <code>RenameTrack</code>, <code>SplitClip</code>, <code>SetClipFade</code>, <code>TransportPlay</code></td>
                    <td><code>AddTrack</code>, <code>MoveClip</code>, <code>SetTempo</code>, <code>ExportProject</code>, <code>GenerateMusic</code>, <code>AddSampleToTimeline</code>, <code>DeleteClip</code></td></tr>
            </table>
            <p><strong>New tools:</strong> <code>RenameTrack{track,name}</code>, <code>SplitClip{clip_id,position_sec}</code>, <code>DeleteClip{clip_id}</code>, <code>SetClipFade{clip_id,fade_in_sec,fade_out_sec}</code>, <code>TransportPlay{from_beginning}</code> and <code>TransportStop</code> (playback stops and the playhead rewinds to 0, like the Stop button), and <code>GetAIProviders</code>, which reports which AI/SI providers (OpenAI, Claude, DeepSeek, Suno, Luma) have a stored credential - it never returns the key itself. The server's <code>tools/list</code> now publishes a real description and typed input schema (required fields, ranges) for every tool, so an MCP-aware AI/SI can discover how to use each one without guessing. This is how you <strong>delegate</strong> a project to an external AI/SI: give it the endpoint and token, and it works through the same validated, undoable commands you use.</p>

            <h4>Connecting an MCP client to UDAW (setup, step by step)</h4>
            <ol>
                <li><strong>Open a project</strong> in UDAW - the server acts on whichever project is open; with none open every call answers "no project is currently open".</li>
                <li>On the AI/SI tab check <strong>Expose UDAW as an MCP server</strong> (registration required). Read the <strong>endpoint</strong> and <strong>bearer token</strong> from the AI/SI log: the endpoint is <code>http://127.0.0.1:8765</code> unless you changed the port, and the token line starts <em>MCP server bearer token</em>.</li>
                <li><strong>Sanity-check it</strong> from a terminal on the same machine (replace TOKEN):<br>
                    <code>curl -X POST http://127.0.0.1:8765 -H "Authorization: Bearer TOKEN" -H "Content-Type: application/json" -d "{\"jsonrpc\":\"2.0\",\"id\":1,\"method\":\"tools/list\"}"</code><br>
                    A JSON list of tools means it is working. <code>401</code> = wrong/missing token.</li>
                <li><strong>Register it in your client.</strong> Typical forms (check your client's own documentation for its exact current syntax):
                    <ul>
                        <li><em>Claude Code</em> (HTTP transport): <code>claude mcp add --transport http udaw http://127.0.0.1:8765 --header "Authorization: Bearer TOKEN"</code></li>
                        <li><em>Clients that only launch stdio servers</em> (some desktop apps): use a small HTTP-to-stdio bridge such as <code>npx mcp-remote http://127.0.0.1:8765 --header "Authorization: Bearer TOKEN"</code> as the server command.</li>
                        <li><em>Anything else</em>: point it at the endpoint with the <code>Authorization: Bearer</code> header; UDAW needs nothing else special.</li>
                    </ul></li>
                <li><strong>Ask the client to use it</strong>, e.g. "list the tracks in my project", "set track 2 gain to -6 dB", "add a marker called Chorus at 60 seconds". Every change appears in UDAW immediately and can be undone with Ctrl+Z.</li>
            </ol>
            <p><strong>Working with it safely:</strong> leave <strong>Allow destructive tools</strong> off while a client is only reading or making small mix moves; turn it on only for a session where you want the client to add tracks, move/delete clips, change tempo, export or generate music (each such call must also carry <code>confirm: true</code>, which a well-behaved client will ask you about). Stop the server by unchecking the box. Reaching UDAW from another computer needs a non-loopback bind address (e.g. <code>0.0.0.0</code>) <em>and</em> a firewall rule for the port; there is no TLS, so do that only on a network you trust.</p>
            <table class="udaw-table udaw-col-30">
                <tr><th>Symptom</th><th>Likely cause</th><th>Fix</th></tr>
                <tr><td>Client cannot connect</td><td>Server not started, wrong port, or firewall (non-loopback)</td><td>Check the box is ticked and the AI/SI log says "exposing UDAW on ..."; match the port.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>401 Unauthorized</code></td><td>Token missing or stale</td><td>Copy the token from the newest log line; send it as <code>Authorization: Bearer ...</code>.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>403 Forbidden</code></td><td>A browser-origin request to a loopback server (DNS-rebinding guard)</td><td>Use a normal MCP client/CLI, not a web page.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>405 Method Not Allowed</code> on GET</td><td>Client probing for a push stream</td><td>Harmless; UDAW answers POST only and clients fall back to it.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Tool result "destructive ... AllowDestructive disabled"</td><td>Safety default</td><td>Tick <strong>Allow destructive tools</strong> if you really want it.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Tool result "resend with arguments.confirm = true"</td><td>Destructive call without confirmation</td><td>The client must resend with <code>confirm: true</code>.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>"no project is currently open"</td><td>No project loaded</td><td>Open or create a project in UDAW first.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Server refuses to start on a LAN address</td><td>No token</td><td>Start it once on loopback to generate a token, or leave the default bind.</td></tr>
            </table>

            <h4>Using external MCP servers from UDAW (worked examples)</h4>
            <ul>
                <li><strong>A local stdio server:</strong> MCP name <code>files</code>, command line <code>npx -y @modelcontextprotocol/server-filesystem C:\Samples</code> (any stdio MCP server works the same way) &rarr; <strong>Test</strong> lists its tools &rarr; <strong>Add</strong>. In chat, ask "use the files tool to find kick samples"; the reply is fed back as context, and you then ask UDAW's assistant to act on it with a normal DAW command.</li>
                <li><strong>A remote HTTP server:</strong> MCP name <code>docs</code>, command line <code>https://example.com/mcp</code>, paste its bearer token into <strong>HTTP auth token</strong> &rarr; <strong>Test</strong> &rarr; <strong>Add</strong>. The token goes to the secure credential store, not the config file.</li>
                <li><strong>Two UDAWs:</strong> one instance exposes itself as a server while another registers it as an HTTP server (<code>http://HOST:8765</code> plus that token) - the second instance's assistant then sees the first's DAW tools as informational MCP tools.</li>
            </ul>
            <div class="udaw-note">
                <strong>Current limits:</strong> only tools are used from external servers (resources/prompts are not pulled into chat); no server-push (GET/SSE) stream is supported on the client side; server-exposure is Windows-only today and exposes tools only. Removing a server from the list takes effect for the next conversation.
            </div>
            <div class="udaw-warn">
                Only enable exposure with a bind address you understand, and only leave "Allow destructive
                tools" on while you trust whatever client will be connecting - it can mutate the open
                project (add tracks, change tempo, export, trigger a paid Suno generation) subject only to
                the confirm-flag requirement described above.
            </div>
            <h3 id="ai-rights">Using AI/SI: Copyright &amp; AI/SI-Generated Vocals</h3>
            <div class="udaw-purpose">
                <p><b>Purpose</b> Explain, in plain and legally grounded terms, where an AI/SI model's ability to sing, play, and arrange actually comes from, and what that means for your rights and your risk when you release AI/SI-assisted work commercially.</p>
                <p><b>Why</b> UDAW is a professional production tool. Professional musicians and producers have always needed to understand sampling clearance, mechanical licensing, and session-player rights; AI/SI-assisted creation adds one more professional skill to that same list. Knowing this up front lets you use these tools confidently and release work that is unambiguously yours.</p>
            </div>

            <div class="udaw-note">
                UDAW's AI/SI features are powerful and are provided so you can create quickly and
                professionally - but having the feature does not remove your responsibility as the
                releasing artist. Before commercially using anything AI/SI-assisted, do your own research:
                actively compare it against existing songs and artists rather than assuming a generated
                result is automatically clear. Treat that research as a required step of your release
                process, not an optional one.
            </div>

            <h4>How AI/SI models actually learn sound</h4>
            <p>An AI/SI voice or music model is not inventing singing, playing, or arranging from nothing. It learned by listening to enormous quantities of existing, already-recorded music: real vocal performances, real instrumental performances, real mixes, real productions, made by real people - working musicians, session players, and famous recording artists alike. When a generated vocal sounds breathy like one singer, or gritty like another, or carries phrasing that resembles a particular vocalist's signature style, that is not coincidence; it is the model echoing patterns it absorbed from real human performances during training. Real musicians - artists as we recognize them today, people like Dolly Parton, Jimi Hendrix, the members of Led Zeppelin, and every artist of that caliber - spent careers building a distinct, recognizable voice and style. An AI/SI model can imitate the surface texture of that style because it was trained on recordings of it; it did not earn that style, and neither do you simply by generating with a prompt.</p>

            <h4>What copyright actually protects (United States)</h4>
            <p>Under the U.S. Copyright Act (Title 17, U.S. Code), an original work of authorship is protected the moment it is fixed in a tangible medium of expression (17 U.S.C. &sect;102(a)); no registration is required for the protection to exist, though registration is required before an infringement suit can be filed (17 U.S.C. &sect;411). In music this splits into layers worth knowing separately:</p>
            <table class="udaw-table">
                <tr><th>Layer</th><th>What it covers</th><th>Who owns it</th></tr>
                <tr><td>Musical composition</td><td>Melody, lyrics, chord/harmonic structure - 17 U.S.C. &sect;102(a)(2)</td><td>Songwriter(s) / publisher</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Sound recording ("master")</td><td>The specific captured performance - 17 U.S.C. &sect;102(a)(7)</td><td>Recording artist / label</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Right of publicity / voice</td><td>A real person's name, voice and likeness - state law, not federal</td><td>The individual person</td></tr>
            </table>
            <p>Right-of-publicity and voice-misappropriation claims are separate from copyright entirely and apply even when no copyrighted recording is literally copied. The leading cases are <em>Midler v. Ford Motor Co.</em>, 849 F.2d 460 (9th Cir. 1988) and <em>Waits v. Frito-Lay, Inc.</em>, 978 F.2d 1093 (9th Cir. 1992), both of which found liability for hiring a sound-alike singer to imitate a specific artist's voice. These precedents apply directly to AI/SI voice cloning. Tennessee's ELVIS Act, effective July 1, 2024, went further and amended that state's publicity statute to name "voice" as protected specifically against unauthorized AI/SI cloning; other states are actively considering similar laws.</p>

            <h4>Infringement turns on similarity, not intent</h4>
            <p>You do not need to have deliberately copied anything to infringe. Courts generally look at ownership plus copying of original elements, assessed through access and "substantial similarity" - would an ordinary listener recognize your work as too close to an existing one (approaches vary slightly by circuit, e.g. the Ninth Circuit's extrinsic/intrinsic test versus the Second Circuit's ordinary-observer test). It does not matter that an AI/SI generated the material instead of you performing it, or that you never heard the original while working - if the model reproduced enough of an artist's pattern during training for the resemblance to be obvious, you are the one publishing and profiting from it, and liability generally follows whoever releases the work.</p>
            <p>Consequences are real and quantified: under 17 U.S.C. &sect;504 a copyright owner can elect statutory damages of up to $30,000 per infringed work, or up to $150,000 per work if the infringement is willful, separate from platform-level DMCA takedowns (17 U.S.C. &sect;512) that can pull a release down or terminate a distribution account on their own.</p>

            <h4>Who owns AI/SI-generated material</h4>
            <p>The U.S. Copyright Office's 2023 guidance and the ruling in <em>Thaler v. Perlmutter</em>, 687 F. Supp. 3d 140 (D.D.C. 2023), hold that copyright requires human authorship: material generated purely by an AI/SI system with no meaningful human creative control is not copyrightable in the United States at all. The more you meaningfully arrange, edit, select, and creatively direct AI/SI output, the stronger your own claim to authorship of the final result - and that is a separate question from whether the output infringes someone else's existing rights. It is possible to both fail to own a track and infringe another artist's rights in the same track. Separately, whether training AI/SI models on copyrighted recordings without a license is itself infringement is actively being litigated - the RIAA sued AI/SI music generators Suno and Udio in 2024 on exactly this theory - so the legal status of the training data behind any AI/SI tool, including tools inside UDAW, may itself be contested.</p>

            <h4>Vocals: the most sensitive case</h4>
            <p>A human voice is one of the most recognizable and personally identifying things an artist has. If a generated vocal sounds like a specific, identifiable real singer - famous or not - do not use it in anything released commercially. This includes an AI/SI voice model built to imitate a named artist, a style-transfer or voice-clone result a listener would reasonably associate with a real singer, and any vocal you personally notice sounds like someone specific even if that was not your intent. This holds regardless of how much you believe you "changed it" - both substantial-similarity and right-of-publicity claims turn on what an ordinary listener perceives, not on how many parameters were altered.</p>

            <h4>Before you release AI/SI-assisted material</h4>
            <ol>
                <li>Listen critically, all the way through, before committing to a generation.</li>
                <li>Ask honestly whether it reminds you of a specific existing song, artist, or recording - melody, vocal tone, chord progression, groove, and arrangement can each trigger a "too close" feeling worth trusting.</li>
                <li>When unsure, check it against other sources directly - search for and compare similar melodies or vocal qualities rather than assuming you would just know.</li>
                <li>If a result is even somewhat close to existing work, do not use it - discard it rather than rationalizing a one-time exception.</li>
                <li>Regenerate or remix with a different prompt, seed, structure, or direction until the result is honestly and clearly your own; this can take several attempts and that is normal.</li>
                <li>When still in doubt after all of the above, treat the doubt as a reason to stop and consult a music attorney or licensing professional before commercial release.</li>
            </ol>

            <p><strong>Outside the United States:</strong> the same concerns apply under different statutes. The Berne Convention sets baseline copyright protection across most countries without requiring registration; the UK's Copyright, Designs and Patents Act 1988 protects compositions and sound recordings similarly to U.S. law; the EU AI Act's Article 53 requires providers of general-purpose AI/SI models to publish a summary of copyrighted training material, a direct response to these same concerns. Assume the strictest applicable jurisdiction's rules may apply to you, not just your home country's.</p>
            <p><strong>Australia:</strong> the Copyright Act 1968 (Cth) protects original musical works and sound recordings automatically on creation, with no registration system - musical works are protected for the life of the author plus 70 years (s 33), and sound recordings for 70 years from the year of publication (s 93). Australia has no single federal statute equivalent to the U.S. right of publicity or Tennessee's ELVIS Act; unauthorised use of a real performer's voice or likeness is instead pursued through the tort of passing off and through s 18 of the Australian Consumer Law (misleading or deceptive conduct), as in cases such as <em>Henderson v Radio Corporation Pty Ltd</em> (1960) and <em>Newton-John v Scholl-Plough (Aust) Pty Ltd</em> (1986), both involving unauthorised use of a performer's identity in advertising. The Attorney-General's Department has been consulting on AI/SI-specific copyright reform since 2023; the Copyright Act does not yet contain AI/SI-specific voice-cloning provisions, so the general passing-off and Australian Consumer Law protections above are what currently apply to an AI/SI-cloned Australian artist's voice.</p>

            <div class="udaw-warn">
                This section is educational, not legal advice, and copyright/publicity law differs by country and state and is actively changing because of AI/SI. Confirm current status and consult a qualified attorney before commercial release of any AI/SI-assisted work you are unsure about. Never use an AI/SI-generated vocal that sounds like a specific, identifiable real artist in commercial work - respect the artist and the work that trained the model that made your generation possible.
            </div>

        </section>

        <!-- ============================================================ -->
        <section id="settings">
            <h2>14. Settings &amp; Themes</h2>
            <div class="udaw-purpose">
                <p><b>Purpose</b> Configure where UDAW keeps its files and how it looks.</p>
                <p><b>Why</b> Separating startup/working-directory configuration from the project file itself means the same project opens correctly regardless of which machine or user account you're on.</p>
            </div>
            <p>
                The Settings tab controls startup configuration and working directories, and lets you
                choose a visual theme from the bundled style set for the application's look and
                feel. Changes here are saved independently of any open project.
            </p>
            <h3 id="audio-output">Audio output, backend and exclusive mode</h3>
            <div class="udaw-purpose">
                <p><b>Purpose</b> Choose the device and the way UDAW talks to your sound hardware.</p>
                <p><b>Why</b> The right choice here decides whether you hear UDAW from the speakers you expect, how quickly sound responds when you press play or record, and whether other programs can play sound at the same time. Every setting on this page is saved and applied the moment you change it.</p>
            </div>
            <p>
                On the General page, the <strong>Startup &amp; Audio Engine</strong> group holds:
            </p>
            <table class="udaw-table udaw-col-22">
                <tr><td>Audio backend</td><td>How UDAW talks to the hardware. <b>auto</b> picks the best one available (recommended). <b>wasapi</b> is the modern Windows audio system: lowest latency, and the only one that offers true exclusive mode. <b>directsound</b> is the older, classic Windows audio system: choose it for an older interface or driver that misbehaves with wasapi, or if you want its behaviour. <b>software</b> uses no hardware at all (nothing is audible) - for testing. Choosing a different backend reopens audio at once and resets the Input and Output devices to <em>Default (system)</em>, because a device is named differently under each backend.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Sample rate (Hz)</td><td>The rate the device runs at. <em>Device default</em> follows the hardware. Use 44100 for most music, 48000 if you also work with video or game audio.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Buffer size (frames)</td><td>Smaller buffers give lower latency (a tighter feel when recording) but risk crackles on a busy machine; larger buffers are safer for mixing. Try 256 or 512, and raise it if you hear dropouts. The directsound backend never goes below 256.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Output channels / Output device</td><td><strong>Output device</strong> is the device <em>everything</em> plays through: the Timeline, WAV editor, Tracker, Piano Roll and drum machine, Foley pads, previews and the warning sounds. <em>Default (system)</em> follows Windows. Pick a specific device to send UDAW to your interface or monitors while Windows sounds stay on the laptop speakers. The choice is remembered. <em>Why:</em> a producer with a studio interface usually wants UDAW on the interface and everything else on the speakers.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Input channels / Input device</td><td>The microphone or line input used for recording and live monitoring; <em>Default (system)</em> follows Windows.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Auto-start audio engine on launch</td><td>Opens the audio device when UDAW starts so playback is ready immediately.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Enable audio input (capture)</td><td>Needed for recording and voice control. Leave it off if you only play back, so UDAW does not hold your microphone.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Exclusive-mode output</td><td>Takes over the chosen backend's strongest control of the device. With <b>wasapi</b> this is true exclusive mode: lowest latency, but other programs lose their sound while UDAW is open. With <b>directsound</b> there is no true exclusive mode, so UDAW takes the highest sharing level and sets the device to its own format; other programs may still play. If the device refuses exclusive access UDAW usually falls back to shared mode. <em>Why:</em> turn it on for the tightest possible response when tracking, off when you need to hear other applications.</td></tr>
            </table>
            <p>
                To check what UDAW is really using, look at the <strong>Out</strong> entry in the status box at the right of the menu bar (see <a href="#timeline-transport">Transport buttons and status box</a>).
                If a device you chose can no longer be found, UDAW generally falls back to the system default device rather than going silent.
                Further settings on the page - Log level, Locale, Timeline refresh, tempo options, hints and the
                visual theme - are described in the paragraphs below and in <a href="#registration">Registration &amp; Updates</a>.
            </p>

            <h3 id="system-sounds">Warning and system sounds</h3>
            <div class="udaw-purpose">
                <p><b>Purpose</b> Make the Windows beeps and chimes that go with warnings and errors come out of the same Output device as the rest of UDAW.</p>
                <p><b>Why</b> If your music goes to an audio interface, a warning chime from a different set of speakers is confusing - and worse, it could land on a monitor system at an unexpected volume. Routing them together keeps all sound in one place.</p>
            </div>
            <p>
                When UDAW shows a warning, error, information or confirmation message, it plays the sound
                that <strong>your own Windows Sound settings</strong> assign to that kind of message, through
                the audio engine and the Output device you chose. If your Windows sound scheme is set to
                <em>No Sounds</em>, UDAW stays silent too. If the audio engine is not open, the ordinary Windows
                beep is used instead so a warning is never missed. Messages that appear before UDAW has opened
                its audio, during start-up, use the Windows default.
            </p>
            
            <p>
                Working directories include a <strong>Video Captures</strong> folder and a
                <strong>Video Capture Filename</strong> pattern (use <code>%DATETIME%</code> for a
                yyyymmdd_hhnnss timestamp) used by the screen-capture hotkeys below.
            </p>
            <p>
                <strong>Ctrl+F1</strong> starts a screen capture: a dedicated video thread grabs the
                primary monitor and, whenever an audio input is open, a dedicated audio thread
                simultaneously drains it - both muxed into one uncompressed AVI - until
                <strong>Ctrl+Esc</strong> stops and finalizes it, or <strong>Ctrl+Pause</strong>
                emergency-stops and finalizes it (also triggered automatically if UDAW is closed while
                a capture is running). Every frame/sample already on disk survives even if the app
                closes unexpectedly; only the final header patch could be lost outside these paths.
            </p>
            <p>
                A separate <strong>Colors</strong> page lets you fine-tune every individual colour role
                of whichever theme is currently selected - background/panel/text/border, the waveform,
                selection colour (used for selected Timeline clips and channels and selected Mixer strips), grid lines, meters (including clip colour), automation and MIDI
                note colours, and the log severity colours (Trace through Critical). Pick a
                swatch from the list, click <strong>Change Color...</strong> to open the colour picker -
                the change applies immediately everywhere that colour is used. <strong>Save to
                Theme</strong> writes your edits to disk so they are still there next time UDAW starts;
                <strong>Reset Theme Colors</strong> reverts the current theme back to its shipped
                colours.
            </p>

            <h3 id="registration">Registration &amp; Updates</h3>
            <p>
                <strong>Note:</strong> in this edition UDAW runs as the full version from the start, so the
                Registration page is hidden and nothing is locked. The description below applies to editions
                that include online registration.
            </p>
            <p>
                The <strong>Registration</strong> page (Settings tab, right after General) registers this
                copy of UDAW. Enter the <strong>username</strong>, <strong>password</strong> and
                <strong>serial number key</strong> from your account at ultimanium.com and press
                <em>Register</em>. The check is made online; the serial is tied to one computer at a time
                (<em>Unregister</em> frees it so you can move it). Your details are stored encrypted for your
                Windows account, so you are not asked again on later launches, and UDAW re-verifies them
                online on every start. If the server cannot be reached, a previously registered copy keeps
                working until it can be re-checked.
            </p>
            <p>
                <strong>Unregistered</strong> UDAW has full functionality except that <strong>exporting of any
                kind</strong> (audio, MP3/MP4/FLAC, MIDI, tracker modules, samples, takes, themes) and
                <strong>any use of AI/SI</strong> (chat, composition, mixing/mastering assistants, Suno, MCP) are
                disabled; attempting either opens the Registration page. The window title and the page show
                whether this copy is registered.
            </p>
            <p>
                At startup UDAW also compares its own executable's SHA-256 checksum with the one published
                online for its version; a modified executable runs as unregistered. The same check looks for
                a newer version: a release build downloads it, verifies it, installs it and restarts
                automatically. <em>Check for updates</em> and <em>Download &amp; install update</em> on the
                Registration page do the same on demand (save your project first &mdash; UDAW restarts).
            </p>
        </section>

        <!-- ============================================================ -->
        <section id="platform-support">
            <h2>15. Platform Support</h2>
            <div class="udaw-purpose">
                <p><b>Purpose</b> Know what's actually verified today versus planned.</p>
            </div>
            <p>
                UDAW's primary, fully verified target is <strong>Windows</strong>. A
                portable software audio backend exists for future macOS/Linux support, but that
                cross-platform work is currently deferred; treat Windows as the only tested
                configuration. The MCP server-exposure feature (see
                <a href="#ai-mcp">Model Context Protocol</a>) is likewise Windows-only today - the
                MCP client (consuming external servers) works the same everywhere the app runs.
            </p>
        </section>

        <!-- ============================================================ -->
        <section id="faq">
            <h2>16. FAQ</h2>
            <dl class="udaw-faq">
                <dt>Does UDAW need any plugins, VST hosts or codec packs installed?</dt>
                <dd>No. Every DSP effect, the mixer, the tracker engine and every supported file format (WAV/RF64, FLAC, MP3, MP4/AAC-LC, MOD, XM, MPTM/IT, MIDI) is implemented inside UDAW itself.</dd>

                <dt>Can I mix a tracker module, recorded audio and MIDI in the same project?</dt>
                <dd>Yes. The project format embeds the graph, mixer, timeline, tracker module and MIDI sequence together in one file, and they all run through the same mixer and audio graph.</dd>

                <dt>Which speakers or device is UDAW using, and how do I change it?</dt>
                <dd>Look at the Out entry in the status box at the right of the menu bar: it names the device UDAW is really playing through. To change it, open Settings and choose another <b>Output device</b> in the Startup &amp; Audio Engine group (next to Output channels). The change applies immediately, and it covers the Timeline, WAV editor, Tracker, Foley pads and the warning sounds.</dd>

                <dt>Why do I hear nothing, or hear it from the wrong place?</dt>
                <dd>Check the Out entry in the menu bar status box first. If it says <em>Audio: not open</em>, turn on <em>Auto-start audio engine</em> or change any audio setting to reopen it. If it shows a device you did not mean, pick the right Output device in Settings. If you use an audio interface with an older driver, try the other Audio backend choice (wasapi or directsound) - a device that is quiet under one is often fine under the other.</dd>

                <dt>Other programs go silent while UDAW is open. Why?</dt>
                <dd>Exclusive-mode output is on with the wasapi backend, which takes sole control of the device. Untick <em>Exclusive-mode output</em> in Settings to share it.</dd>

                <dt>How do I play sound effects against a video?</dt>
                <dd>Open the Foley Sampler (the Foley icon in the Timeline transport strip), load sounds onto the pads, tick <em>Record hits while the Timeline plays</em>, press Play and hit the pads in time. Then choose <em>Add hits to Timeline</em> and each hit becomes a clip where you struck it.</dd>

                <dt>UDAW showed an "Unexpected error" window. What do I do?</dt>
                <dd>Press <b>Copy</b> (or <b>Save...</b>) to keep the report, then <b>Exit</b>, and post it on the UDAW forum with the steps that led up to it - see <a href="#crash-window">If UDAW shows the crash window</a>. Your project can be recovered from its last autosave when you start UDAW again.</dd>

                <dt>What happens if UDAW crashes while I'm recording?</dt>
                <dd>The active take is written to a disk-streaming recoverable container as it's captured; its index can be rebuilt on next launch so the audio already recorded is not lost.</dd>

                <dt>Will an older project file still open after an update?</dt>
                <dd>Yes. The project format is versioned and newer sections (for example MIDI) are simply empty when loading a file saved before that feature existed.</dd>

                <dt>How do I know UDAW is still working during a long operation, like a big render?</dt>
                <dd>The status bar at the bottom of the window shows a live progress bar and description for operations that take noticeable time - rendering the timeline for playback, applying an effect or mastering pass, and building a track's waveform display. For a few file formats whose internal decoder doesn't expose a step-by-step position (currently MP3 and MP4/AAC), the bar shows start and finish rather than a smooth in-between percentage; every other long operation reports real incremental progress.</dd>

                <dt>Where does the AI/SI Assistant's knowledge come from?</dt>
                <dd>It is a chat interface to your selected external AI/SI provider (OpenAI, Claude or DeepSeek); music generation additionally calls out to Suno. Both require the relevant API access to be configured. See <a href="#ai">AI/SI Assistant &amp; Voice Control</a>.</dd>

                <dt>Can an AI/SI-driven change break my project or bypass Undo?</dt>
                <dd>No. Every tool command the assistant runs - including ones reached through an MCP server - executes through the same validated command path a mouse-driven edit uses, so it is always undoable, and structural/destructive actions always ask for confirmation first.</dd>

                <dt>What is MCP and do I need to use it?</dt>
                <dd>Model Context Protocol - a way to plug external tool servers into the AI/SI chat, and optionally let an external client drive UDAW back. It is entirely optional; with no MCP servers configured and server-exposure left off (the default), UDAW behaves exactly as if MCP did not exist. See <a href="#ai-mcp">Model Context Protocol (MCP)</a>.</dd>

                <dt>Is it safe to expose UDAW as an MCP server?</dt>
                <dd>It is off by default and binds to loopback (127.0.0.1) only. Read-only tools are always safe to expose; destructive tools additionally require the external caller to pass a confirm flag, and only when you've explicitly enabled "Allow destructive tools" yourself.</dd>
            </dl>
        </section>

        <!-- ============================================================ -->
        <section id="report-bugs">
            <h2>17. Reporting Problems &amp; Bugs</h2>
            <div class="udaw-purpose">
                <p><b>Purpose</b> Get a problem in front of us - and in front of other UDAW users - so it can be fixed.</p>
                <p><b>Why</b> If you do not tell us about a problem, there is no way we can fix it for you.</p>
            </div>
            <p>
                Found a bug, or something that is not working the way it should? Please report it on our
                <strong>Forum Boards</strong>. Every product has its own discussion forum, and the
                <strong>UDAW</strong> forum is where UDAW bugs should be reported &ndash; that way other users can
                see the problem as well, add anything they have found, and follow it through to the fix.
            </p>
            <h3>First, register a free account</h3>
            <p>
                To post on the Forum Boards you must first register a free account on our website. It costs
                nothing, and it takes <strong>no longer than a few minutes of your time</strong>:
                <code>https://www.ultimanium.com/index.php?action=register</code>
            </p>
            <h3>Then post in the UDAW forum</h3>
            <p><code>https://www.ultimanium.com/index.php?action=forums</code></p>
            <p>Once a reported problem has been fixed and resolved, we will delete it from the Forum Board.</p>
            <h3 id="crash-window">If UDAW shows the crash window</h3>
            <div class="udaw-purpose">
                <p><b>Purpose</b> Capture exactly what went wrong when UDAW hits an unexpected error, so you can send it to us.</p>
                <p><b>Why</b> A technical detail such as a stack trace is what lets a problem be found and fixed, and it is exactly what a screenshot of an error message leaves out. The crash window collects it for you.</p>
            </div>
            <p>
                If UDAW meets an error it cannot recover from, it stops and opens the
                <strong>Unexpected error</strong> window. It shows a report in a scrollable box (scroll bars
                appear when the report is longer or wider than the box): the time, UDAW version, Windows version,
                <strong>which thread</strong> raised the error (the main window, or a named background worker thread, for
                example one of the audio or recording threads), what was raised, its message, and the trail of
                routines it passed through. A copy of the report is also saved automatically in UDAW's logs folder
                as <span class="kbd">crash-&lt;date&gt;-&lt;time&gt;.txt</span>; the window tells you the path.
            </p>
            <table class="udaw-table udaw-col-22">
                <tr><td>Copy</td><td>Puts the whole report on the clipboard, ready to paste into a forum post.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Save...</td><td>Writes the report to a text file you choose.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Exit</td><td>Closes UDAW. This is the only way out of the window.</td></tr>
            </table>
            <div class="udaw-warn">
                <strong>Only the first error is reported.</strong> Once the crash window has appeared, UDAW does not
                show another for any later error - after a first failure nothing that follows can be trusted, and a
                second window would only bury the first. Later errors are still written to the log.
            </div>
            <p>
                Copy or save the report <em>before</em> you press Exit, then post it with the steps you took (see
                below). UDAW's autosave and recovery (<a href="#project-doc">Projects, Undo &amp; Recovery</a>)
                will offer to restore your work from the last snapshot the next time you start.
            </p>

            <h3>Tell us exactly what you did, step by step</h3>
            <p>
                The most important part of any bug report is <strong>exactly what you did to get the bug, step by
                step</strong>, in the order you did it &ndash; every tab, menu, button and key press that led up to
                the problem. If we can follow your steps and see the bug happen for ourselves, we can fix it.
            </p>
            <ol>
                <li>What you did, step by step.</li>
                <li>What you expected to happen.</li>
                <li>What actually happened instead, including any error message word for word.</li>
                <li>Your UDAW version (shown at the top of this page) and your version of Windows.</li>
                <li>If UDAW showed its <a href="#crash-window">crash window</a>, the report it displayed (use Copy and paste it into your post).</li>
            </ol>
            <h3>Need to discuss it further?</h3>
            <p>
                If further discussion is required, you can visit our <strong>IRC chat server</strong> online during
                business hours &ndash; Monday to Friday, 9:00 AM to 5:00 PM, Melbourne, Australia time &ndash; and
                talk your problem through with us directly. The current <strong>Server Time</strong> (Melbourne
                time) is shown at the top left of every page on our website, so you can check whether we are in:
                <code>https://www.ultimanium.com/index.php?action=chat-rooms</code>
            </p>
            <div class="udaw-warn">
                <strong>If you do not tell us about the problems you are experiencing, then there is NO way we can
                fix them for you!</strong> We need to know about it first, right!?
            </div>
        </section>

        <!-- ============================================================ -->
        <section id="about">
            <h2>18. About &amp; Version</h2>
            <p>
                UDAW is made up of complete, independently tested subsystems: platform
                framework and audio devices; the internal DSP foundation; the audio graph; the project
                system; the WAV codec; the timeline; recording; the mixer; the DSP effect suite; the
                native tracker engine and its MOD/XM/MPTM(IT) import-export; MP3; the MP4 container; MIDI
                and the Piano Roll; multiple AI/SI providers; Suno-based music generation with a voice
                command pipeline; AI/SI mixing/mastering assistants; AI/SI tool commands; the Model Context
                Protocol client/server layer; the AI/SI song-specification/arrangement engine (with
                samples-and-loops-before-MIDI track realisation - see
                <a href="#ai-compose">Samples and Loops Before MIDI</a>); and a testing/hardening pass
                covering provider-failure handling, project-reopen compatibility, deterministic AI/SI
                regeneration and real-time plugin-crash isolation.
            </p>
            <p>
                The version line at the top of this page (<em>Version &middot; Built</em>)
                is stamped automatically on every release - it always names the exact build this copy of
                the help page was published alongside, not just the last time someone manually edited the
                page's wording.
            </p>
            <p>This page is bundled with the application and opened from the desktop client's own Help menu.</p>
        </section>

        <!-- ============================================================ -->
        <section id="license">
            <h2>19. License &amp; EULA</h2>
            <div class="udaw-purpose">
                <p><b>Purpose</b> Read the agreement that covers your use of UDAW.</p>
                <p><b>Why</b> UDAW is free and open source software provided without warranty, and you are responsible for the material you use and create.</p>
            </div>
            <p>
                Open <strong>Help &rarr; License and EULA (Read the Agreement)...</strong> to read the full
                License and End-User License Agreement in its own window. The same text ships with UDAW as
                <code>LICENSE.md</code>.
            </p>
            <ul>
                <li>UDAW is Free and Open Source Software, provided <strong>as is</strong>, with no warranty.</li>
                <li>UDAW and any Python plug-ins made with or for it may be shared free of charge, but may not be sold or distributed for commercial gain. The music and other work you create with UDAW is yours.</li>
                <li>You may not reverse engineer UDAW &ndash; by hand or with AI or automated analysis tools &ndash; to use what you learn for profit. This is a breach of the agreement (section 2.11).</li>
                <li>The developer(s) are not responsible for what UDAW does to your computer, devices or data at any time. Back up your work and keep your listening volume safe.</li>
                <li>Services such as Suno, Luma, OpenAI, Anthropic and DeepSeek are independent engines run by other companies. What you import, generate, publish or sell &ndash; audio, video or anything else &ndash; and its copyright status is your responsibility.</li>
                <li>Report problems on our Discord channel &ndash; the invite link is in the README that accompanies UDAW.</li>
            </ul>
        </section>

        <div class="udaw-footer">UDAW Help Centre &mdash; for use inside the UDAW desktop application only.</div>

    </main>
</div>
<script>
/* Language: Google Translate in the header bar. It loads when the page is online and the bar stays hidden
   otherwise. Code samples are marked so they are never translated. */
(function () {
    function markNoTranslate() {
        var els = document.querySelectorAll('.udaw-code, code, pre');
        for (var i = 0; i < els.length; i++) { els[i].setAttribute('translate', 'no'); els[i].className += ' notranslate'; }
    }
    window.googleTranslateElementInit = function () {
        try {
            new google.translate.TranslateElement({
                pageLanguage: 'en',
                layout: google.translate.TranslateElement.InlineLayout.SIMPLE,
                autoDisplay: false
            }, 'google_translate_element');
            var box = document.getElementById('udaw-translate');
            if (box) { box.style.display = 'flex'; }
        } catch (e) { /* offline or blocked: the language box stays hidden */ }
    };
    function load() {
        markNoTranslate();
        var sc = document.createElement('script');
        sc.src = 'https://translate.google.com/translate_a/element.js?cb=googleTranslateElementInit';
        sc.async = true;
        sc.onerror = function () { };
        document.head.appendChild(sc);
    }
    if (document.readyState === 'loading') { document.addEventListener('DOMContentLoaded', load); } else { load(); }
})();
</script>
<script>
(function () {
    "use strict";
    var main = document.querySelector(".udaw-main");
    var input = document.getElementById("udaw-q");
    var nextBtn = document.getElementById("udaw-next");
    var prevBtn = document.getElementById("udaw-prev");
    var countLabel = document.getElementById("udaw-count");
    var marks = [];
    var currentIndex = -1;

    function clearHighlights() {
        for (var i = 0; i < marks.length; i++) {
            var m = marks[i];
            if (m.parentNode) {
                var t = document.createTextNode(m.textContent);
                m.parentNode.replaceChild(t, m);
                t.parentNode.normalize();
            }
        }
        marks = [];
        currentIndex = -1;
    }

    function walkTextNodes(root, callback) {
        var walker = document.createTreeWalker(root, NodeFilter.SHOW_TEXT, {
            acceptNode: function (node) {
                if (!node.nodeValue.trim()) { return NodeFilter.FILTER_REJECT; }
                var tag = node.parentNode && node.parentNode.nodeName;
                if (tag === "SCRIPT" || tag === "STYLE" || tag === "MARK") { return NodeFilter.FILTER_REJECT; }
                return NodeFilter.FILTER_ACCEPT;
            }
        });
        var node, nodes = [];
        while ((node = walker.nextNode())) { nodes.push(node); }
        for (var i = 0; i < nodes.length; i++) { callback(nodes[i]); }
    }

    function escapeRegExp(s) { return s.replace(/[.*+?^${}()|[\]\\]/g, "\\$&"); }

    function runSearch(term) {
        clearHighlights();
        countLabel.textContent = "";
        if (!term || term.length < 2) { return; }
        var pattern = new RegExp(escapeRegExp(term), "ig");

        walkTextNodes(main, function (textNode) {
            var text = textNode.nodeValue;
            pattern.lastIndex = 0;
            if (!pattern.test(text)) { return; }
            pattern.lastIndex = 0;

            var fragment = document.createDocumentFragment();
            var lastIndex = 0, match;
            while ((match = pattern.exec(text)) !== null) {
                if (match.index > lastIndex) {
                    fragment.appendChild(document.createTextNode(text.slice(lastIndex, match.index)));
                }
                var mark = document.createElement("mark");
                mark.className = "udaw-hit";
                mark.textContent = match[0];
                fragment.appendChild(mark);
                marks.push(mark);
                lastIndex = match.index + match[0].length;
                if (match[0].length === 0) { pattern.lastIndex++; }
            }
            if (lastIndex < text.length) {
                fragment.appendChild(document.createTextNode(text.slice(lastIndex)));
            }
            textNode.parentNode.replaceChild(fragment, textNode);
        });

        countLabel.textContent = marks.length ? ("1 / " + marks.length) : "0 / 0";
        if (marks.length) { goToMatch(0); }
    }

    function goToMatch(index) {
        if (!marks.length) { return; }
        if (currentIndex >= 0 && marks[currentIndex]) {
            marks[currentIndex].classList.remove("udaw-current");
        }
        currentIndex = ((index % marks.length) + marks.length) % marks.length;
        var mark = marks[currentIndex];
        mark.classList.add("udaw-current");
        mark.scrollIntoView({ behavior: "smooth", block: "center" });
        countLabel.textContent = (currentIndex + 1) + " / " + marks.length;
    }

    var debounceTimer;
    input.addEventListener("input", function () {
        clearTimeout(debounceTimer);
        var term = input.value.trim();
        debounceTimer = setTimeout(function () { runSearch(term); }, 150);
    });
    input.addEventListener("keydown", function (e) {
        if (e.key === "Enter") {
            e.preventDefault();
            if (e.shiftKey) { goToMatch(currentIndex - 1); } else { goToMatch(currentIndex + 1); }
        } else if (e.key === "Escape") {
            input.value = "";
            clearHighlights();
            countLabel.textContent = "";
        }
    });
    nextBtn.addEventListener("click", function () { goToMatch(currentIndex + 1); });
    prevBtn.addEventListener("click", function () { goToMatch(currentIndex - 1); });
    document.addEventListener("keydown", function (e) {
        if ((e.ctrlKey || e.metaKey) && (e.key === "k" || e.key === "K")) {
            e.preventDefault();
            input.focus();
            input.select();
        }
    });
})();

/* Floating left navigation: keep the side panel visible while the page
   scrolls. Modern engines honour `position: sticky` on .udaw-nav directly;
   the embedded MSHTML browser does not, so fall back to a scroll-driven
   fixed position with a flex spacer that preserves the column width. */
(function () {
    var nav = document.querySelector(".udaw-nav");
    var shell = document.querySelector(".udaw-shell");
    if (!nav || !shell) { return; }

    var stickyOK = false;
    try { stickyOK = !!(window.CSS && CSS.supports && CSS.supports("position", "sticky")); } catch (e) { stickyOK = false; }
    if (stickyOK) { return; }

    var GAP = 16;
    var spacer = document.createElement("div");
    spacer.className = "udaw-nav-spacer";
    spacer.style.display = "none";
    nav.parentNode.insertBefore(spacer, nav.nextSibling);

    function scrollY() {
        return window.pageYOffset || document.documentElement.scrollTop || document.body.scrollTop || 0;
    }
    function shellTop() {
        var el = shell, top = 0;
        while (el) { top += el.offsetTop; el = el.offsetParent; }
        return top;
    }

    function unfix() {
        nav.style.position = "";
        nav.style.top = "";
        nav.style.left = "";
        nav.style.width = "";
        spacer.style.display = "none";
        nav.removeAttribute("data-fixed");
    }

    function update() {
        if ((window.innerWidth || document.documentElement.clientWidth) <= 900) {
            if (nav.getAttribute("data-fixed") === "1") { unfix(); }
            return;
        }
        if (scrollY() > shellTop() - GAP) {
            if (nav.getAttribute("data-fixed") !== "1") {
                var w = nav.offsetWidth;
                var left = nav.getBoundingClientRect().left;
                spacer.style.cssText = "display:block;width:" + w + "px;flex:0 0 " + w + "px;align-self:stretch;";
                nav.style.position = "fixed";
                nav.style.top = GAP + "px";
                nav.style.left = left + "px";
                nav.style.width = w + "px";
                nav.setAttribute("data-fixed", "1");
            }
        } else if (nav.getAttribute("data-fixed") === "1") {
            unfix();
        }
    }

    function onResize() {
        if (nav.getAttribute("data-fixed") === "1") { unfix(); }
        update();
    }

    if (window.addEventListener) {
        window.addEventListener("scroll", update, false);
        window.addEventListener("resize", onResize, false);
    } else if (window.attachEvent) {
        window.attachEvent("onscroll", update);
        window.attachEvent("onresize", onResize);
    }
    update();
})();
</script>
</body>
</html>
